var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"api/fitting/#Fitting","page":"Fitting","text":"The sessions to fit, with their data, models and settings, and what runs after them, which are the same for every inference backend. A backend fits several sessions with a full model in three parts: a parameter model gives or samples the parameters of every session, for the cognitive model and for the environment model, as on the page Parameter models, the sessions run with them, and a downstream model can use the trajectories the sessions saved. Each backend has a page of its own, with the models it makes and what a model can add for it: Fitting with Turing.jl.\n\nPages = [\"fitting.md\"]","title":"Fitting"},{"category":"section","location":"api/fitting/#Sessions-and-settings","page":"Fitting","text":"","title":"Sessions and settings"},{"category":"section","location":"api/fitting/#Preparing-a-full-model","page":"Fitting","text":"This is what an inference backend calls before it makes its full model. It is public, but not exported.","title":"Preparing a full model"},{"category":"section","location":"api/fitting/#Reading-the-sessions","page":"Fitting","text":"A session is identified in three ways: by its order among the sessions, by its data identifier, which is the value of its session column, or a NamedTuple of the values of several, and by its symbol name, a Symbol made from the data identifier, which the chains of a fit name its entries after. These functions are public, but not exported.","title":"Reading the sessions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/fitting/#Workflow-and-postprocessing","page":"Fitting","text":"What is done with the sessions and with an inference backend's model around a fit. prepare_for_prediction is the same for every backend, while each backend adds the methods of predict_session_trajectories for its own models, and its page describes which models they take.","title":"Workflow and postprocessing"},{"category":"type","location":"api/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.AbstractDownstreamModel","page":"Fitting","text":"AbstractDownstreamModel\n\nSupertype for downstream models: the final submodel of a full model, which is run after the sessions with the trajectories they saved, such as for correlations with other kinds of data, or a regression of a measurement on a state. NoDownstreamModel is the default.\n\nImplementation\n\nCreate a subtype, and for each inference backend it is used with, add a method of that backend's function for downstream models, as the backend's documentation describes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.AbstractDownstreamModel"},{"category":"type","location":"api/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.MissingValue","page":"Fitting","text":"MissingValue()\n\nStand-in for an action or an observation that happened on a timestep but isn't in the data, and which fitting imputes, as one entry of a column or a NamedTuple, such as (; choice = MissingValue()). Whether it is an action or an observation is given by where it is.\n\nFitting imputes a missing action by sampling it from the model's action distribution on that timestep. It imputes a missing observation from what an environment model gives for the observation on that timestep: as a latent variable with that distribution, or as the value of a Deterministic. A value that isn't in the data and isn't to be imputed is UnimputedMissingValue instead. NoValue is different: it says that nothing was done or observed on the timestep. Unlike missing, arithmetic on it is an error. By default, a SessionInfo replaces missing in the data by it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.MissingValue"},{"category":"type","location":"api/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.MultipleSessionsInfo","page":"Fitting","text":"MultipleSessionsInfo(cognitive_model, table; observation_columns, action_columns,\n                     session_columns, <keyword arguments>)\nMultipleSessionsInfo(sessions, session_ids; table=nothing, session_rows=nothing)\n\nMultiple behavioral sessions in a generative model that are fitted together, as a vector of SessionInfo with a data identifier for each, which an inference backend's models of several sessions take.\n\nThe first form splits a table into sessions. table is a NamedTuple with one vector for each column, or any other table that Tables.jl can read, such as a DataFrame or a CSV.File, whose columns are read when Tables.jl is loaded, as it is with DataFrames.jl and CSV.jl. The values of a column that stand for a missing observation or action become MissingValue, as for SessionInfo: by default those that are missing. As for SessionInfo, a session includes its environment where an observation or an action is MissingValue(), where environment_save_history saves anything or where check_stopping_criterion is true, and in an inference backend's full model that estimates the environment's parameters, unless include_environment says otherwise. The table is kept in the field table, without being copied, with the rows of each session in session_rows, so that a parameter model can read the columns it needs, such as those that a HierarchicalGroupedParameters groups the sessions by, and the predictors of a HierarchicalRegressionParameters.\n\nMultipleSessionsInfo(sessions_info; cognitive_save_history, environment_save_history)\n\nThe same sessions with other save histories for every one, see SessionInfo.\n\nArguments\n\ncognitive_model::AbstractTimestepModel: the cognitive model, usually an AbstractCognitiveModel.\ntable: the data of every session, in columns with one entry for each timestep, such as (; id = [\"S1\", \"S1\", \"S2\", \"S2\"], reward = [1.0, 0.0, 1.0, 1.0], choice = [true, false, true, true]), or a DataFrame with those columns.\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nobservation_columns: the columns with the observations. When the model has only one kind of observation in its attributes, a single name, such as :outcome, is its column, whatever the observation is called. Several observations are given in a tuple, each as the name in the model's attributes when the column is named the same, or as a pair from the column's name to the model's, as in a NameMap, such as (:outcome => :reward, :cue), so that one model fits tables with other names. A single name among the actions in the model's attributes is an error, since the two keyword arguments may then be swapped. An observation or action whose value is composed from several columns is specified by how to build it from the columns, followed by the model's name: a Tuple of column names for a Tuple in each row, (:choicecol, :rtcol) => :ssm_action, a NamedTuple of column names for a NamedTuple, (; choice = :choicecol, rt = :rtcol) => :ssm_action, or a Tuple of column names and a function of their values, which needs parentheses around it, (:choicecol, :rtcol) => ((choice, rt) -> [choice, rt]) => :ssm_action. When the model has only one kind of them in its attributes, the name can be left out of the last two, as in (; choice = :choicecol, rt = :rtcol). A missing value in any of the columns makes the whole value missing.\naction_columns: the columns with the actions, in the same forms.\nsession_columns: the column or columns which separate sessions, such as :id or (:id, :block). A session is every row with one combination of their values, in the order of the first row of each. The data identifier of a session is its value in a single column, or a NamedTuple of its values in several.\ntimestep_sizes=1: how much time each timestep covers, as one size for all, or the name of a column with the size of each timestep.\nmissing_observation_values=(missing,), missing_action_values=(missing,), impute_missing_observations=true and impute_missing_actions=true: the values in the columns which stand for a missing observation or action, and whether those are imputed, as for SessionInfo. They are marked in the whole table before it is split, so that the columns of every session have the same element types.\nThe other keyword arguments of SessionInfo, which every session gets.\n\nThe second form takes sessions that are already prepared. They have no table, unless it is given as table, with the rows of each session in it as session_rows, a vector of vectors of row numbers. Each argument is kept in a field of the same name.\n\nEach session is identified in three ways: by its order, its index in sessions, which the models of several sessions number it by and CognitiveModeling.session_index finds; by its data identifier, the value of its session column, or a NamedTuple of the values when there are several, as in session_ids; and by its symbol name, a Symbol made from the data identifier by CognitiveModeling.session_id_to_symbol, such as S1 or id_S1__block_2, which the chains of a fit name its entries after.\n\nIn the REPL, the sessions show a summary on one line each: how many there are, and the models and the names in the data of the first.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> struct Counter <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\njulia> CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(::Counter) = ModelAttributes(; parameters = :probability, states = :n_timesteps, inputs = :reward, outputs = :choice);\n\njulia> table = (; id = [\"S1\", \"S1\", \"S2\", \"S2\", \"S2\"], outcome = [1.0, 0.0, 1.0, 1.0, 0.0], choice = [true, false, true, missing, false]);\n\njulia> sessions_info = MultipleSessionsInfo(Counter(), table; observation_columns = :outcome => :reward, action_columns = :choice, session_columns = :id, who_starts = EnvironmentStarts())\nMultipleSessionsInfo\nSessions: 2\nCognitive model: Counter\nEnvironment: none\nObservations: reward\nActions: choice\n\njulia> sessions_info.session_ids\n2-element Vector{String}:\n \"S1\"\n \"S2\"\n\njulia> sessions_info.sessions[2].actions\n(choice = Union{MissingValue, Bool}[true, MissingValue(), false],)\n\nA DataFrame is given in the same way:\n\njulia> using DataFrames\n\njulia> data = DataFrame(id = [\"S1\", \"S1\", \"S2\", \"S2\"], reward = [1.0, 0.0, 1.0, 1.0], choice = [true, missing, false, true]);\n\njulia> sessions_info = MultipleSessionsInfo(Counter(), data; observation_columns = :reward, action_columns = :choice, session_columns = :id, who_starts = EnvironmentStarts());\n\njulia> sessions_info.sessions[1].actions\n(choice = Union{MissingValue, Bool}[true, MissingValue()],)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.MultipleSessionsInfo"},{"category":"type","location":"api/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.NoDownstreamModel","page":"Fitting","text":"NoDownstreamModel()\n\nThe downstream_model of a full model that has no third part, so that nothing runs after the sessions. This is the default.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.NoDownstreamModel"},{"category":"type","location":"api/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.NoEnvironment","page":"Fitting","text":"NoEnvironment()\n\nThe environment of a session that has none, which is the default of SessionInfo. Every observation then comes from the data, and none can be MissingValue, since nothing imputes it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.NoEnvironment"},{"category":"type","location":"api/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.ParallelSessions","page":"Fitting","text":"ParallelSessions()\n\nGiven as parallelization to an inference backend's model of several sessions, it spreads the sessions over the threads Julia was started with, as Threads.@threads does. What the model then needs, which automatic differentiation packages work with it and how much faster it is are described in the documentation of each inference backend.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.ParallelSessions"},{"category":"type","location":"api/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.SerialSessions","page":"Fitting","text":"SerialSessions()\n\nThe default parallelization of an inference backend's models of several sessions: the sessions run one after the other. See ParallelSessions for the other choice.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.SerialSessions"},{"category":"type","location":"api/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.SessionInfo","page":"Fitting","text":"SessionInfo(cognitive_model, observations, actions; who_starts, <keyword arguments>)\n\nEverything about one behavioral session that is fixed within a generative model to be fitted: the data, the models and the settings, checked and prepared once. An inference backend's model of the session takes it together with the parameters, which are what changes between evaluations. MultipleSessionsInfo makes one for each session in a table.\n\nArguments\n\ncognitive_model::AbstractTimestepModel: the cognitive model, usually an AbstractCognitiveModel.\nobservations: the observations in the data, as a NamedTuple with one vector for each name and one entry for each timestep, such as (; reward = [1.0, 0.0, 1.0]). The data must have every observation in the model's attributes, from attribute_names, and can have more. When the model has only one kind of observation in its attributes, a single vector, [1.0, 0.0, 1.0], is the data of that observation, whatever its name. An observation that wasn't recorded is MissingValue, which environment imputes unless impute_missing_observations is false, and one that wasn't made is NoValue.\nactions: the actions in the data, in the same forms, with exactly the actions in the model's attributes. An action that was taken but not recorded is MissingValue(), which is imputed unless impute_missing_actions is false, and an action that wasn't taken is NoValue(). Neither is scored.\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nwho_starts::Union{AgentStarts, EnvironmentStarts}: whether the model acts on the observation of the same timestep, EnvironmentStarts(), or on the observation of the timestep before, AgentStarts(), as in simulate. It has no default, because a wrong choice shifts the model by a timestep without any error.\nenvironment::Union{NoEnvironment, AbstractTimestepModel}=NoEnvironment(): an environment model, usually an AbstractEnvironmentModel, which imputes the observations that are MissingValue(), and against whose distributions the observations in the data are scored. Its outputs must be the inputs of cognitive_model and the other way round, directly or through observation_map and action_map. Without it, the data can't have observations that are MissingValue(). Whether the session includes it is decided by include_environment, and it is kept in the session either way.\ninclude_environment::Union{Nothing, Bool}=nothing: whether the session includes environment, so that it runs on every timestep. By default, with nothing, it is included where an observation or an action is MissingValue(), where environment_save_history saves anything, or where check_stopping_criterion is true: the scored observations then inform what is imputed, such as which arm of a bandit a missing choice was, from the reward that followed. It is also included in every session of an inference backend's full model that estimates the environment's parameters, so that the observations inform them. Otherwise it is left out, since the scores of its observations would be the same for every value of the parameters, and the session has none of its variables. With true it is always included, such as for a model of one's own which estimates its parameters. With false it never is: an observation that is MissingValue(), a selection in environment_save_history and check_stopping_criterion = true are then errors, and an action that is MissingValue() is imputed without the observations informing it.\nobservation_map::Union{Nothing, NameMap}=nothing and action_map::Union{Nothing, NameMap}=nothing: which observations of environment the cognitive model receives, and which of its actions the environment receives, and under which names, as NameMaps, as for simulate. The data is in the cognitive model's names, and is scored against the environment's distributions as the map passes them on. An output of the environment that the map doesn't pass on isn't in the data, so it is imputed on every timestep. Each model's history is in its own names.\ntimestep_sizes=1: how much time each timestep covers, as in simulate.\ncognitive_save_history::SaveHistory=SaveHistory(): which states, inputs, outputs and output distributions of cognitive_model a fitted session returns, by name, and whether timestep 0 is, as a SaveHistory in the model's own names. Nothing is selected by default. What is returned names them by their role, as states, observations, actions and action_distributions, as simulate does.\nenvironment_save_history::SaveHistory=SaveHistory(): the same for environment, whose inputs are the actions and whose outputs are the observations, returned under environment. Selecting anything makes the environment run in the session, also when nothing is missing, which adds its scores of the observations to the log density; with fixed environment parameters they are the same for every value of the cognitive parameters, so the posterior is unchanged. Whether timestep 0 is saved follows cognitive_save_history.\ncheck_stopping_criterion::Union{Nothing, Bool}=nothing: when the stopping_criterion of the two models is checked before a timestep, so that the session ends there, with what it saved up to it. With nothing, it is checked on the timesteps after the last one with data. With true, it is checked before every timestep, and a criterion that is met while the data continues is an error. With false, it is never checked. The extended help says why.\ncheck_parameter_domains::Bool=true: whether the parameters of the two models are checked against their domains in parameter_domains each time the session runs, where a value outside its domain throws a ParameterDomainError, which stops a fit.\nmissing_observation_values=(missing,) and missing_action_values=(missing,): the values in the data which stand for a missing observation or action, as one value, such as -1, or several in a tuple or a vector, such as (missing, -1). Each is replaced by MissingValue(). A value is compared to them with isequal, and () keeps every value as it is, missing too. MissingValue() can also be put in the data directly.\nimpute_missing_observations::Bool=true and impute_missing_actions::Bool=true: whether the observations and the actions that are MissingValue() are imputed, as latent variables. With false, nothing is sampled for them, and they are replaced by UnimputedMissingValue here, which the models receive as it is.\nprevious_observation and previous_action: what the first timestep receives as the observation and the action before it, in the cognitive model's names, with BeforeFirstTimestep for each name by default, as in simulate. The environment starts from the same observation in its own names, with BeforeFirstTimestep() for an output that observation_map doesn't pass on. The states where the session starts are not given here, since they come from initialize_state with the parameters that are fitted.\n\nEach argument is kept in a field of the same name, except the values that stand for a missing one and whether they are imputed, whose work is done in the data here. The other fields are includes_environment, whether the session includes the environment, as include_environment decides it, environment_observations, the data of the environment's outputs in its own names, previous_environment_observation, the observation the environment starts from in its own names, actions_to_condition_on and observations_to_condition_on, the values in the data of each name, which fitting conditions on, with the observations in the environment's names, n_timesteps, saved_timestep_sizes, the sizes a fitted session returns with what it saves, and first_timestep_to_check_stopping, the first timestep before which the stopping criterion is checked, as check_stopping_criterion decides it.\n\nSessionInfo(session_info; cognitive_save_history, environment_save_history)\n\nUpdate a session to have other save histories, which returns a new SessionInfo. This is for after a fit: a session is fitted fastest when it saves nothing, and its states, or anything else it can save, are read afterwards from the updated session, by evaluating its model with each sample of the fit. The two models have the same variables, so every sample fits both. The documentation of each inference backend describes how.\n\nIn the REPL, a session shows a summary on one line each: its models, its number of timesteps, the names of the observations and the actions in its data, which are the cognitive model's names, and who starts.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> struct Counter <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\njulia> CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(::Counter) = ModelAttributes(; parameters = :probability, states = :n_timesteps, inputs = :reward, outputs = :choice);\n\njulia> session_info = SessionInfo(Counter(), (; reward = [1.0, 0.0, missing]), (; choice = [true, missing, false]); who_starts = EnvironmentStarts(), impute_missing_observations = false)\nSessionInfo\nCognitive model: Counter\nEnvironment: none\nTimesteps: 3\nObservations: reward\nActions: choice\nWho starts: environment\n\njulia> session_info.observations.reward[3], session_info.actions.choice[2]\n(UnimputedMissingValue(), MissingValue())\n\njulia> session_info.actions_to_condition_on\n(choice = Bool[1, 0],)\n\nExtended help\n\nWhen the stopping criterion is checked follows from the data. The last timestep with data is the last one where an observation or an action isn't MissingValue(). With check_stopping_criterion = nothing, the criterion is only checked after it, so that a fit, where every timestep has data, never checks it, and a session prepared by prepare_for_prediction, whose data is all MissingValue(), ends as a simulation does. With true, the session includes its environment, whose criterion is checked too. A criterion that is met while the data continues is then an error, since the values in the data after that timestep couldn't be scored, which checks that the models agree with the length of the data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.SessionInfo"},{"category":"type","location":"api/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.UnimputedMissingValue","page":"Fitting","text":"UnimputedMissingValue()\n\nStand-in for an action or an observation that happened on a timestep but isn't in the data, and which fitting doesn't impute, as one entry of a column or a NamedTuple, such as (; reward = UnimputedMissingValue()).\n\nNothing is sampled or scored for it, and the models receive it as it is: a cognitive model as its observation, or as its previous action on the next timestep, and an environment model as the action it responds to. A model which is fitted to such data must handle it, as it does the other subtypes of AbstractNoValue. Useful for a session without an environment model whose data lacks some observations, and for missing actions which shouldn't become latent variables. A SessionInfo puts it in place of the missing observations with impute_missing_observations = false, and of the missing actions with impute_missing_actions = false.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.UnimputedMissingValue"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.prepare_full_model-Tuple{Any, MultipleSessionsInfo}","page":"Fitting","text":"prepare_full_model(cognitive_parameters_model, sessions_info::MultipleSessionsInfo;\n                   environment_parameters_model=NoParameters(),\n                   downstream_model=NoDownstreamModel(),\n                   fixed_cognitive_parameters=(;),\n                   fixed_environment_parameters=(;))\n\nPrepare the parts of a full model once, before an inference backend makes its model of them, and return them in a NamedTuple with cognitive_parameters_model, environment_parameters_model, downstream_model and sessions_info. Useful for an inference backend, whose full model calls it, so that what doesn't change between the evaluations of the model is worked out once.\n\nA parameter model can be given as a tuple of parameter models, or a NamedTuple of them, which is made into a CompositeParameters of them by as_parameters_model, with each part of a NamedTuple under its name. It checks that the two parameter models and the downstream model are of the right kind. The values in fixed_cognitive_parameters and fixed_environment_parameters, such as (; initial_value = 0.3), are given to every session for the parameters they name: each parameter model is combined with them in a CompositeParameters, so a parameter is either fixed or given by its parameter model, and a fixed parameter must be one in the model's attributes. It checks the parameters that each parameter model gives, when CognitiveModeling.parameter_names knows them, against those of the cognitive model and of the environment model: a parameter that isn't in the model's attributes is an error, and so is one in its attributes which is neither given nor has a default value, unless it is a parameter of an environment that no session runs. It prepares each parameter model for the sessions with CognitiveModeling.prepare_parameters_model. When environment_parameters_model estimates parameters, every session with an environment includes it in the returned sessions_info.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.prepare_full_model"},{"category":"function","location":"api/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.n_sessions","page":"Fitting","text":"n_sessions(sessions_info)\n\nReturn how many sessions a MultipleSessionsInfo has, which a parameter model gives the parameters of. Used by the parameter models of the inference backends, such as those of IndependentParameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.n_sessions"},{"category":"function","location":"api/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.session_index","page":"Fitting","text":"session_index(sessions_info, session_id)\n\nReturn the index i of the session with the data identifier session_id in a MultipleSessionsInfo, which is how the models of several sessions number it: its parameters are element i of each vector a parameter model gives, and its saved trajectories are element i of what the sessions return. The data identifier is the value in the session column, or a NamedTuple of the values when there are several, as in sessions_info.session_ids. Used by users reading the results of a fit, not by the models.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.session_index"},{"category":"function","location":"api/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.session_id_to_symbol","page":"Fitting","text":"session_id_to_symbol(session_id)\n\nReturn the symbol name of a session, a Symbol made from its data identifier: the value of a single session column, as in S1, and the name and the value of each of several columns, joined by _ within a column and by __ between columns, as in id_S1__block_2. The chains of a fit name the entries of a session after it, such as S1.learning_rate, and SessionwiseFixedParameters can find the values of a session by it. After using CognitiveModeling: session_id_to_symbol, the learning rate of a session is chain[@varname($(session_id_to_symbol(session_id)).learning_rate)].\n\nExamples\n\njulia> CognitiveModeling.session_id_to_symbol(\"S1\")\n:S1\n\njulia> CognitiveModeling.session_id_to_symbol((; id = \"S1\", block = 2))\n:id_S1__block_2\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.session_id_to_symbol"},{"category":"function","location":"api/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.session_column_values","page":"Fitting","text":"session_column_values(sessions_info, column)\n\nReturn the values of column in the table that the sessions in sessions_info were split from, as one vector for each session, of its rows in the table. It is an error when the sessions were given already prepared, without a table, and when the table has no such column. Useful for a parameter model that reads a column, such as one to group the sessions by, or the covariate of a regression. Used by read_one_value_of_each_session.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.session_column_values"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.prepare_for_prediction-Tuple{SessionInfo}","page":"Fitting","text":"prepare_for_prediction(session_info; check_stopping_criterion=nothing)\nprepare_for_prediction(sessions_info; check_stopping_criterion=nothing)\n\nReturn a copy of a SessionInfo, or of every session in a MultipleSessionsInfo, where every action is MissingValue, and every observation too when the session has an environment model that it can include, so that an inference backend's model of the copy simulates them instead of fitting to them. This can be used for predictive checks: evaluating the model of the copy with the samples of a fit to the sessions, or sampling it from its prior, simulates each session again, with the same number of timesteps at most, the same settings and the same environment. The observations of a session without an environment stay those in the data, as for an experiment whose observations are fixed in advance. Every action is simulated, whatever it was in the data, also one that was UnimputedMissingValue or NoValue. The copy checks the stopping_criterion of the models as check_stopping_criterion says, as for SessionInfo, whatever the session was prepared with: by default after the last timestep with data, which in a session with an environment is before every timestep, so that the copy can end before the data did, as a simulation does.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> struct Counter <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\njulia> CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(::Counter) = ModelAttributes(; parameters = :probability, states = :n_timesteps, inputs = :reward, outputs = :choice);\n\njulia> session_info = SessionInfo(Counter(), [1.0, 0.0, 1.0], [true, false, true]; who_starts = EnvironmentStarts());\n\njulia> prepare_for_prediction(session_info).actions\n(choice = [MissingValue(), MissingValue(), MissingValue()],)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.prepare_for_prediction"},{"category":"function","location":"api/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.predict_session_trajectories","page":"Fitting","text":"predict_session_trajectories([rng,] model, chain;\n                             cognitive_save_history=SaveHistory(),\n                             environment_save_history=SaveHistory())\n\nSimulate the sessions of model, an inference backend's model of fitted sessions, again with the parameters of every sample in chain, and return the trajectories they saved for every sample. This can be used for prior and posterior predictive checks.\n\nThe sessions are prepared with prepare_for_prediction, so that every action is simulated, and every observation too when the sessions have an environment, and they save what the two save histories select. The model of those sessions then runs once for each sample, with the sample's parameters, and any start that a model samples, and with every variable within the timesteps drawn again, as data is in a predictive check: an action imputed in the fit is simulated like the others. The trajectories the sessions return are kept. The sessions have at most the number of timesteps of their data, and fewer when a stopping_criterion of a model is met, since no timestep of a predictive session has data. For a prior predictive check, give a chain sampled from the prior.\n\nEach inference backend has methods for its own models and chains, which need its package to be loaded. Which models they take, and the form of the chain they return, are described in the documentation of the inference backend.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.predict_session_trajectories"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API","page":"Overview","text":"What users work with, by topic:\n\nDefining models: the types that a cognitive model or an environment model is written with, and the functions it implements.\nSimulation: running models, alone or together.\nSaving histories: what a simulation or a fitted session saves of each model.\nFitting: the sessions to fit, and what runs after them.\nParameter models: how the parameters of several sessions are given.\nFitting with Turing.jl: the Turing.jl models of the sessions, and reading the trajectories they saved from a chain.\n\nThe functions and types that are only used inside the package are under Internals.\n\nPages = [\n    \"defining_models.md\",\n    \"simulation.md\",\n    \"saving_histories.md\",\n    \"fitting.md\",\n    \"parameter_models.md\",\n    \"fitting_with_turing.md\",\n]","title":"API"},{"category":"module","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling","page":"Overview","text":"Computational modeling of cognition and behavior.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling"},{"category":"section","location":"ad_and_reactant/#Automatic-differentiation-and-Reactant","page":"Automatic differentiation and Reactant","text":"Simulations can be differentiated with ForwardDiff, ReverseDiff, Enzyme and Mooncake, and compiled with Reactant. Every commit is tested against every form of each backend, on the cases in test/ad_and_reactant/test_cases.jl: simulations with an environment, with sampled actions and rewards, with timestep sizes and with gradients of the sizes themselves, without states, with a state changed in place, with a selection of what is saved, with maps of names between the model and the environment, with an environment which ends the simulation, with a reward that isn't given on every timestep, with an environment of fixed observations, with parameter domains, and on single observations, and agents simulated with an environment or on single observations, with and without a history, and with a state saved with deepcopy. Code in the package's extensions is differentiated in suites of its own, each with its own environment, beside the other tests of the extension. The one for Turing.jl, in test/extensions/turing/ad, differentiates the log density of Turing.jl models of a session, in its turing_cases.jl: with the agent or the environment starting, with imputed actions, with an environment filling in observations, with an environment which doesn't run because nothing in the data is missing, with observations imputed from an environment's distributions, with a reward that isn't given on every timestep, with three observations, also with one that isn't given on every timestep, with parameter domains, and with a model which samples within each timestep, and of the full model of several sessions, also with three parameters, with parameter domains, with an environment parameter shared by the sessions, and with parameters from the distributions of groups and from regressions, also one with a family distribution. That keeps the package's own code tested without Turing.jl, since the backends work without it and with other probabilistic programming languages. Every gradient is compared with finite differences. Zygote isn't supported, because the package uses for loops.\n\nThe Turing.jl models can't be compiled with Reactant, so the functions that the model of a session calls within an inference backend are compiled in sessions that are run by hand, in test/ad_and_reactant/session_by_hand.jl: the log density is added up directly, and everything else is done by the package's functions, in the steps of the Turing.jl model of a session. They run with the agent or the environment starting, with a reward that isn't given on every timestep, with imputed actions, with an environment whose observations are imputed, which doesn't run, or which scores every observation, with maps of names, with saved histories, with a size for each timestep, with a session that ends when a stopping criterion is met, and with several sessions.\n\nBackend Forms tested\nForwardDiff default, chunk size 1\nReverseDiff default, compiled tape\nEnzyme forward, reverse, and both with runtime activity\nMooncake forward, reverse\nReactant compiled value, and compiled Enzyme reverse gradient, on the cases without a Xoshiro and on the sessions run by hand","title":"Automatic differentiation and Reactant"},{"category":"section","location":"ad_and_reactant/#Known-failures","page":"Automatic differentiation and Reactant","text":"These are bugs in the backends, which the package doesn't work around. Their tests are marked broken in runtests.jl of test/ad_and_reactant/ad and of test/extensions/turing/ad, so that the suites pass while the bug is there, and a fix shows up as an unexpected pass. Last checked with Julia 1.13.4, ReverseDiff 1.18.10, Enzyme 0.13.220, Mooncake 0.5.70, Turing 0.49.0 and DynamicPPL 0.42.14.\n\nBackend When What happens Status\nEnzyme reverse, with and without runtime activity simulate when the agent starts, actions and rewards are sampled from one seeded Xoshiro, and the number of timesteps is a constant wrong gradient, no error reported as Enzyme.jl issue 3912\nEnzyme forward with runtime activity simulate! of an agent with an environment, when the model's state starts as a constant such as (; expected_reward = 0.5) wrong gradient, no error reported as Enzyme.jl issue 3911\nEnzyme reverse with runtime activity the same throws a MethodError from create_shadow_ret reported as Enzyme.jl issue 3911\nEnzyme forward and reverse without runtime activity simulate with a FixedObservationsEnvironment whose observations have two types, such as a reward that is NoValue on some timesteps throws an EnzymeRuntimeActivityError use runtime activity, which is right. Observations of one type pass\nEnzyme forward and reverse without runtime activity the log density of a Turing.jl model of a session with an imputed action, an environment, or a model which samples within a timestep, and of the full model throws an EnzymeRuntimeActivityError a limitation Turing.jl documents: use runtime activity. A plain session passes\nEnzyme reverse, with and without runtime activity, and Enzyme forward when the agent starts a session with one observation, which is NoValue on some timesteps, so that a row of the data is one of two NamedTuples throws an IllegalTypeAnalysisException reported as Enzyme.jl issue 4031; an action that is MissingValue on some timesteps is fine, and so is a session with three observations of which one is NoValue on some timesteps\n\nA state which depends on the parameters from the start isn't affected, and neither is simulate! on single observations. A simulation where an observation isn't made on every timestep is right on every backend when the observations aren't saved, and on Enzyme's forward mode when they are. The other forms of Enzyme, and the other backends, are right on all of these cases. ForwardDiff, ReverseDiff and Mooncake are right on every Turing.jl case, and so are both forms of Enzyme with runtime activity on a session where every column of the data has one type.","title":"Known failures"},{"category":"section","location":"ad_and_reactant/#Pitfalls","page":"Automatic differentiation and Reactant","text":"These aren't failures of the package's own code, but a model can run into them.\n\nEnzyme reverse with runtime activity and a softmax in one line: a softmax written as exp.(v .- maximum(v)) ./ sum(exp.(v .- maximum(v))) gives wrong gradients, with no error, when it runs in a loop on values that differ between the timesteps. The softmax of LogExpFunctions.jl is right, and so is any softmax which keeps its exponentials in a variable and divides them by their sum. It is reported as Enzyme.jl issue 4030.\nEnzyme without runtime activity and objects that hold data: an object with an array in it that is made within the function that is differentiated, or captured by it, such as a FixedObservationsEnvironment made from the observations there, throws an EnzymeRuntimeActivityError. Make it once outside the function, as a constant, or use runtime activity.\nCompiled ReverseDiff tapes: a tape records one path through the code, so a model with a branch on a value gives wrong gradients at points where the other branch is taken, with no error. The tests prepare the tape at a different point to catch this.\nTuring.jl models and Enzyme: Enzyme needs runtime activity to differentiate a Turing.jl model, as Turing's own documentation says; without it, it throws an EnzymeRuntimeActivityError.\nTuring.jl models and Mooncake: Mooncake can't differentiate logjoint(model, values) when the model has latent variables under an indexed prefix, such as the imputed actions of a session or a model which samples within a timestep, because building the variables runs DynamicPPL's warning about growable arrays, and a @warn holds a try. Samplers go through a LogDensityFunction, where the variables exist already, and that is fine. The same try is why turing_catch_invalid_parameters is only used when asked for, and a compiled ReverseDiff tape doesn't see the error it catches either, so the rejected parameters get a finite log density with no error.\nCatching invalid parameters: turing_catch_invalid_parameters, and a full model with catch_invalid_parameters = true, work with ForwardDiff and with Enzyme. Anything that uses what the model returns after a rejection, which is nothing, can't be differentiated with Enzyme, so return or stop at once when it is nothing. Mooncake can't differentiate a model with a try block at all, and a compiled ReverseDiff tape doesn't see the error, so the rejected parameters get a finite log density with no warning.\nTypes known when a session is compiled: a session costs about 10 ns per timestep to evaluate, with or without latent variables, as long as every type is known when it is compiled. Earlier versions of the package built a NamedTuple from names only known at run time, which made every timestep about a hundred times slower, and that cost was taken for one of DynamicPPL.jl's. The rows of the data get the element types of their columns for the same reason, so a column which holds two types costs nothing extra. A model's own code can lose the same factor in the same way, so check a slow model with JET's @report_opt.\nReactant: the constructors of Distributions.jl don't accept traced numbers, so a model built on them can't be compiled, and sets reactant = false in its test case. An if on a traced value throws, where ifelse works. A Xoshiro can't run in a compiled function, and the default generator repeats its numbers on every run, so pass a Reactant.ReactantRNG() as a positional argument. Compile times grow with the number of timesteps, because the loop is unrolled, so a few hundred timesteps is the practical limit. A function can't be compiled when it is given, or holds, an object with a field declared as NamedTuple without its names and types, such as a model with settings::NamedTuple: give the field a type parameter instead.","title":"Pitfalls"},{"category":"section","location":"api/parameter_models/#Parameter-models","page":"Parameter models","text":"How a full model gives the parameters of every session, for the cognitive model and for the environment model, which is the same for every inference backend. The sessions themselves are on the page Fitting, and each backend's page says how its models sample the parameters: Fitting with Turing.jl.\n\nPages = [\"parameter_models.md\"]","title":"Parameter models"},{"category":"section","location":"api/parameter_models/#Models","page":"Parameter models","text":"","title":"Models"},{"category":"section","location":"api/parameter_models/#Regressions","page":"Parameter models","text":"","title":"Regressions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/parameter_models/#Functions-that-a-parameter-model-can-implement","page":"Parameter models","text":"A parameter model of one's own can add methods of these. They are public, but not exported.","title":"Functions that a parameter model can implement"},{"category":"type","location":"api/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.AbstractParametersModel","page":"Parameter models","text":"AbstractParametersModel\n\nSupertype for parameter models: the part of a full model, the model that an inference backend fits to several sessions, which gives or estimates the parameters of every session, for the cognitive model and for the environment model alike. The package comes with NoParameters, FixedParameters, SessionwiseFixedParameters, IdenticalParameters, IndependentParameters, HierarchicalGroupedParameters and HierarchicalRegressionParameters, and CompositeParameters combines several, each giving some of the parameters.\n\nImplementation\n\nCreate a subtype, and for each inference backend it is used with, add a method of that backend's function for parameter models, as the backend's documentation describes. A method of CognitiveModeling.parameter_names lets the parameters it gives be checked before a fit, and a method of CognitiveModeling.prepare_parameters_model lets it work out once, before the backend's model is made, what doesn't change between evaluations. A model which only gives values, and estimates nothing, adds a method of CognitiveModeling.estimates_parameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.AbstractParametersModel"},{"category":"type","location":"api/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.NoParameters","page":"Parameter models","text":"NoParameters()\n\nThe parameter model of a full model which gives no parameters, so that each parameter takes the value the model gives it in default_parameters. It is the default for the parameters of the environment.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.NoParameters"},{"category":"type","location":"api/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.FixedParameters","page":"Parameter models","text":"FixedParameters(parameters)\n\nThe parameter model of a full model which gives every session the same values, such as (; scale = 2.0), with nothing sampled. The values are kept in the field parameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.FixedParameters"},{"category":"type","location":"api/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.SessionwiseFixedParameters","page":"Parameter models","text":"SessionwiseFixedParameters(parameters_by_session)\n\nThe parameter model of a full model which gives each session parameters of its own, with nothing sampled. parameters_by_session holds the parameters of each session in one of three forms: by the session's symbol name, in a NamedTuple with an entry for each session, such as (; S1 = (; scale = 2.0), S2 = (; scale = 3.0)); by its data identifier, in a dictionary, such as Dict(\"S1\" => (; scale = 2.0), \"S2\" => (; scale = 3.0)); or in the order of the sessions, as a NamedTuple with one vector for each parameter, such as (; scale = [2.0, 3.0]). A session with several session columns has a data identifier like (; id = \"S1\", block = 2) and a symbol name like id_S1__block_2, as CognitiveModeling.session_id_to_symbol gives it. Every session must have the same parameters. A full model makes the first two forms into the third once, before it is made. The parameters are kept in the field parameters_by_session.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.SessionwiseFixedParameters"},{"category":"type","location":"api/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.IdenticalParameters","page":"Parameter models","text":"IdenticalParameters(priors)\n\nThe parameter model of a full model which samples each parameter once, from its prior, and gives every session that value. priors is a NamedTuple with the name of each parameter that is fitted and the distribution it is sampled from, such as (; learning_rate = Beta(2, 2)). The priors are kept in the field priors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.IdenticalParameters"},{"category":"type","location":"api/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.IndependentParameters","page":"Parameter models","text":"IndependentParameters(priors)\n\nThe parameter model of a full model which samples each parameter for every session from the same prior, with nothing shared between the sessions. priors is a NamedTuple with the name of each parameter that is fitted and the distribution it is sampled from, such as (; learning_rate = Beta(2, 2), inverse_temperature = LogNormal(0, 1)). The priors are kept in the field priors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.IndependentParameters"},{"category":"type","location":"api/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.HierarchicalGroupedParameters","page":"Parameter models","text":"HierarchicalGroupedParameters(parameters)\n\nThe parameter model of a full model which samples each parameter of every session from a group-level distribution, whose own parameters are sampled from priors, once for each group of sessions. parameters is a NamedTuple with a HierarchicalParameter for each parameter that is fitted, which has its priors, its group-level distribution, its transformation and the columns that group the sessions, so the parameters can be grouped by different columns. It is kept in the field parameters.\n\nA full model finds the group of every session once, before it is made, from the table that MultipleSessionsInfo keeps, so sessions that were prepared one at a time can only be in one group. The field groups is nothing until then, and afterwards has, for each parameter, the group of every session, group_of_each_session, the number of groups, n_groups, the symbol name of each group, group_symbol_names, and the priors of the group-level parameters, group_priors, each a NamedTuple with the prior under the symbol name of each group when the sessions are grouped, and the prior itself when they aren't.\n\nExamples\n\nusing Turing\nusing LogExpFunctions: logistic\n\nparameters_model = HierarchicalGroupedParameters((;\n    learning_rate = HierarchicalParameter(\n        (; mean = Normal(0, 1), sd = Exponential(1));\n        transformation = logistic,\n        group_by = :diagnosis,\n    ),\n    inverse_temperature = HierarchicalParameter(\n        (; mean = Normal(0, 1), sd = Exponential(1)); transformation = exp\n    ),\n))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.HierarchicalGroupedParameters"},{"category":"type","location":"api/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.HierarchicalParameter","page":"Parameter models","text":"HierarchicalParameter(priors; distribution=nothing, transformation=identity,\n                      group_by=nothing, centered=nothing)\n\nHow one parameter of a HierarchicalGroupedParameters is sampled for every session: from a group-level distribution, whose own parameters are sampled from priors, once for each group of sessions.\n\nArguments\n\npriors::NamedTuple: the priors of the parameters of the group-level distribution, in the order in which the distribution takes them, such as (; mean = Normal(0, 1), sd = Exponential(1)). Their names name the variables of the group-level parameters.\n\nKeyword arguments\n\ndistribution=nothing: the group-level distribution, as a function of its parameters, such as Normal or (location, scale) -> Laplace(location, scale). nothing is a normal distribution, whose two priors are those of its mean and its standard deviation.\ntransformation=identity: a function applied to the value of each session after it is sampled, such as exp for a parameter that must be positive or logistic for one between 0 and 1, as an inverse link function does in a regression.\ngroup_by=nothing: a column of the table that the sessions were split from, or a tuple of columns, whose values group the sessions, so that each group has a group-level distribution of its own, such as :diagnosis for a clinical and a non-clinical group. Each session must have one value of each of the columns. The group-level parameters then have a value for each group under the group's symbol name, made from its values as session_id_to_symbol makes that of a session. With nothing, every session is in one group, and each group-level parameter is one value.\ncentered=nothing: for the default normal distribution, whether the value of each session is sampled from the distribution of its group, with true, or as a standardized value which the group's mean shifts and its standard deviation scales, with false, which is the default, since it usually samples better when the data of a session informs its parameters little. Any other distribution is always sampled from directly, and giving centered with one is an error.\n\nEach argument is kept in a field of the same name, with group_by as a tuple of column names, which is empty for one group, centered as true or false, and a distribution that is given as a type inside a function which makes it.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using Distributions: Normal, Exponential, Laplace\n\njulia> learning_rate = HierarchicalParameter((; mean = Normal(0, 1), sd = Exponential(1)); transformation = exp, group_by = :diagnosis);\n\njulia> learning_rate.group_by, learning_rate.centered\n((:diagnosis,), false)\n\njulia> inverse_temperature = HierarchicalParameter((; location = Normal(0, 1), scale = Exponential(1)); distribution = Laplace, transformation = exp);\n\njulia> inverse_temperature.centered\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.HierarchicalParameter"},{"category":"type","location":"api/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.CompositeParameters","page":"Parameter models","text":"CompositeParameters(parts...; named_parts...)\n\nThe parameter model of a full model which gives some of the parameters with each of its parts, which are parameter models, such as a HierarchicalGroupedParameters for the learning rate, an IndependentParameters for the inverse temperature and a FixedParameters for a bias. What the parts give is merged. The variables of a part in parts are named as that part names them, and those of a part given by keyword in named_parts are named under its keyword, such as noise.learning_rate.session for a part given as noise. No two parts can give the same parameter: this is checked when it is made, for the parts whose CognitiveModeling.parameter_names are known, and when it runs, for the others. It samples when any part does. The parts are kept in the field parts, as a tuple, with those in named_parts last.\n\nExamples\n\nusing Turing\n\nparameters_model = CompositeParameters(\n    IndependentParameters((; learning_rate = Beta(2, 2))),\n    FixedParameters((; inverse_temperature = 2.0)),\n)\n\n#The variables of the part given as noise are named under noise, as noise.bias.session\nparameters_model = CompositeParameters(\n    IndependentParameters((; learning_rate = Beta(2, 2)));\n    noise = IndependentParameters((; bias = Normal(0, 1))),\n)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.CompositeParameters"},{"category":"type","location":"api/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.CategoricalPredictor","page":"Parameter models","text":"CategoricalPredictor(columns, prior=nothing; reference_level=nothing, coding=nothing,\n                     name)\n\nA categorical predictor of a RegressionParameter: a coefficient for each level other than the reference level, or for every level with the coding :full_dummy, with prior as its prior, such as Normal(0, 1), or with nothing the regression's default prior for a categorical predictor, from its DefaultRegressionPriors. Its levels are the values of columns, a column of the table that the sessions were split from, or the combinations of the values of several columns given as a tuple. In VaryingEffects it gives varying slopes, a deviation of each coefficient for each level of the varying effects, and prior is the prior of the standard deviation of the deviations.\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nreference_level=nothing: the reference level, such as \"control\", or a NamedTuple of values for several columns, such as (; diagnosis = \"control\", site = 1). With nothing, it is the level of the first session. The coding :full_dummy has no reference level.\ncoding=nothing: how the coefficients are coded, :dummy, :effects or :full_dummy, or with nothing the regression's default coding, from its DefaultRegressionSettings, which is :dummy unless another is given. With :dummy, each is the difference between a level and the reference level, such as the difference between a clinical and a non-clinical group. With :effects, each is the difference between a level and the mean of the levels, and the difference of the reference level is minus the sum of the others, which gives the reference level a wider prior than the others when there are three levels or more. With :full_dummy, every level has a coefficient, which is added for the sessions of that level. Without an Intercept, each is the value of its level, and every level has the same prior. With an Intercept, or another predictor coded this way, only the priors tell their coefficients apart.\nname: the name of the predictor, which is the name of its column, or the names of its columns joined by two underscores, as in diagnosis__site. The coefficient of a level of one column is named after the predictor and the level, such as diagnosis_clinical, and that of a level of several columns after the level alone, by its symbol name, made as session_id_to_symbol makes that of a session, such as diagnosis_clinical__site_2.\n\nEach argument is kept in a field of the same name, with columns as a tuple of names.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using Distributions: Normal\n\njulia> CategoricalPredictor((:diagnosis, :site), Normal(0, 1); coding = :effects).name\n:diagnosis__site\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.CategoricalPredictor"},{"category":"type","location":"api/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.ContinuousPredictor","page":"Parameter models","text":"ContinuousPredictor(column, prior=nothing; name=column)\n\nA continuous predictor of a RegressionParameter: a coefficient which is multiplied by the value of column in each session, a column of the table that the sessions were split from whose values are numbers, with prior as its prior, such as Normal(0, 0.5), or with nothing the regression's default prior for a continuous predictor, from its DefaultRegressionPriors. In VaryingEffects it is a varying slope, a deviation of the coefficient for each level, and prior is the prior of the standard deviation of the deviations. The predictor and its coefficient are named name. Each argument is kept in a field of the same name.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using Distributions: Normal\n\njulia> ContinuousPredictor(:age, Normal(0, 0.5); name = :years).column\n:age\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.ContinuousPredictor"},{"category":"type","location":"api/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.DefaultRegressionPriors","page":"Parameter models","text":"DefaultRegressionPriors(; intercept=nothing, continuous_predictor=nothing,\n                        categorical_predictor=nothing, interaction=nothing,\n                        varying_intercept=nothing, varying_slope=nothing)\n\nThe priors that a RegressionParameter gives to the terms that are given without one, by their kind: intercept for an Intercept, continuous_predictor for a ContinuousPredictor, categorical_predictor for a CategoricalPredictor and interaction for an Interaction, and for the terms of VaryingEffects the priors of the standard deviations of their deviations, varying_intercept for an Intercept and varying_slope for a predictor or an interaction. With nothing, terms of that kind need a prior of their own. Each prior is kept in a field of the same name.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using Distributions: Normal, Exponential\n\njulia> default_priors = DefaultRegressionPriors(; continuous_predictor = Normal(0, 0.5), varying_intercept = Exponential(1));\n\njulia> learning_rate = RegressionParameter(Intercept(Normal(0, 1)), ContinuousPredictor(:age), VaryingEffects(:participant, Intercept()); default_priors);\n\njulia> learning_rate.terms.age.prior == Normal(0, 0.5)\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.DefaultRegressionPriors"},{"category":"type","location":"api/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.DefaultRegressionSettings","page":"Parameter models","text":"DefaultRegressionSettings(; coding=:dummy, centered=false)\n\nThe settings that a RegressionParameter gives to the terms that are given without them: coding, the coding of the levels of a CategoricalPredictor, which is :dummy, :effects or :full_dummy, and centered, whether the deviations of VaryingEffects are sampled from their normal distribution directly. A term's own setting is used when it has one. Each setting is kept in a field of the same name.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using Distributions: Normal, Exponential\n\njulia> default_settings = DefaultRegressionSettings(; coding = :effects, centered = true);\n\njulia> learning_rate = RegressionParameter(Intercept(Normal(0, 1)), CategoricalPredictor(:diagnosis, Normal(0, 1)), VaryingEffects(:participant, Intercept(Exponential(1))); default_settings);\n\njulia> learning_rate.terms.diagnosis.coding, learning_rate.terms.participant.centered\n(:effects, true)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.DefaultRegressionSettings"},{"category":"type","location":"api/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.HierarchicalRegressionParameters","page":"Parameter models","text":"HierarchicalRegressionParameters(parameters)\n\nThe parameter model of a full model which gives each parameter of every session by a regression on columns of the table that the sessions were split from, such as the age and the diagnosis of a participant, with varying effects, which are also called random effects. parameters is a NamedTuple with a RegressionParameter for each parameter that is fitted, which has the terms of its regression and their priors. It is kept in the field parameters. Useful for estimating the effect of a predictor on a parameter, or the difference between groups, together with the parameters of the sessions.\n\nA full model builds the design of every regression once, before it is made, from the table that MultipleSessionsInfo keeps, so sessions that were prepared one at a time can only have an intercept. The field designs is nothing until then, and afterwards has, for each parameter:\n\ncoefficient_priors and design_columns: the prior of each coefficient of the intercept, the predictors and the interactions, and the values it is multiplied by, one for every session, under the name of the coefficient: 1 for the intercept, the values of a continuous predictor, and those of the coefficients of a categorical predictor and of an interaction, as CategoricalPredictor and Interaction describe them.\nreference_levels: the reference level of each categorical predictor, which is nothing with the coding :full_dummy, and under the name of an interaction those of the categorical predictors it was given themselves, in a NamedTuple.\nvarying_effects: for the varying effects of each column, the prior of the standard deviation of the deviations of each of their coefficients, coefficient_sd_priors, which is a NamedTuple with the prior under the symbol name of each group when the levels are grouped, the values that the deviations are multiplied by, design_columns, the reference_levels of their predictors, whether they are centered, and levels. These have the level of every session, level_of_each_session, the number of levels, n_levels, the symbol name of each level, level_symbol_names, and groups, which is nothing without groups and otherwise has the group of every level, group_of_each_level, the number of groups, n_groups, and the symbol name of each group, group_symbol_names.\n\nExamples\n\nusing Turing\nusing LogExpFunctions: logistic\n\nparameters_model = HierarchicalRegressionParameters((;\n    learning_rate = RegressionParameter(\n        Intercept(Normal(0, 1)),\n        ContinuousPredictor(:age, Normal(0, 0.5)),\n        CategoricalPredictor(:diagnosis, Normal(0, 1); reference_level = \"control\"),\n        CategoricalPredictor(:condition, Normal(0, 1)),\n        Interaction((:diagnosis, :condition), Normal(0, 0.5)),\n        VaryingEffects(\n            :participant,\n            Intercept(Exponential(1)),\n            CategoricalPredictor(:condition, Exponential(0.5));\n            group_by = :diagnosis,\n        ),\n        VaryingEffects((:id, :block), Intercept(Exponential(1)); name = :session);\n        transformation = logistic,\n    ),\n    inverse_temperature = RegressionParameter(\n        Intercept(Normal(0, 1)),\n        VaryingEffects((:id, :block), Intercept(Exponential(1)); name = :session);\n        transformation = exp,\n    ),\n))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.HierarchicalRegressionParameters"},{"category":"type","location":"api/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.Interaction","page":"Parameter models","text":"Interaction(predictors, prior=nothing; name)\n\nAn interaction of the predictors in predictors, a tuple of two or more ContinuousPredictors or CategoricalPredictors of the same RegressionParameter or VaryingEffects, each given by its name or itself. A predictor given by its name, such as :age, is a term there itself, with coefficients of its own, which are its main effect. A predictor given itself, such as ContinuousPredictor(:age), is part of the interaction only, which then comes without that main effect: it has no coefficients of its own, so it is given without a prior, and a categorical one has a coding and a reference level of its own. With Interaction((ContinuousPredictor(:age), CategoricalPredictor(:diagnosis; coding = :full_dummy))), each diagnosis has its own slope of the age.\n\nThe interaction has a coefficient for each combination of one coefficient of each predictor, which is multiplied by the product of their values, as the predictors give them after coding the levels, with prior as its prior, such as Normal(0, 0.5), or with nothing the regression's default prior for an interaction, from its DefaultRegressionPriors. Two continuous predictors give one coefficient, a continuous and a categorical predictor one for each coefficient of the categorical predictor, and two categorical predictors one for each combination of their coefficients. In VaryingEffects it gives varying slopes, and prior is the prior of the standard deviation of their deviations.\n\nThe interaction is named name. By default, that is the names of its predictors joined by _by_, as in diagnosis_by_condition. Each of its coefficients is named after the coefficients it multiplies, joined by _by_, such as diagnosis_clinical_by_condition_punishment, whatever the interaction itself is named. Each argument is kept in a field of the same name, with predictors as a tuple.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using Distributions: Normal\n\njulia> Interaction((:diagnosis, :condition), Normal(0, 0.5)).name\n:diagnosis_by_condition\n\njulia> Interaction((ContinuousPredictor(:age), :diagnosis), Normal(0, 0.5)).name\n:age_by_diagnosis\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.Interaction"},{"category":"type","location":"api/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.Intercept","page":"Parameter models","text":"Intercept(prior=nothing; name=:intercept)\n\nThe intercept of a RegressionParameter: a coefficient that every session has, with prior as its prior, such as Normal(0, 1), or with nothing the regression's default prior for an intercept, from its DefaultRegressionPriors. In VaryingEffects it is a varying intercept, a deviation for each level, and prior is the prior of the standard deviation of the deviations, such as Exponential(1). Its coefficient is named name. Each argument is kept in a field of the same name.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using Distributions: Normal\n\njulia> Intercept(Normal(0, 1)).name\n:intercept\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.Intercept"},{"category":"type","location":"api/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.RegressionFamily","page":"Parameter models","text":"RegressionFamily(distribution_parameters; distribution)\n\nThe family of a RegressionParameter: the distribution that the value of each session is sampled from, with one of its parameters given by the regression, as in a generalized linear model.\n\nArguments\n\ndistribution_parameters::NamedTuple: the parameters of the distribution, in the order in which it takes them, as the priors of a HierarchicalParameter are, with RegressionOutcome for the one that the regression gives and a prior for each of the others, such as (; mean = RegressionOutcome(), sd = Exponential(1)). Each of the others is sampled once, for every session, and their names name their variables.\n\nKeyword arguments\n\ndistribution: the distribution, as a function of its parameters, such as Normal, OrderedLogistic or (mean, dispersion) -> NegativeBinomial(dispersion, dispersion / (dispersion + mean)).\n\nThe argument is kept in the field distribution_parameters, and the distribution in distribution, inside a function which makes it when it is given as a type. The field priors has the priors among the parameters, without the outcome of the regression.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using Distributions: Normal, Exponential\n\njulia> family = RegressionFamily((; mean = RegressionOutcome(), sd = Exponential(1)); distribution = Normal);\n\njulia> keys(family.priors)\n(:sd,)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.RegressionFamily"},{"category":"type","location":"api/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.RegressionOutcome","page":"Parameter models","text":"RegressionOutcome()\n\nThe marker for the outcome of the regression, which is the transformed sum of each session, among the parameters of the distribution of a RegressionFamily: it stands in the place of the prior of the parameter that the regression gives.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.RegressionOutcome"},{"category":"type","location":"api/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.RegressionParameter","page":"Parameter models","text":"RegressionParameter(terms...; transformation=identity, family=nothing,\n                    default_priors=DefaultRegressionPriors(),\n                    default_settings=DefaultRegressionSettings(), name=nothing)\nRegressionParameter(formula, terms...; categorical_predictors=(), priors=(;),\n                    transformation=identity, family=nothing,\n                    default_priors=DefaultRegressionPriors(),\n                    default_settings=DefaultRegressionSettings())\n\nHow one parameter of a HierarchicalRegressionParameters is given to every session: by a regression on columns of the table that the sessions were split from, whose terms are an Intercept, ContinuousPredictors, CategoricalPredictors, Interactions and VaryingEffects. The value of a session is the sum of each coefficient of the intercept, the predictors and the interactions times its value in the session, and of the deviations of the session's levels of the varying effects, each times its value in the session, with transformation applied to the sum. Each session must have one value of every column that is used, which isn't missing. The second form writes the terms as a formula, see below.\n\nA regression commonly has varying effects whose levels are the sessions, over the column or columns that identify them, such as VaryingEffects(:id, Intercept(Exponential(1))), which give each session a value of its own. Without them, sessions with the same values in every column that is used have the same value of the parameter.\n\nWith a family, the value of a session is sampled from a distribution instead, one of whose parameters is the transformed sum of the session, as in a generalized linear model. A discrete parameter needs one, since a sum is never discrete: with family = BernoulliLogit, the sum is the log odds that the parameter is true in a session. The values of the sessions are then variables of their own.\n\nEvery term has a name of its own, which its coefficients or its variables are named after. A term is named after its column or columns, and an interaction after its predictors, unless it is given another name.\n\nKeyword arguments\n\ntransformation=identity: a function applied to the sum for each session, such as exp for a parameter that must be positive or logistic for one between 0 and 1, as an inverse link function does in a regression.\nfamily=nothing: the distribution that the value of each session is sampled from. A distribution whose only parameter is the transformed sum of the session is given as it is, as a function of that sum, such as BernoulliLogit, LogPoisson or logit -> BinomialLogit(7, logit). One with other parameters is given as a RegressionFamily, which has the priors of the others, such as RegressionFamily((; mean = RegressionOutcome(), sd = Exponential(1)); distribution = Normal). With nothing, the value of a session is its transformed sum.\ndefault_priors=DefaultRegressionPriors(): the priors of the terms that are given without one, by their kind, as a DefaultRegressionPriors.\ndefault_settings=DefaultRegressionSettings(): the coding of the categorical predictors and whether varying effects are centered, for the terms that are given without them, as a DefaultRegressionSettings.\nname=nothing: the name of the parameter, which a HierarchicalRegressionParameters checks against the name the regression is given under, or nothing for no check. A formula gives it.\n\nThe terms are kept in the field terms, as a NamedTuple with each term under its name, those without a prior with the one from default_priors, and those without a coding or a choice of centering with the ones from default_settings. The transformation, the family and the name are kept in fields of the same name, with a family that is given as a distribution in a RegressionFamily of it.\n\nFormulas\n\nWith StatsModels.jl loaded, the terms can be written as a @formula, in the syntax of MixedModels.jl, such as @formula(learning_rate ~ 1 + age + diagnosis + (1 + condition | participant)). Its left-hand side is the name of the parameter. An intercept is included unless the formula has 0 +, and the same holds within varying effects. Columns joined by & are an Interaction, and a * b gives a, b and their interaction. A column of an interaction that isn't a term of its own in the same sum is given to the Interaction itself, so that age & diagnosis on its own is the interaction without the main effects. (terms | columns) are VaryingEffects over columns, or over the combinations of the values of several columns joined by &, as in (1 | participant & block), and their deviations are independent, where MixedModels.jl correlates those within one bar. Terms that a formula can't express, such as VaryingEffects with group_by, are given after the formula.\n\nA column is a ContinuousPredictor, unless categorical_predictors has it, which makes it a CategoricalPredictor. categorical_predictors takes the name of a column, such as :diagnosis, or a tuple of names, such as (:diagnosis, :site). A column that is named has the coding of default_settings, and the level of the first session as its reference level, where StatsModels.jl takes the first level in sorted order. In the place of a name, a CategoricalPredictor gives the coding or the reference level of its column, such as CategoricalPredictor(:diagnosis; coding = :effects, reference_level = \"control\"). It reads one column and is named after it, and its prior is given in priors, as those of the other terms are. A column is the same predictor wherever the formula has it: as a term of its own, in an interaction and in varying effects.\n\nEach term is named as the term of its kind is named, and priors gives the priors of terms by those names, such as (; age = Normal(0, 0.05), age_by_diagnosis = Normal(0, 0.5), participant = (; intercept = Exponential(0.5))), with the name of an interaction joining those of its predictors by _by_, and with a NamedTuple for the terms of varying effects. The other terms have the default priors.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using Distributions: Normal, Exponential\n\njulia> default_priors = DefaultRegressionPriors(; intercept = Normal(0, 1), continuous_predictor = Normal(0, 0.5), categorical_predictor = Normal(0, 1), interaction = Normal(0, 0.5), varying_intercept = Exponential(1));\n\njulia> learning_rate = RegressionParameter(Intercept(), ContinuousPredictor(:age), CategoricalPredictor(:diagnosis), Interaction((:age, :diagnosis)), VaryingEffects(:participant, Intercept()); default_priors);\n\njulia> keys(learning_rate.terms)\n(:intercept, :age, :diagnosis, :age_by_diagnosis, :participant)\n\nA discrete parameter, which is true in a session with log odds that depend on the age, and a parameter whose value in a session is sampled around the sum of its regression, with a standard deviation that is sampled too:\n\nuses_rewards = RegressionParameter(\n    Intercept(Normal(0, 1)), ContinuousPredictor(:age, Normal(0, 0.5));\n    family = BernoulliLogit,\n)\n\nbias = RegressionParameter(\n    Intercept(Normal(0, 1)), ContinuousPredictor(:age, Normal(0, 0.5));\n    family = RegressionFamily(\n        (; mean = RegressionOutcome(), sd = Exponential(1)); distribution = Normal\n    ),\n)\n\nA regression written as a formula, with varying intercepts of the participants whose standard deviations differ between diagnoses, which a formula can't express, after it:\n\nusing StatsModels\n\nlearning_rate = RegressionParameter(\n    @formula(learning_rate ~ 1 + age + diagnosis + age & diagnosis),\n    VaryingEffects(:participant, Intercept(); group_by = :diagnosis);\n    categorical_predictors = CategoricalPredictor(:diagnosis; reference_level = \"control\"),\n    priors = (; age = Normal(0, 0.05), age_by_diagnosis = Normal(0, 0.5)),\n    default_priors,\n)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.RegressionParameter"},{"category":"type","location":"api/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.VaryingEffects","page":"Parameter models","text":"VaryingEffects(columns, terms...; group_by=nothing, centered=nothing, name)\n\nEffects of a RegressionParameter that vary between the levels of columns, such as between participants: for each level, a deviation of each coefficient of its terms, from a normal distribution around zero whose standard deviation has the term's prior as its prior. Varying effects are also called random effects, and the other terms of a regression fixed effects. The levels are the values of columns, a column of the table that the sessions were split from, or the combinations of the values of several columns given as a tuple. The terms are an Intercept, for a varying intercept, and ContinuousPredictors, CategoricalPredictors and Interactions, for varying slopes, as in the examples below. A term without a prior has the regression's default prior for a varying intercept or a varying slope, from its DefaultRegressionPriors. Each coefficient has a standard deviation of its own. A varying slope needs values that differ between the sessions of a level, such as a condition that changes between the blocks of a participant, and a predictor that an interaction in varying effects names is a term of the same varying effects.\n\nKeyword arguments\n\ngroup_by=nothing: a column of the table that the sessions were split from, or a tuple of columns, whose values group the levels, so that each group has a standard deviation of its own, such as :diagnosis for a clinical and a non-clinical group of participants. The sessions of one level must all be in one group. With nothing, the levels share one standard deviation.\ncentered=nothing: whether each deviation is sampled from the normal distribution directly, with true, or as a standardized value which the standard deviation scales, with false, or with nothing as the regression's DefaultRegressionSettings say, which is false unless they say otherwise. The standardized form usually samples better when the data informs each deviation little.\nname: the name of the varying effects, which their variables are named under: the name of their column, or the names of their columns joined by two underscores, as in id__block.\n\nEach argument is kept in a field of the same name, with columns and group_by as tuples of names, group_by being empty without groups, and terms as a NamedTuple with each term under its name.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using Distributions: Exponential\n\njulia> by_participant = VaryingEffects(:participant, Intercept(Exponential(1)), ContinuousPredictor(:time, Exponential(0.5)); group_by = :diagnosis);\n\njulia> keys(by_participant.terms), by_participant.group_by\n((:intercept, :time), (:diagnosis,))\n\njulia> VaryingEffects((:id, :block), Intercept(Exponential(1))).name\n:id__block\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.VaryingEffects"},{"category":"function","location":"api/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.parameter_names","page":"Parameter models","text":"parameter_names(parameters_model)\n\nReturn the names of the parameters that parameters_model gives every session, as a tuple of Symbols, or nothing when they aren't known before it runs. prepare_full_model checks them against the parameters of the cognitive model and of the environment model, and CompositeParameters checks that no two of its parts give the same one.\n\nImplementation\n\nA parameter model of one's own can add a method of this function. The default returns nothing, so that nothing is checked before a fit.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> CognitiveModeling.parameter_names(FixedParameters((; scale = 2.0, offset = 0.0)))\n(:scale, :offset)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.parameter_names"},{"category":"function","location":"api/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.estimates_parameters","page":"Parameter models","text":"estimates_parameters(parameters_model)\n\nReturn whether parameters_model, a parameter model, estimates the parameters it gives from the data, instead of being given their values: false for NoParameters, FixedParameters and SessionwiseFixedParameters, whether any part does for a CompositeParameters, and true for every other kind. When the environment's parameters are estimated, a full model includes the environment in every session, so that the observations in the data inform them, and it warns when the parameters of the two models can have the same variables.\n\nImplementation\n\nA parameter model of one's own can add a method of this function. The default returns true, and a model which only gives values returns false.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> CognitiveModeling.estimates_parameters(FixedParameters((; scale = 2.0)))\nfalse\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.estimates_parameters"},{"category":"function","location":"api/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.prepare_parameters_model","page":"Parameter models","text":"prepare_parameters_model(parameters_model, sessions_info)\n\nReturn parameters_model prepared for the sessions in sessions_info, with what doesn't change between the evaluations of an inference backend's model worked out once, before the model is made. A SessionwiseFixedParameters has the values of every session looked up, a HierarchicalGroupedParameters has the group of every session found, a HierarchicalRegressionParameters has the design of every regression built, and a CompositeParameters has each of its parts prepared. Used by prepare_full_model.\n\nImplementation\n\nA parameter model of one's own can add a method of this function, which returns a parameter model that the inference backends have methods for. The default returns parameters_model as it is.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.prepare_parameters_model"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/within_models/#Utility-functions-within-models","page":"Within models","text":"What an inference backend's model of the sessions calls.\n\nPages = [\"within_models.md\"]","title":"Utility functions within models"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/within_models/#Reading-what-a-session-is-set-up-with","page":"Within models","text":"","title":"Reading what a session is set up with"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/within_models/#Reading-the-data-of-one-timestep","page":"Within models","text":"","title":"Reading the data of one timestep"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/within_models/#Values-to-condition-on","page":"Within models","text":"","title":"Values to condition on"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/within_models/#Saving-what-a-fitted-session-returns","page":"Within models","text":"","title":"Saving what a fitted session returns"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/within_models/#CognitiveModeling.check_environment_parameters-Tuple{SessionInfo{<:Any, <:CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel}, NamedTuple}","page":"Within models","text":"check_environment_parameters(session_info, environment_parameters)\ncheck_environment_parameters(sessions_info, environment_parameters)\n\nThrow an informative error if environment parameters are given for a session, or for sessions, without an environment model, which is decided by the types. Used by the models of several sessions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_environment_parameters"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/within_models/#CognitiveModeling.has_environment-Tuple{SessionInfo{<:Any, NoEnvironment}}","page":"Within models","text":"has_environment(session_info)\nhas_environment(sessions_info)\n\nReturn whether a SessionInfo has an environment model, or every session of a MultipleSessionsInfo has, which is decided by the type. A session can have an environment which doesn't run, see SessionInfo. Used by check_environment_parameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.has_environment"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/within_models/#CognitiveModeling.last_timestep_with_data-Tuple{NamedTuple}","page":"Within models","text":"last_timestep_with_data(columns)\n\nReturn the last timestep on which any column in columns, a NamedTuple of vectors, has a value that isn't MissingValue, and 0 when none has. Any other marker counts as data, since it says that the timestep happened. Used by first_timestep_to_check_stopping.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.last_timestep_with_data"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/within_models/#CognitiveModeling.sessions_save_anything-Tuple{MultipleSessionsInfo}","page":"Within models","text":"sessions_save_anything(sessions_info)\n\nReturn whether the save history of either model of any session in sessions_info selects anything. Used by the full models of the inference backends, to warn when the sessions save trajectories that nothing in the model uses.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.sessions_save_anything"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/within_models/#CognitiveModeling.build_named_entry-Union{Tuple{name}, Tuple{Val{name}, Any}} where name","page":"Within models","text":"build_named_entry(::Val{name}, value)\n\nReturn (; name = value). A NamedTuple which holds a value that can have two types is built with merge of these. Used by select_timestep_values_to_save and by the timesteps of fitted sessions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.build_named_entry"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/within_models/#CognitiveModeling.has_missing_value-Tuple{NamedTuple}","page":"Within models","text":"has_missing_value(data)\n\nReturn whether any entry of data, the NamedTuple of the actions or the observations of one timestep, is MissingValue. Used by the timesteps of fitted sessions, to skip imputing when nothing is missing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.has_missing_value"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/within_models/#CognitiveModeling.prepend_value-Tuple{Any, Tuple}","page":"Within models","text":"prepend_value(value, values)\n\nReturn the tuple values with value in front. Used by read_row_values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.prepend_value"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/within_models/#CognitiveModeling.read_row_values-Tuple{Tuple{}, Any}","page":"Within models","text":"read_row_values(columns, t)\n\nReturn the values at timestep t of columns, a tuple of vectors, as a tuple. Each value is put in front of the values of the remaining columns by prepend_value, a call of its own, so that a value which can have two types gives one of two tuples with concrete elements, since the compiler splits a call over the types of its arguments. Used by select_row.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.read_row_values"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/within_models/#CognitiveModeling.select_row-Union{Tuple{names}, Tuple{NamedTuple{names}, Any}} where names","page":"Within models","text":"select_row(columns, t)\n\nReturn the NamedTuple of the values at timestep t of columns, a NamedTuple of vectors with one entry for each timestep. The values are read as a tuple with read_row_values, so that a column which holds two types, such as an action that is MissingValue on some timesteps, gives one of two NamedTuples with concrete fields rather than one without a definite type. Used by the timesteps of fitted sessions, by the models of several sessions, which read the parameters of one session with it, and by a FixedObservationsEnvironment, which reads the observations of a timestep.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.select_row"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/within_models/#CognitiveModeling.action_names_to_condition_on-Tuple{SessionInfo}","page":"Within models","text":"action_names_to_condition_on(session_info)\nobservation_names_to_condition_on(session_info)\n\nReturn the names of the actions, or of the observations, that have values to condition on in the data of a session, which are scored against the distributions the cognitive model, or the environment model, gave for them, in a Val. A session without an environment scores no observations, so it has no such names. Used by fitted sessions, to select the distributions they score.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.action_names_to_condition_on"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/within_models/#CognitiveModeling.find_outputs_to_condition_on-Union{Tuple{names}, Tuple{NamedTuple{names}, NamedTuple, Any}} where names","page":"Within models","text":"find_outputs_to_condition_on(data_output, output_distribution, model)\n\nReturn whether each output of model on one timestep has a value to condition on in the data, as a NamedTuple of Bools with the names of data_output: the actions of a cognitive model, or the observations an environment model gives. Such a value is scored against the distribution the model gave for it in output_distribution, so it is an error when the model gave nothing for it, NoValue. Used by the timesteps of fitted sessions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.find_outputs_to_condition_on"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/within_models/#CognitiveModeling.initialize_output_distributions_for_conditioning-Tuple{NamedTuple}","page":"Within models","text":"initialize_output_distributions_for_conditioning(distributions_for_conditioning)\n\nReturn an empty vector for the distributions of each output that has values to condition on in a session, typed by the distribution the model gave on the first timestep, so that the vectors keep one type through the session when every timestep gives the same type. An output the model doesn't give on the first timestep gets a Vector{Union{}}(), which the first distribution kept sets the type of, as for push_value!. Used by fitted sessions, after their first timestep.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.initialize_output_distributions_for_conditioning"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/within_models/#CognitiveModeling.is_value_to_condition_on-Tuple{Any}","page":"Within models","text":"is_value_to_condition_on(value)\n\nReturn whether a single action or observation in the data is a value to condition on: neither MissingValue nor an AbstractNoValue, such as UnimputedMissingValue or NoValue. Used by collect_values_to_condition_on and find_outputs_to_condition_on.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.is_value_to_condition_on"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/within_models/#CognitiveModeling.store_output_distributions_for_conditioning-Union{Tuple{names}, Tuple{NamedTuple{names}, NamedTuple, NamedTuple}} where names","page":"Within models","text":"store_output_distributions_for_conditioning(distributions,\n                                            distributions_for_conditioning,\n                                            outputs_to_condition_on)\n\nAdd the distributions of the outputs to condition on at one timestep, which outputs_to_condition_on says, to the vectors in distributions, one for each output that has values to condition on in the session, and return them. A distribution of another type than its vector widens the vector, as for push_value!. Used by fitted sessions, on every timestep.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.store_output_distributions_for_conditioning"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/within_models/#CognitiveModeling.select_cognitive_timestep_values_to_save-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Within models","text":"select_cognitive_timestep_values_to_save(cognitive_model, timestep_values, save_history)\nselect_environment_timestep_values_to_save(environment, environment_timestep_values,\n                                           save_history)\n\nReturn what save_history selects of one model's part of the values of a timestep: the states, observations, actions and action_distributions of the cognitive model, or the states, observations, actions and observation_distributions of the environment, from the values of the environment alone. The observation and the action can have one of two types, so they go in through build_named_entry. Used by select_timestep_values_to_save.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.select_cognitive_timestep_values_to_save"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/within_models/#CognitiveModeling.select_initial_timestep_values_to_save-Tuple{Val{true}, SessionInfo{<:Any, NoEnvironment}, Any}","page":"Within models","text":"select_initial_timestep_values_to_save(save_initial_timestep_values::Val, session_info,\n                                       previous)\n\nReturn what a session saves from before the first timestep, as timestep 0, when save_initial_timestep_values is Val(true), and nothing otherwise. previous has the values that the first timestep starts from, arranged as those of a timestep. The distributions of timestep 0 are BeforeFirstTimestep for every output of each model, since none was given yet. Used by fitted sessions, before their first timestep runs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.select_initial_timestep_values_to_save"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/within_models/#CognitiveModeling.select_timestep_values_to_save-Tuple{SessionInfo{<:Any, NoEnvironment}, Any}","page":"Within models","text":"select_timestep_values_to_save(session_info, timestep_values)\n\nReturn what a session saves of the values of one timestep, or of where it starts, as the save histories of session_info select it, arranged as the session returns it: the cognitive model's states, observations, actions and action_distributions, and with an environment its states, observations, actions and observation_distributions under environment, as map_saved arranges them. timestep_values has the cognitive model's state, observation, action and action_distribution, and under environment the environment's state, observation, action and observation_distribution, each in its own names. Used by fitted sessions, on every timestep.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.select_timestep_values_to_save"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/within_models/#CognitiveModeling.timestep_sizes_until-Tuple{AbstractVector, Any}","page":"Within models","text":"timestep_sizes_until(timestep_sizes, n_saved)\n\nReturn the vector of the sizes of the timesteps that a session returns, cut to its first n_saved elements, for a session that ended before its data did when a stopping_criterion was met. An empty vector of sizes, for a session that saves nothing, is kept. Used by fitted sessions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.timestep_sizes_until"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/within_models/#CognitiveModeling.with_empty_environment_entry-Tuple{NamedTuple}","page":"Within models","text":"with_empty_environment_entry(saved_trajectories)\n\nReturn saved_trajectories, what a session without an environment saved, with an environment entry whose vectors hold an empty NamedTuple for each saved timestep, for a session which has an environment but didn't include it, so that every session with an environment returns the same entries, in the same order, since the model's return otherwise has two types. Used by fitted sessions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.with_empty_environment_entry"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/interfacing_with_reinforcement_learning/#Models-from-ReinforcementLearning.jl-as-cognitive-models","page":"Models from ReinforcementLearning.jl as cognitive models","text":"An agent or a learner from ReinforcementLearning.jl can do the learning inside a cognitive model. The cognitive model keeps their object in its state, gives it what was observed on each timestep, and returns the probabilities of its actions, which a simulation samples from and fitting scores the recorded actions against.\n\nThis page shows two such cognitive models. It doesn't simulate or fit them yet, and its code isn't run when the documentation is built, because ReinforcementLearning.jl is a large package to have in the documentation's environment. The package's tests run it instead, with ReinforcementLearningCore.jl 0.15.\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\nusing Distributions: Categorical\nusing LogExpFunctions: softmax\nusing ReinforcementLearningBase: ReinforcementLearningBase as RLBase\nusing ReinforcementLearningCore: ReinforcementLearningCore as RLCore\nusing ReinforcementLearningCore: Agent, QBasedPolicy, TDLearner, TabularQApproximator\nusing ReinforcementLearningCore: EpsilonGreedyExplorer, PreEpisodeStage, PostActStage\nusing ReinforcementLearningTrajectories: Trajectory, CircularArraySARTSTraces\nusing ReinforcementLearningTrajectories: DummySampler, InsertSampleRatioController\n\nThe attributes of both models have the observations observation, reward and terminated as inputs, which are the names that CommonRLEnvironment gives, so they can be used with an environment from ReinforcementLearning.jl as they are. The observation must be the number of the state the environment is in, since the learners here keep a table of values.\n\nBoth also need the environment to move first on each timestep, with EnvironmentStarts, so that there is an observation to act on. On the first timestep of an episode there is no reward yet: the reward is then NoValue, and the models check for AbstractNoValue, which covers that.","title":"Models from ReinforcementLearning.jl as cognitive models"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/interfacing_with_reinforcement_learning/#A-whole-agent","page":"Models from ReinforcementLearning.jl as cognitive models","text":"An agent from ReinforcementLearning.jl reads the state, the reward and the end of the episode from an environment object, at the stages of its own run loop. This small environment holds what the cognitive model observed on a timestep, for the agent to read.\n\nstruct ObservedByAgent <: RLBase.AbstractEnv\n    state::Int\n    reward::Float64\n    terminated::Bool\n    n_actions::Int\nend\n\nRLBase.state(rl_env::ObservedByAgent) = rl_env.state\nRLBase.reward(rl_env::ObservedByAgent) = rl_env.reward\nRLBase.is_terminated(rl_env::ObservedByAgent) = rl_env.terminated\nRLBase.action_space(rl_env::ObservedByAgent) = Base.OneTo(rl_env.n_actions)\n\nThe cognitive model creates the agent in its initial state, from its parameters: here a temporal difference learner with a table of action values, which explores with a probability of epsilon.\n\nstruct TemporalDifferenceAgent <: AbstractCognitiveModel\n    n_states::Int\n    n_actions::Int\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(model::TemporalDifferenceAgent, parameters)\n\n    #The learner, with the learning rate and the discount factor from the parameters\n    rl_approximator = TabularQApproximator(;\n        n_state=model.n_states, n_action=model.n_actions\n    )\n    rl_learner = TDLearner(\n        rl_approximator, :SARS; α=parameters.learning_rate, γ=parameters.discount\n    )\n\n    #The explorer, which picks any action with a probability of epsilon\n    rl_explorer = EpsilonGreedyExplorer(parameters.epsilon; is_break_tie=true)\n\n    #The agent's memory of the last transition, which the learner learns from\n    rl_traces = CircularArraySARTSTraces(;\n        capacity=1,\n        state=Int64 => (),\n        action=Int64 => (),\n        reward=Float64 => (),\n        terminal=Bool => (),\n    )\n    rl_trajectory = Trajectory(rl_traces, DummySampler(), InsertSampleRatioController())\n\n    return (; rl_agent=Agent(QBasedPolicy(rl_learner, rl_explorer), rl_trajectory))\nend\n\nOn each timestep, the cognitive model takes the agent through the stage of its run loop that fits what was observed, and returns the probabilities its policy gives each action. The agent is changed in place, so the same object is passed on as the new state.\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.run_timestep(\n    model::TemporalDifferenceAgent, context::ModelContext\n)\n\n    # Unpack the context ##\n    rl_agent = context.previous_state.rl_agent\n    (; observation, reward, terminated) = context.input\n\n    # Update state ##\n    if reward isa AbstractNoValue\n        #An episode starts, so there is nothing to learn from yet\n        rl_env = ObservedByAgent(observation, 0.0, false, model.n_actions)\n        push!(rl_agent, PreEpisodeStage(), rl_env)\n    else\n        #Give the agent what followed its previous action, and let it learn from it\n        rl_env = ObservedByAgent(observation, reward, terminated, model.n_actions)\n        push!(rl_agent, PostActStage(), rl_env, context.previous_output.move)\n        RLBase.optimise!(rl_agent, PostActStage())\n    end\n\n    # Compute action probabilities ##\n    #The policy gives a distribution over the actions, which the action is sampled from\n    Pₜ = RLBase.prob(rl_agent.policy, rl_env)\n\n    return ModelOutput((; move=Pₜ), (; rl_agent))\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::TemporalDifferenceAgent)\n    return ModelAttributes(;\n        parameters=(:learning_rate, :discount, :epsilon),\n        states=:rl_agent,\n        inputs=(:observation, :reward, :terminated),\n        outputs=:move,\n    )\nend\n\nSince the agent is changed in place, every saved state would be the same object, showing the agent after the last timestep. This method saves a copy of the agent on each timestep instead, made with deepcopy. It keeps the name rl_agent, since a SaveHistory selects states by the names in attribute_names. The table of action values of a saved agent is in rl_agent.policy.learner.approximator.model.\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.prepare_state_for_saving(model::TemporalDifferenceAgent, state)\n    return (; rl_agent=deepcopy(state.rl_agent))\nend","title":"A whole agent"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/interfacing_with_reinforcement_learning/#Only-the-learner","page":"Models from ReinforcementLearning.jl as cognitive models","text":"A learner can also be used on its own, with one transition at a time, which needs no environment object and no trajectory. The action probabilities are then the cognitive model's own: here a softmax over the learner's action values, with an inverse temperature. The softmax is the one from LogExpFunctions.jl.\n\nstruct TemporalDifferenceLearner <: AbstractCognitiveModel\n    n_states::Int\n    n_actions::Int\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(model::TemporalDifferenceLearner, parameters)\n    rl_approximator = TabularQApproximator(;\n        n_state=model.n_states, n_action=model.n_actions\n    )\n    rl_learner = TDLearner(\n        rl_approximator, :SARS; α=parameters.learning_rate, γ=parameters.discount\n    )\n\n    #The learner needs the observation before the action too, so that is kept in the state\n    return (; rl_learner, previous_observation=0)\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.run_timestep(\n    model::TemporalDifferenceLearner, context::ModelContext\n)\n\n    # Unpack the context ##\n    (; rl_learner, previous_observation) = context.previous_state\n    (; observation, reward, terminated) = context.input\n    β = context.parameters.inverse_temperature\n\n    # Update state ##\n    #Let the learner learn from what followed the previous action, unless an episode starts\n    if !(reward isa AbstractNoValue)\n        rl_transition = (;\n            state=previous_observation,\n            next_state=observation,\n            action=context.previous_output.move,\n            reward,\n            terminal=terminated,\n        )\n        RLBase.optimise!(rl_learner, rl_transition)\n    end\n\n    # Compute action probabilities ##\n    #The learner's value of each action where the agent is now\n    Qₜ = RLCore.forward(rl_learner, observation)\n    #Softmax over the action values\n    Pₜ = Categorical(softmax(β .* Qₜ))\n\n    return ModelOutput((; move=Pₜ), (; rl_learner, previous_observation=observation))\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::TemporalDifferenceLearner)\n    return ModelAttributes(;\n        parameters=(:learning_rate, :discount, :inverse_temperature),\n        states=(:rl_learner, :previous_observation),\n        inputs=(:observation, :reward, :terminated),\n        outputs=:move,\n    )\nend\n\nThe learner is changed in place too, so a copy of it is saved in the same way.\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.prepare_state_for_saving(model::TemporalDifferenceLearner, state)\n    return (; rl_learner=deepcopy(state.rl_learner), state.previous_observation)\nend","title":"Only the learner"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/interfacing_with_reinforcement_learning/#Fitting","page":"Models from ReinforcementLearning.jl as cognitive models","text":"This page doesn't fit the cognitive models yet. When they are fitted with a sampler that uses gradients, use Enzyme.jl in reverse mode with runtime activity as the automatic differentiation backend, which for Turing.jl is AutoEnzyme(; mode = set_runtime_activity(Reverse), function_annotation = Const). It gave the right gradient of the log density of a session for both cognitive models here, when checked against finite differences with Enzyme.jl 0.13. ForwardDiff.jl and ReverseDiff.jl can't differentiate either of them, because the learner keeps its learning rate and its discount in Float64 fields, which the number types of those two don't fit in.\n\nUse the softmax from LogExpFunctions.jl, as above, and not one written by hand. Written in one line, a softmax can get a wrong gradient from Enzyme's reverse mode, with no error, which the page Automatic differentiation and Reactant describes under its pitfalls.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Fitting"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/cognitive_abm_with_agents/#Cognitive-agent-based-modeling-with-Agents.jl","page":"Cognitive agent-based modeling with Agents.jl","text":"Placeholder tutorial. It will show how to put a CognitiveAgent into an agent-based model from Agents.jl, with what each agent observes in the world and what its actions do to it.\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Cognitive agent-based modeling with Agents.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/saving_histories/#Internals-of-saving-histories","page":"Saving histories","text":"Pages = [\"saving_histories.md\"]","title":"Internals of saving histories"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/saving_histories/#Types","page":"Saving histories","text":"","title":"Types"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/saving_histories/#Utility-functions","page":"Saving histories","text":"","title":"Utility functions"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/saving_histories/#Reading-the-selection-of-a-save-history","page":"Saving histories","text":"","title":"Reading the selection of a save history"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/saving_histories/#Selecting-the-values-to-save","page":"Saving histories","text":"","title":"Selecting the values to save"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/saving_histories/#Checking-the-names-a-save-history-selects","page":"Saving histories","text":"","title":"Checking the names a save history selects"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/saving_histories/#The-sizes-of-the-timesteps-that-are-saved","page":"Saving histories","text":"","title":"The sizes of the timesteps that are saved"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/saving_histories/#Storing-what-is-saved","page":"Saving histories","text":"","title":"Storing what is saved"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/saving_histories/#CognitiveModeling.as_selection-Tuple{SaveEverything}","page":"Saving histories","text":"as_selection(names)\n\nReturn names as a selection, which is how a SaveHistory keeps it: SaveEverything as it is, and otherwise a Tuple of Symbols, as as_tuple makes it. Used by the constructor of SaveHistory.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.as_selection"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/saving_histories/#CognitiveModeling.saves_initial_timestep_values-Union{Tuple{SaveHistory{Tstates, Tinputs, Toutputs, Toutput_distributions, Tsave_initial_timestep_values}}, Tuple{Tsave_initial_timestep_values}, Tuple{Toutput_distributions}, Tuple{Toutputs}, Tuple{Tinputs}, Tuple{Tstates}} where {Tstates, Tinputs, Toutputs, Toutput_distributions, Tsave_initial_timestep_values}","page":"Saving histories","text":"saves_initial_timestep_values(save_history)\n\nReturn whether save_history saves the values from before the first timestep, as timestep\n\nUsed by simulate, InstantiatedModel, saved_timestep_sizes\n\nand fitted sessions, to decide whether timestep 0 is saved.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.saves_initial_timestep_values"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/saving_histories/#CognitiveModeling.saves_nothing-Union{Tuple{SaveHistory{(), (), (), (), Tsave_initial_timestep_values}}, Tuple{Tsave_initial_timestep_values}} where Tsave_initial_timestep_values","page":"Saving histories","text":"saves_nothing(save_history)\n\nReturn whether save_history selects nothing of any kind. Whether it saves the initial timestep values doesn't matter, since there is nothing to save of them. Used by simulate!, InstantiatedModel and fitted sessions, which save nothing at all then.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.saves_nothing"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/saving_histories/#CognitiveModeling.state_selection-Union{Tuple{SaveHistory{Tstates, Tinputs, Toutputs, Toutput_distributions, Tsave_initial_timestep_values}}, Tuple{Tsave_initial_timestep_values}, Tuple{Toutput_distributions}, Tuple{Toutputs}, Tuple{Tinputs}, Tuple{Tstates}} where {Tstates, Tinputs, Toutputs, Toutput_distributions, Tsave_initial_timestep_values}","page":"Saving histories","text":"state_selection(save_history)\ninput_selection(save_history)\noutput_selection(save_history)\noutput_distribution_selection(save_history)\n\nReturn the selection of one kind in a SaveHistory as a Val. Used wherever something is saved, by simulations, instantiated models and fitted sessions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.state_selection"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/saving_histories/#CognitiveModeling.select_names-Tuple{Any, Val{SaveEverything()}}","page":"Saving histories","text":"select_names(x, selection::Val)\n\nReturn what a SaveHistory selects of x: x itself for SaveEverything, an empty NamedTuple for no names, and the named entries of a NamedTuple for a tuple of names. Used wherever something is saved, by simulations, instantiated models and fitted sessions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.select_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/saving_histories/#CognitiveModeling.select_state_to_save-Tuple{Any, Any, Val}","page":"Saving histories","text":"select_state_to_save(model, state, selection::Val)\n\nReturn what is saved of state: what selection selects of the output of prepare_state_for_saving. When nothing is selected, the state isn't prepared at all, so that no copy is made of a state which isn't saved. Used wherever a state is saved, by simulations, instantiated models and fitted sessions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.select_state_to_save"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/saving_histories/#CognitiveModeling.check_savehistory_selection_names-Tuple{SaveHistory, CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel}","page":"Saving histories","text":"check_savehistory_selection_names(save_history, model)\n\nThrow an informative error if save_history selects a state, an input, an output or an output distribution by name that isn't in the attributes of model, a cognitive model or an environment model, from attribute_names, since a history starts from those names, and prepare_state_for_saving returns a state with them. Used by InstantiatedModel, simulate and SessionInfo, before anything is saved.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_savehistory_selection_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/saving_histories/#CognitiveModeling.check_selection_names-Tuple{SaveEverything, Any, Any, Any}","page":"Saving histories","text":"check_selection_names(selection, names, kind, model)\n\nThrow an informative error if selection has a name that isn't among names, those of kind in the attributes of model, where kind is \"states\", \"inputs\", \"outputs\" or \"output distributions\", the last being named like the outputs. SaveEverything can always be selected. Used by check_savehistory_selection_names for each kind.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_selection_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/saving_histories/#CognitiveModeling.each_timestep_size-Tuple{AbstractVector, Any}","page":"Saving histories","text":"each_timestep_size(timestep_sizes, n_steps)\n\nReturn the size of each of n_steps timesteps as a vector, for a history. A vector of sizes is returned as it is, and anything else is repeated for every timestep. Used by simulate!, for the sizes it adds to the histories, and by saved_timestep_sizes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.each_timestep_size"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/saving_histories/#CognitiveModeling.returned_timestep_sizes-Tuple{AbstractVector, Any, Val{false}}","page":"Saving histories","text":"returned_timestep_sizes(timestep_sizes, n_timesteps, save_initial_timestep_values::Val)\n\nReturn the sizes of the n_timesteps timesteps that ran, as a simulation returns them. A vector of sizes is cut to those timesteps, since a model can stop a simulation early, and starts with BeforeFirstTimestep for timestep 0 when save_initial_timestep_values is Val(true), so that it has an element for each element of what is saved. Anything else is returned as it is, since it is the size of every timestep. Used by simulate, simulate! and saved_timestep_sizes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.returned_timestep_sizes"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/saving_histories/#CognitiveModeling.saved_timestep_sizes-Tuple{Any, Any, SaveHistory}","page":"Saving histories","text":"saved_timestep_sizes(timestep_sizes, n_steps, save_history)\n\nReturn the sizes of n_steps timesteps as they are returned with a history: one size for each timestep when save_history saves anything, after BeforeFirstTimestep for timestep 0 when it saves the initial timestep values, and an empty vector when it saves nothing. Used by SessionInfo, for the sizes a fitted session returns.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.saved_timestep_sizes"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/saving_histories/#CognitiveModeling.initialize_storage-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Saving histories","text":"initialize_storage(first_value, n_steps)\ninitialize_storage(initial_value, first_value, n_steps)\n\nCreate the vector for saving one kind of value over n_steps timesteps, using the value of the first timestep to set its type. With initial_value, what is saved from before the first timestep, the vector starts with it, as timestep 0, and its type holds both. Used by simulations and by fitted sessions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.initialize_storage"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/saving_histories/#CognitiveModeling.push_value!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Vector{T}, Any}} where T","page":"Saving histories","text":"push_value!(storage, value)\n\nAdd value at the end of storage, and return the vector. As for store_value!, a value that doesn't fit the type of storage moves the values so far to a wider vector, which is returned instead. Starting from Vector{Union{}}() lets the first value set the type. Used by fitted sessions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.push_value!"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/saving_histories/#CognitiveModeling.store_value!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Vector{T}, Any, Any}} where T","page":"Saving histories","text":"store_value!(storage, value, t)\n\nSave value as element t of storage, and return the vector.\n\nIf the value doesn't fit the type of storage, the values so far are moved to a wider vector which can hold both types, and that vector is returned instead. Used by simulations and by fitted sessions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.store_value!"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/parameter_models/#Internals-of-parameter-models","page":"Parameter models","text":"Pages = [\"parameter_models.md\"]","title":"Internals of parameter models"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.collect_parameters_in_session_order-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Parameter models","text":"collect_parameters_in_session_order(parameters_by_session, session_ids)\n\nReturn the parameters of each session in parameters_by_session, as SessionwiseFixedParameters holds them, as one vector for each parameter with a value for every session, in the order of session_ids, with the parameters of each session found by parameters_of_session. A session with other parameters than the first is an error. Parameters that are already in the order of the sessions are returned as they are, after checking that each vector has a value for every session. Used by prepare_parameters_model, and by the models of SessionwiseFixedParameters in the inference backends.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.collect_parameters_in_session_order"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.has_vector_for_each_parameter-Tuple{Any}","page":"Parameter models","text":"has_vector_for_each_parameter(parameters_by_session)\n\nReturn whether parameters_by_session, as a SessionwiseFixedParameters holds it, gives the parameters in the order of the sessions, as a NamedTuple with one vector for each parameter, and not by the sessions' symbol names or data identifiers. Used by parameter_names and collect_parameters_in_session_order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.has_vector_for_each_parameter"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.parameters_of_session-Tuple{NamedTuple, Any}","page":"Parameter models","text":"parameters_of_session(parameters_by_session, session_id)\n\nReturn the parameters of the session with the data identifier session_id in parameters_by_session: the entry under the session's symbol name, as session_id_to_symbol gives it, in a NamedTuple, and the entry under the data identifier itself in a dictionary. A session without parameters is an error. Used by collect_parameters_in_session_order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.parameters_of_session"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.ConstructorOf","page":"Parameter models","text":"ConstructorOf{T}()\n\nA function which makes an object of the type T from its arguments, so that ConstructorOf{Normal}()(0, 1) is Normal(0, 1). Used by HierarchicalParameter for a group-level distribution that is given as a type, and by RegressionFamily for a distribution that is.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.ConstructorOf"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.check_is_hierarchical_parameter-Tuple{Symbol, HierarchicalParameter}","page":"Parameter models","text":"check_is_hierarchical_parameter(name, hierarchical_parameter)\n\nThrow an informative error if what a HierarchicalGroupedParameters was given for the parameter name isn't a HierarchicalParameter. Used by the constructor of HierarchicalGroupedParameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_is_hierarchical_parameter"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.AbstractRegressionTerm","page":"Parameter models","text":"AbstractRegressionTerm\n\nSupertype of the terms of a RegressionParameter: Intercept, ContinuousPredictor, CategoricalPredictor, Interaction and VaryingEffects. Each has its name in the field name, and each but VaryingEffects has its prior in the field prior.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.AbstractRegressionTerm"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.as_regression_family-Tuple{Nothing}","page":"Parameter models","text":"as_regression_family(family)\n\nReturn family, what the keyword argument family of a RegressionParameter was given, as a RegressionFamily or nothing: itself when it is one of them, and otherwise a RegressionFamily of the distribution family, whose only parameter is the outcome of the regression, such as BernoulliLogit. Used by the constructor of RegressionParameter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.as_regression_family"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.build_coefficients-Tuple{MultipleSessionsInfo, NamedTuple, Symbol, String}","page":"Parameter models","text":"build_coefficients(sessions_info, terms, parameter_name, description)\n\nReturn the coefficients of terms, a NamedTuple with an intercept, predictors and interactions of description, such as \"the regression of learning_rate\", for the sessions in sessions_info, as a NamedTuple with the prior of each coefficient, priors, and the values it is multiplied by in every session, columns, both under the name of the coefficient and in the order of the terms, and with the reference level of each categorical predictor, reference_levels, under the names of the predictors, and those of the categorical predictors that an interaction was given themselves in a NamedTuple under the name of the interaction. The coefficients of each term come from build_term_columns and build_interaction_columns, and must have names of their own. Used by build_regression_design and build_varying_effects_design.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.build_coefficients"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.build_contrast_columns-Tuple{MultipleSessionsInfo, CategoricalPredictor, Symbol}","page":"Parameter models","text":"build_contrast_columns(sessions_info, predictor, parameter_name)\n\nReturn the coefficients of predictor, a CategoricalPredictor in the regression of the parameter parameter_name, in a NamedTuple with names, columns and reference_level: for each level other than the reference level, or for every level with the coding :full_dummy, in the order of their first session, the name of its coefficient and a vector with 1 for the sessions of that level and 0 for the others. The levels are the values of a single column, whose coefficients are named after the predictor and the level, as in diagnosis_clinical, or the NamedTuples of the values of several, whose coefficients are named by the symbol name of the level alone, as in diagnosis_clinical__site_2. With the coding :effects, the sessions of the reference level have -1 in every column. The reference level is the predictor's, or the level of the first session, and nothing with the coding :full_dummy. It is an error when there is one level only, and when the reference level isn't one of them. Used by build_term_columns.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.build_contrast_columns"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.build_family_distribution-Tuple{RegressionFamily, Any, Any}","page":"Parameter models","text":"build_family_distribution(family, regression_outcome, sampled_values)\n\nReturn the distribution that the value of one session is sampled from: the distribution of family, a RegressionFamily, with regression_outcome, the transformed sum of the session, in the place of RegressionOutcome, and sampled_values, the values of the other parameters in the order of their priors, as a NamedTuple or a tuple, in the places of their priors, as merge_family_arguments arranges them. Used by the models of the inference backends.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.build_family_distribution"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.build_interaction_columns-Tuple{MultipleSessionsInfo, Interaction, AbstractDict, Symbol, String}","page":"Parameter models","text":"build_interaction_columns(sessions_info, interaction, columns_of_predictors,\n                          parameter_name, description)\n\nReturn the coefficients of interaction, an Interaction of description, such as \"the regression of learning_rate\", as build_term_columns returns those of a predictor: one for each combination of one coefficient of each of its predictors, named after the coefficients it multiplies joined by _by_, with the product of their columns. The coefficients of each predictor come from columns_of_interaction_predictor. The combinations are in the order of the coefficients of the first predictor, then of the second, and so on. The reference levels of the categorical predictors that the interaction was given themselves are returned as reference_levels, under their names. A column that is zero in every session is an error, since no session informs its coefficient. Used by build_coefficients.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.build_interaction_columns"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.build_regression_design-Tuple{MultipleSessionsInfo, RegressionParameter, Symbol}","page":"Parameter models","text":"build_regression_design(sessions_info, regression_parameter, parameter_name)\n\nReturn the design of the regression of the parameter parameter_name for the sessions in sessions_info, as HierarchicalRegressionParameters keeps it in its field designs: the prior and the values of each coefficient of the intercept, the predictors and the interactions, from build_coefficients, and the design of the varying effects of each column, from build_varying_effects_design. Used by prepare_parameters_model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.build_regression_design"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.build_term_columns-Tuple{MultipleSessionsInfo, Intercept, Symbol}","page":"Parameter models","text":"build_term_columns(sessions_info, term, parameter_name)\n\nReturn the coefficients of term, an Intercept or a predictor in the regression of the parameter parameter_name, for the sessions in sessions_info, as a NamedTuple with the name of each coefficient, names, and the values it is multiplied by in every session, columns: 1 for the intercept, the values of a continuous predictor, and the columns of the levels of a categorical predictor, from build_contrast_columns, with its reference_level. Used by build_coefficients.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.build_term_columns"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.build_varying_effects_design-Tuple{MultipleSessionsInfo, VaryingEffects, Symbol}","page":"Parameter models","text":"build_varying_effects_design(sessions_info, varying_effects, parameter_name)\n\nReturn the design of varying_effects, a VaryingEffects of the regression of the parameter parameter_name, for the sessions in sessions_info, as HierarchicalRegressionParameters describes it: the prior of the standard deviation of the deviations of each coefficient of their terms, with the prior under the symbol name of each group when the levels are grouped, the values that the deviations are multiplied by, from build_coefficients, the reference levels of their predictors, whether they are centered, and the levels of the sessions, from find_varying_effects_levels. The values of a coefficient other than the intercept must differ between the sessions of some level, or it is an error. Used by build_regression_design.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.build_varying_effects_design"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.check_coding-Tuple{Symbol, String}","page":"Parameter models","text":"check_coding(coding, description)\n\nThrow an informative error if coding, the coding of the levels of a categorical predictor given to description, such as \"a CategoricalPredictor\", isn't :dummy, :effects or :full_dummy. Used by the constructors of CategoricalPredictor and DefaultRegressionSettings.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_coding"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.check_has_columns-Tuple{Tuple{Vararg{Symbol}}, String}","page":"Parameter models","text":"check_has_columns(columns, description)\n\nThrow an informative error if columns, the tuple of columns given to a term, is empty. description names the kind of term, such as \"A CategoricalPredictor\". Used by the constructors of CategoricalPredictor and VaryingEffects.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_has_columns"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.check_has_no_prior_of_its_own-Tuple{Symbol}","page":"Parameter models","text":"check_has_no_prior_of_its_own(predictor)\n\nThrow an informative error if predictor, a predictor given to an Interaction itself, has a prior, since it has no coefficients of its own there. A predictor given by its name passes. Used by the constructor of Interaction.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_has_no_prior_of_its_own"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.check_has_prior-Tuple{Union{CategoricalPredictor, ContinuousPredictor, Interaction, Intercept}}","page":"Parameter models","text":"check_has_prior(term)\n\nThrow an informative error if term, a term of a RegressionParameter after it was given the default priors, has no prior, or if a term of VaryingEffects has none, naming the field of DefaultRegressionPriors that would give it one. Used by the constructor of RegressionParameter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_has_prior"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.check_interaction_terms-Tuple{Interaction, NamedTuple, String}","page":"Parameter models","text":"check_interaction_terms(interaction, terms_by_name, description)\n\nThrow an informative error if interaction, an Interaction, multiplies a predictor given by its name that isn't in terms_by_name, the terms given to description under their names, or that isn't a ContinuousPredictor or a CategoricalPredictor there. A predictor that the interaction was given itself needs no term. Used by collect_terms_by_name.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_interaction_terms"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.check_is_regression_parameter-Tuple{Symbol, RegressionParameter}","page":"Parameter models","text":"check_is_regression_parameter(name, regression_parameter)\n\nThrow an informative error if what a HierarchicalRegressionParameters was given for the parameter name isn't a RegressionParameter, or is one named after another parameter, as by the left-hand side of its formula. Used by the constructor of HierarchicalRegressionParameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_is_regression_parameter"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.check_no_varying_effects_named-Tuple{NamedTuple, Symbol, String}","page":"Parameter models","text":"check_no_varying_effects_named(terms_by_name, reserved_name, named_so)\n\nThrow an informative error if terms_by_name, the terms of a RegressionParameter with a family under their names, has VaryingEffects named reserved_name, which is the name of the variable that named_so describes, such as \"the values of the sessions\". Used by the constructor of RegressionParameter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_no_varying_effects_named"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.check_unique_coefficient_names-Tuple{Any, String}","page":"Parameter models","text":"check_unique_coefficient_names(coefficient_names, description)\n\nThrow an informative error if two coefficients in coefficient_names, the names of the coefficients of description, such as \"the regression of learning_rate\", are the same. Used by build_coefficients.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_unique_coefficient_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.check_unique_term_names-Tuple{Any, String}","page":"Parameter models","text":"check_unique_term_names(term_names, description)\n\nThrow an informative error if two terms in term_names, the names of the terms given to description, such as \"a RegressionParameter\", are the same. Used by collect_terms_by_name.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_unique_term_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.check_varies_within_levels-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractVector, Symbol, String}","page":"Parameter models","text":"check_varies_within_levels(column, level_of_each_session, coefficient_name, description)\n\nThrow an informative error if the values in column, which the deviations of the varying slope coefficient_name of description, such as \"the varying effects participant of learning_rate\", are multiplied by, are the same in all the sessions of each level, where level_of_each_session has the level of every session. Used by build_varying_effects_design.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_varies_within_levels"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.collect_terms_by_name-Tuple{Tuple, String}","page":"Parameter models","text":"collect_terms_by_name(terms, description)\n\nReturn terms, the terms given to a RegressionParameter or to VaryingEffects, as a NamedTuple with each term under its name, after checking that each is an AbstractRegressionTerm, that their names differ, that there is one Intercept at most, and that each Interaction multiplies predictors among them, or ones it was given themselves. description says what was given the terms, such as \"a RegressionParameter\", for the errors. Used by the constructors of RegressionParameter and VaryingEffects.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.collect_terms_by_name"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.columns_of_interaction_predictor-Tuple{MultipleSessionsInfo, Symbol, AbstractDict, Symbol}","page":"Parameter models","text":"columns_of_interaction_predictor(sessions_info, predictor, columns_of_predictors,\n                                 parameter_name)\n\nReturn the coefficients of predictor, a predictor of an Interaction, as build_term_columns returns them: those in columns_of_predictors under its name for a predictor given by its name, which is a term of its own, and those that build_term_columns builds for a predictor that the interaction was given itself. Used by build_interaction_columns.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.columns_of_interaction_predictor"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.default_prior_field-Tuple{Intercept}","page":"Parameter models","text":"default_prior_field(term)\n\nReturn the name of the field of a DefaultRegressionPriors with the default prior of term, a term of a RegressionParameter other than VaryingEffects, such as :continuous_predictor for a ContinuousPredictor. Used by with_default_prior and check_has_prior.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.default_prior_field"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.describe_columns-Tuple{Tuple{Symbol}}","page":"Parameter models","text":"describe_columns(columns)\n\nReturn how columns, a tuple of names, is named in an error message: as the column age for one, and as the columns (:diagnosis, :site) for several. Used by the errors of build_contrast_columns.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.describe_columns"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.find_groups_of_levels-Tuple{MultipleSessionsInfo, Tuple{}, Any, Any, String}","page":"Parameter models","text":"find_groups_of_levels(sessions_info, group_by, level_of_each_session,\n                      level_symbol_names, description)\n\nReturn the groups of the levels of varying effects by the values of the columns in group_by, a tuple of names, so that each group has a standard deviation of its own: a NamedTuple with the group of every level, group_of_each_level, the number of groups, n_groups, and the symbol name of each group, group_symbol_names, as group_sessions gives them for the sessions. Without columns it returns nothing, for one standard deviation. A level whose sessions are in more than one group is an error. description says what is grouped, for the errors, such as \"the varying effects participant of learning_rate\". Used by find_varying_effects_levels.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.find_groups_of_levels"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.find_varying_effects_levels-Tuple{MultipleSessionsInfo, VaryingEffects, Symbol}","page":"Parameter models","text":"find_varying_effects_levels(sessions_info, varying_effects, parameter_name)\n\nReturn the levels of varying_effects, a VaryingEffects of the regression of the parameter parameter_name, as a NamedTuple with the level of every session, level_of_each_session, the number of levels, n_levels, the symbol name of each level, level_symbol_names, and groups, from find_groups_of_levels. The levels are the values of a single column, or the combinations of the values of several, numbered in the order of their first session. Used by build_varying_effects_design.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.find_varying_effects_levels"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.merge_family_arguments-Tuple{Tuple{}, Any, Tuple{}}","page":"Parameter models","text":"merge_family_arguments(distribution_parameters, regression_outcome, sampled_values)\n\nReturn the arguments of the distribution of a RegressionFamily, as a tuple in the order of distribution_parameters, a tuple of its parameters: regression_outcome in the place of RegressionOutcome, and the next of sampled_values, a tuple, in the place of each prior. Used by build_family_distribution.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.merge_family_arguments"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.name_of_columns-Tuple{Tuple{Symbol}}","page":"Parameter models","text":"name_of_columns(columns)\n\nReturn the name of a term that reads columns, a tuple of names: the name of a single column, or the names of several joined by two underscores, as the parts of the symbol name of a session are, such as id__block. Used by the constructors of CategoricalPredictor and VaryingEffects.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.name_of_columns"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.name_of_predictor-Tuple{Symbol}","page":"Parameter models","text":"name_of_predictor(predictor)\n\nReturn the name of predictor, a predictor given to an Interaction: the name itself for one given by its name, and the name of a ContinuousPredictor or a CategoricalPredictor that is given itself. Used by the constructor of Interaction.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.name_of_predictor"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.priors_of_groups-Tuple{Any, Nothing}","page":"Parameter models","text":"priors_of_groups(prior, groups)\n\nReturn the prior of a parameter of a group: prior itself without groups, where groups is nothing, and otherwise a NamedTuple with prior under the symbol name of each group in groups, as group_sessions and find_groups_of_levels give them. Used by build_varying_effects_design, for the standard deviations of varying effects, and by prepare_parameters_model for the group-level parameters of a HierarchicalGroupedParameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.priors_of_groups"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.read_predictor_values-Tuple{MultipleSessionsInfo, Symbol, Symbol}","page":"Parameter models","text":"read_predictor_values(sessions_info, column, parameter_name)\n\nReturn the value of the column column of a continuous predictor in every session, as a vector of Float64, for the regression of the parameter parameter_name. A value that isn't a number is an error. Used by build_term_columns.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.read_predictor_values"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.select_terms-Tuple{Any, NamedTuple}","page":"Parameter models","text":"select_terms(predicate, terms)\n\nReturn the terms in terms, a NamedTuple with each term under its name, for which predicate is true, in a NamedTuple of the same form. Used by build_regression_design.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.select_terms"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.varying_default_prior_field-Tuple{Intercept}","page":"Parameter models","text":"varying_default_prior_field(term)\n\nReturn the name of the field of a DefaultRegressionPriors with the default prior of the standard deviation of term, a term of VaryingEffects: :varying_intercept for an Intercept, and :varying_slope for a predictor or an Interaction. Used by with_default_prior and check_has_prior.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.varying_default_prior_field"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.with_default_prior-Tuple{Any, DefaultRegressionPriors}","page":"Parameter models","text":"with_default_prior(term, default_priors)\n\nReturn term, a term given to a RegressionParameter, with the prior of its kind in default_priors, a DefaultRegressionPriors, when it has no prior, and VaryingEffects with the prior of the standard deviation of each of their terms that has none. Terms with a prior are returned as they are, and so is anything that isn't a term, for the constructor to report. Used by the constructor of RegressionParameter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.with_default_prior"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.with_default_settings-Tuple{Any, DefaultRegressionSettings}","page":"Parameter models","text":"with_default_settings(term, default_settings)\n\nReturn term, a term given to a RegressionParameter, with the settings in default_settings, a DefaultRegressionSettings, that it was given without: the coding of a CategoricalPredictor, also of one that an Interaction was given itself, and for VaryingEffects whether they are centered and the coding of their categorical predictors. A term's own settings are kept, and anything else is returned as it is. A reference level is an error when the default coding is :full_dummy, which has none. Used by the constructor of RegressionParameter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.with_default_settings"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.with_prior-Tuple{Intercept, Any}","page":"Parameter models","text":"with_prior(term, prior)\n\nReturn term with prior as its prior, and its other settings as they are. Used by with_default_prior.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.with_prior"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.add_part_without_name-Tuple{CompositeParameters, Any}","page":"Parameter models","text":"add_part_without_name(parameters_model, part)\n\nReturn parameters_model with part added to it as a part without a name: a CompositeParameters with its own parts, which keep their names, and then part, or a new CompositeParameters of parameters_model and part for any other parameter model. Used by with_fixed_parameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.add_part_without_name"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.as_parameters_model-Tuple{Any}","page":"Parameter models","text":"as_parameters_model(parameters_model)\n\nReturn parameters_model as one parameter model: a tuple of parameter models as a CompositeParameters of them, and a NamedTuple of them as a CompositeParameters with each part under its name, which its variables are then named under. Anything else is returned as it is, for prepare_full_model to check. A tuple or a NamedTuple with something other than a parameter model in it is an error. Used by prepare_full_model and by the functions of the inference backends that make a full model, such as turing_full_model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.as_parameters_model"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.check_parts_give_different_parameters-Tuple{Tuple}","page":"Parameter models","text":"check_parts_give_different_parameters(parts)\n\nThrow an informative error if two of parts, the parts of a CompositeParameters, give the same parameter, for the parts whose parameter_names are known. Used by the constructor of CompositeParameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_parts_give_different_parameters"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.merge_parameters_of_parts-Tuple{NamedTuple, NamedTuple}","page":"Parameter models","text":"merge_parameters_of_parts(parameters, other_parameters)\n\nReturn the parameters that two parts of a CompositeParameters give, each a NamedTuple with one vector for each parameter, merged into one, after checking that they give different parameters, which matters for the parts whose names weren't known when the model was made. Used by the models of CompositeParameters in the inference backends.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.merge_parameters_of_parts"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/parameter_models/#CognitiveModeling.part_names-Union{Tuple{CompositeParameters{Tparts, Tpart_names}}, Tuple{Tpart_names}, Tuple{Tparts}} where {Tparts, Tpart_names}","page":"Parameter models","text":"part_names(parameters_model)\n\nReturn the name of each part of parameters_model, a CompositeParameters, in a tuple in the order of its parts, with nothing for a part given without a name. Used by the models of CompositeParameters in the inference backends, which name the variables of a part under its name, and by prepare_parameters_model and add_part_without_name, which keep the names.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.part_names"},{"category":"section","location":"planned/#Planned-tutorials-and-interfaces","page":"Planned tutorials and interfaces","text":"This page lists what is planned and not written yet. The list will grow.","title":"Planned tutorials and interfaces"},{"category":"section","location":"planned/#Cognitive-models-for-tutorials","page":"Planned tutorials and interfaces","text":"Models of metacognition, such as HMeta-d.\nThe models of hBayesDM, probably every one of them.\nThe main models for the Iowa Gambling Task, such as PVL-Delta and ORL.\nDifferent variants of the Rescorla-Wagner model, and models like it.\nEvidence accumulation models, such as attention-based models.\nBayesian models made with RxInfer.jl.\nActive inference models, which a separate package is being made for.\nAn example of a cognitive model which uses Turing.jl to select its actions.\nModels with discrete parameters, to show how they are fitted. The page Discrete parameters is a sketch of it.\nExamples with models from control theory as cognitive models.","title":"Cognitive models for tutorials"},{"category":"section","location":"planned/#Interfaces","page":"Planned tutorials and interfaces","text":"BayesianRegressionModels.jl, which could also give regressions on a parameter with unordered categories, with a regression for each category but one.\nSimulation-based inference, where a neural network is trained on simulations to estimate the parameters: NeuralEstimators.jl, to test or to make an interface with, and perhaps the Python package sbi too. A simulation of a cognitive model is what both need.\nStanBlocks.jl, potentially, as another way to write the probabilistic model around a session.\nGlobalSensitivity.jl, for Sobol sensitivity analyses of cognitive models: how much each parameter, alone and together with the others, changes what a model does.\nCognitive models in continuous time, probably with DifferentialEquations.jl, whose states change between the timesteps as well as on them.","title":"Interfaces"},{"category":"section","location":"planned/#Defining-models","page":"Planned tutorials and interfaces","text":"A macro that defines a cognitive model or an environment in one command, and writes the methods of the functions to extend where it can deduce them, such as attribute_names from the names the model uses.\nWhether a trial needs to be distinguished from a timestep, and whether a model can have nested time scales, such as evidence that accumulates over several timesteps within each trial and learning between the trials.","title":"Defining models"},{"category":"section","location":"planned/#Fitting","page":"Planned tutorials and interfaces","text":"Dyadic interaction, with another agent as the environment of the agent that is fitted. When the other agent's actions are recorded, each agent is a session of its own whose observations are the other's actions. When they aren't, the other agent is the environment that fills them in.\nSession identifiers in the names of the variables of a fitted session, instead of session[i], if a way is found that doesn't compile the session once per identifier.\nA convenient way to fit a single session, with the full model or a variant of it.\nChoosing a sampler for each parameter from whether it is discrete or continuous.\nSumming out a discrete parameter with few values instead of sampling it, so that a sampler which uses gradients can fit the whole model, perhaps with one of the Julia packages for marginalizing discrete variables.\nMixture models and model selection within the fitted model, in the style of Michael D. Lee: a latent variable for each session, or each group, chooses which of several cognitive models generated its behavior, and its posterior compares the models.\nSeveral sets of experimental data in one hierarchical model, such as different experiments or tasks done by the same participants, which share the parameters they have in common.","title":"Fitting"},{"category":"section","location":"planned/#Performance","page":"Planned tutorials and interfaces","text":"Benchmarks which compare the speed of simulating and fitting with other inference backends and packages, such as Stan.\nExtended benchmarks with full data sets, beside the small ones in the benchmark folder, which run on every push.","title":"Performance"},{"category":"section","location":"planned/#Plots","page":"Planned tutorials and interfaces","text":"A plot of the parameters of every fitted session, with the averages of their groups.\nA plot of the trajectories of different fitted sessions over the timesteps.\nPlots of a simulation, a history or a fitted session over time. The entry before the first timestep is timestep 0, at time 0, and its size is BeforeFirstTimestep(). The time of a later timestep is the sum of the sizes of the timesteps up to it.","title":"Plots"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/compiling_with_reactant/#Compiling-simulations-with-Reactant.jl","page":"Compiling simulations with Reactant.jl","text":"Placeholder tutorial. It will show how to compile a simulation with Reactant.jl, and how to take its gradient with Enzyme.jl. Until then, this is what to know before trying.\n\nShort simulations can be compiled with Reactant.jl, if the action distributions work with traced numbers, which those from Distributions.jl don't. Compile a function that takes a Reactant.ReactantRNG() as a positional argument and passes it on as rng, because @jit doesn't trace keyword arguments, and the default generator repeats its numbers on every run. Compile times grow quickly with n_steps, so this is for a few hundred timesteps at most.\n\nThe page Automatic differentiation and Reactant lists what is tested, and the pitfalls a model can run into.\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Compiling simulations with Reactant.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"api/defining_models/#Defining-models","page":"Defining models","text":"These are what a cognitive model or an environment model is written with, since the two are defined in the same way.\n\nPages = [\"defining_models.md\"]","title":"Defining models"},{"category":"section","location":"api/defining_models/#Types","page":"Defining models","text":"","title":"Types"},{"category":"section","location":"api/defining_models/#Functions-that-a-model-must-implement","page":"Defining models","text":"These and the functions that a model can implement are public, but not exported, so use them with the package's name, as in CognitiveModeling.run_timestep.","title":"Functions that a model must implement"},{"category":"section","location":"api/defining_models/#Functions-that-a-model-can-implement","page":"Defining models","text":"","title":"Functions that a model can implement"},{"category":"section","location":"api/defining_models/#Reading-a-model's-parameters","page":"Defining models","text":"","title":"Reading a model's parameters"},{"category":"section","location":"api/defining_models/#Environments-from-other-packages","page":"Defining models","text":"","title":"Environments from other packages"},{"category":"type","location":"api/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.AbstractCognitiveModel","page":"Defining models","text":"AbstractCognitiveModel\n\nSupertype for cognitive models, whose inputs are observations and whose outputs are actions.\n\nIn the REPL, a cognitive model shows its type, the values of its fields if it has any, and the names of its attributes, or not specified if it has no method of attribute_names. A kind of attribute with more than five names shows only how many there are, and attribute_names gives them. On one line, as inside a vector, a model shows its type and its kind.\n\nImplementation\n\nCreate a subtype and add methods of run_timestep, initialize_state and attribute_names for it. A model can also add methods of prepare_state_for_saving, default_parameters and stopping_criterion. Fitting with a PPL can need methods of its own, which the documentation for that PPL describes.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> struct ExampleModel <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\njulia> function CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::ExampleModel)\n           return ModelAttributes(;\n               parameters = (:learning_rate, :noise),\n               states = :expected_reward,\n               inputs = :reward,\n               outputs = (),\n           )\n       end;\n\njulia> ExampleModel()\n-- Cognitive model --\nType: ExampleModel\nParameters: learning_rate, noise\nStates: expected_reward\nInputs: reward\nOutputs: none\n\njulia> print(ExampleModel())\nExampleModel (cognitive model)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.AbstractCognitiveModel"},{"category":"type","location":"api/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.AbstractEnvironmentModel","page":"Defining models","text":"AbstractEnvironmentModel\n\nSupertype for environment models, whose inputs are the actions of a cognitive model and whose outputs are its observations.\n\nAn environment model shows in the REPL and on one line as a cognitive model does, with its own kind.\n\nImplementation\n\nCreate a subtype and add the same methods as for an AbstractCognitiveModel: run_timestep, initialize_state and attribute_names, and optionally prepare_state_for_saving, default_parameters and stopping_criterion. Fitting with a PPL can need methods of its own, which the documentation for that PPL describes.\n\nSee also simulate, which has an example.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> struct ExampleEnvironment <: AbstractEnvironmentModel end\n\njulia> function CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(environment::ExampleEnvironment)\n           return ModelAttributes(;\n               parameters = :reward_probability,\n               states = (),\n               inputs = :choice,\n               outputs = :reward,\n           )\n       end;\n\njulia> ExampleEnvironment()\n-- Environment model --\nType: ExampleEnvironment\nParameters: reward_probability\nStates: none\nInputs: choice\nOutputs: reward\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.AbstractEnvironmentModel"},{"category":"type","location":"api/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.AbstractNoValue","page":"Defining models","text":"AbstractNoValue\n\nSupertype for what stands in for an observation or an action that isn't there: NoValue and BeforeFirstTimestep, and in the data of a fitted session UnimputedMissingValue. A model which treats them all the same can check for it, as in context.input.reward isa AbstractNoValue.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.AbstractNoValue"},{"category":"type","location":"api/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.AgentStarts","page":"Defining models","text":"AgentStarts()\n\nMake the agent move first on each timestep of a simulation or a fitted session.\n\nOn a timestep, the cognitive model updates and acts on the previous observation, and the environment then responds to its action. On the first timestep, the cognitive model receives an observation with every entry BeforeFirstTimestep. Both models receive it as the who_starts of their ModelContext, so that a cognitive model can act before it updates when that is what it means.\n\nSee also EnvironmentStarts, UnknownWhoStarts, simulate and SessionInfo.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.AgentStarts"},{"category":"type","location":"api/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.BeforeFirstTimestep","page":"Defining models","text":"BeforeFirstTimestep()\n\nStand-in for an observation or an action from before the first timestep, when there is none yet, as one entry of a NamedTuple, such as (; choice = BeforeFirstTimestep()).\n\nA model receives it for every previous output on its first timestep, and for every input too when the other side hasn't moved yet: a cognitive model when the agent starts, and an environment model when the environment starts. NoValue is different: it says that nothing was observed or done on a timestep that did run.\n\nBefore the first timestep is timestep 0, which a simulation, a history and a fitted session start with when their SaveHistory saves the initial timestep values, as it does by default. Its observation and action are this marker unless others are given, and so is its size.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.BeforeFirstTimestep"},{"category":"type","location":"api/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.Deterministic","page":"Defining models","text":"Deterministic(value)\n\nAn action or an observation that is always value, for a cognitive model or an environment model which gives it without any randomness, as in (; reward = Deterministic(1.0)).\n\nA simulation samples every action and every observation with rand, and sampling from a Deterministic gives value itself. The value is kept in the field value.\n\nA simulation throws an error for a number, a string or a symbol that isn't wrapped, since none of them is something to sample from. A vector, a tuple or a range that isn't wrapped is sampled from as it is: rand picks one of its elements, with no error.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using Random: Xoshiro\n\njulia> rand(Xoshiro(1), Deterministic(1.0))\n1.0\n\njulia> ModelOutput((; choice = Deterministic(true))).output_distribution.choice.value\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.Deterministic"},{"category":"type","location":"api/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.EnvironmentStarts","page":"Defining models","text":"EnvironmentStarts()\n\nMake the environment move first on each timestep of a simulation or a fitted session.\n\nOn a timestep, the environment gives an observation in response to the previous action, and the cognitive model then updates and acts on it. On the first timestep, the environment receives an action with every entry BeforeFirstTimestep. Both models receive it as the who_starts of their ModelContext.\n\nSee also AgentStarts, UnknownWhoStarts, simulate and SessionInfo.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.EnvironmentStarts"},{"category":"type","location":"api/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.InvalidParametersError","page":"Defining models","text":"InvalidParametersError(message)\nInvalidParametersError()\n\nAn error for a model to throw from run_timestep or initialize_state when its parameters aren't valid, as in throw(InvalidParametersError()).\n\nThe default message says that the parameters are not valid, possibly dependent on the observations, actions and states, and a model can give a more specific one. A simulation stops with it as with any other error, and fitting can catch it to give the parameters a probability of zero, so a model should throw this rather than an ordinary error when the parameters are the problem.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> try\n           throw(InvalidParametersError())\n       catch error\n           sprint(showerror, error)\n       end\n\"InvalidParametersError: Model parameters are not valid, possibly dependent on observations, actions and states.\"\n\njulia> sprint(showerror, InvalidParametersError(\"The learning rate must be between 0 and 1.\"))\n\"InvalidParametersError: The learning rate must be between 0 and 1.\"\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.InvalidParametersError"},{"category":"type","location":"api/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.ModelAttributes","page":"Defining models","text":"ModelAttributes(; parameters, states, inputs, outputs)\n\nThe names of the attributes of a model, which attribute_names returns.\n\nFor a cognitive model the inputs are its observations and the outputs its actions, and for an environment model the inputs are the actions it receives and the outputs the observations it gives.\n\nEach argument is a Tuple of Symbols, read as a property of the same name, such as attributes.parameters. A single Symbol is put in a tuple, and a Vector of them is made into one. All four must be given, and () means that the model has none of that kind. A name can only be used once across the four kinds, so that it says which attribute of the model is meant wherever the attributes are named together, and an ArgumentError is thrown otherwise.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> attributes = ModelAttributes(;\n           parameters = (:learning_rate, :inverse_temperature),\n           states = :expected_reward,\n           inputs = [:reward],\n           outputs = (),\n       )\nModelAttributes\nParameters: learning_rate, inverse_temperature\nStates: expected_reward\nInputs: reward\nOutputs: none\n\njulia> attributes.states\n(:expected_reward,)\n\njulia> ModelAttributes(; parameters = :choice, states = (), inputs = (), outputs = :choice)\nERROR: ArgumentError: The name choice is used for both a parameter and an output, but a model can only use a name for one attribute.\n[...]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.ModelAttributes"},{"category":"type","location":"api/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.ModelContext","page":"Defining models","text":"ModelContext(; input, previous_state, previous_output, parameters, timestep_size=1,\n             rng=default_rng(), who_starts=UnknownWhoStarts())\nModelContext(input, previous_state, previous_output, parameters, timestep_size=1,\n             rng=default_rng(), who_starts=UnknownWhoStarts())\n\nEverything a model needs to run a single timestep, which run_timestep receives. The arguments can be given by name, which says what each one is, or in this order.\n\nEach argument is kept in a field of the same name, such as context.input. For a cognitive model the input is its observation and the output its action, and for an environment model the input is the action of the cognitive model and the output the observation it gives.\n\nArguments\n\ninput: what the model receives on this timestep, in a NamedTuple with the names the other side gave the entries, as in context.input.reward. An entry that isn't there on this timestep is NoValue. On the first timestep, when the other side hasn't moved yet, every entry is BeforeFirstTimestep.\nprevious_state: the state of the model after the previous timestep. On the first timestep, this is the output of initialize_state.\nprevious_output: what the model gave on the previous timestep, in a NamedTuple with the names the model gave the entries, as in context.previous_output.choice. On the first timestep, each of them is BeforeFirstTimestep.\nparameters: the parameters of the model, in a NamedTuple.\ntimestep_size=1: how much time this timestep covers; the difference between the end of the previous and the end of the current timestep.\nrng=default_rng(): the random number generator to sample from if anything in the model is random, so that a simulation can be reproduced.\nwho_starts=UnknownWhoStarts(): who moves first on the timestep, AgentStarts or EnvironmentStarts as the simulation or the fitted session was told, or UnknownWhoStarts when neither is known. A model which can update before it acts or act before it updates can choose by it: for a cognitive model, with EnvironmentStarts() the input is this timestep's observation, and with AgentStarts() the previous timestep's.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> input = (; reward = 1.0);\n\njulia> previous_state = (; expected_reward = 0.5);\n\njulia> previous_output = (; choice = BeforeFirstTimestep());\n\njulia> parameters = (; learning_rate = 0.1);\n\njulia> ModelContext(; input, previous_state, previous_output, parameters).timestep_size\n1\n\njulia> ModelContext(; input, previous_state, previous_output, parameters, timestep_size = 0.5).timestep_size\n0.5\n\njulia> ModelContext(input, previous_state, previous_output, parameters).who_starts\nUnknownWhoStarts()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.ModelContext"},{"category":"type","location":"api/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.ModelOutput","page":"Defining models","text":"ModelOutput(output_distribution, new_state=(;))\n\nThe outputs of a model for a single timestep, which run_timestep returns.\n\nEach argument is kept in a field of the same name, such as output.new_state.\n\nArguments\n\noutput_distribution::NamedTuple: what the model's outputs on this timestep are sampled from, in a NamedTuple with one entry for each output, such as (; choice = Bernoulli(0.7)) for the action of a cognitive model, or (; reward = Bernoulli(0.8)) for the observation an environment model gives. Each entry can be anything that works with rand, such as a distribution from Distributions.jl, or a Deterministic for an output that isn't random, and the sampled outputs keep the names. An output that isn't given on this timestep is NoValue, and a model without outputs gives an empty NamedTuple, (;).\nnew_state::NamedTuple=(;): the state of the model after this timestep, which is the previous_state of the next one, with one entry for each state in the model's attributes, also when the state is a single object, as in (; rl_env). A model without a state gives an empty NamedTuple, (;). The outputs are sampled after the state is made, so it can't depend on them: the model receives them as context.previous_output on the next timestep.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using Distributions: Bernoulli\n\njulia> ModelOutput((; choice = Bernoulli(0.7)), (; expected_reward = 0.5)).new_state\n(expected_reward = 0.5,)\n\njulia> ModelOutput((; choice = Bernoulli(0.7))).new_state\nNamedTuple()\n\njulia> ModelOutput((; choice = NoValue(), bet = Bernoulli(0.2))).output_distribution.choice\nNoValue()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.ModelOutput"},{"category":"type","location":"api/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.NoValue","page":"Defining models","text":"NoValue()\n\nStand-in for an observation that isn't made, or an action that isn't taken, on a timestep, as one entry of the observations or the actions NamedTuple, such as (; reward = NoValue()) or (; choice = NoValue()). Whether it is an observation or an action is given by where it is. Before the first timestep, an observation or an action is BeforeFirstTimestep instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.NoValue"},{"category":"type","location":"api/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.ParameterDomain","page":"Defining models","text":"ParameterDomain(; type=Real, lower=-Inf, upper=Inf)\n\nThe values that a parameter of a cognitive model or an environment model can take, which the model gives for it in parameter_domains: values of type, between lower and upper when they are real numbers.\n\nKeyword arguments\n\ntype=Real: the type of the parameter's values. An abstract type takes every type of its kind, so with Real a value can be a Float32, a Float64 or a number of an automatic differentiation package alike, and no value is converted. A type of whole numbers, such as Integer, takes every whole number, also in another number type, such as 3.0, and Bool takes true, false, 0 and 1. A type that isn't a type of numbers, such as AbstractString or Symbol, is for a parameter such as the name of a strategy. A type of floating-point numbers, such as Float64, is an error: give Real, which takes them all.\nlower=-Inf and upper=Inf: the smallest and the largest value of a parameter that is a real number, which are both in the domain. A parameter of another type has no bounds.\n\nA parameter that is an array has every element in the domain, so type is the type of an element. A parameter is discrete when its type is a type of whole numbers, or isn't a type of real numbers, and continuous otherwise, as CognitiveModeling.discrete_parameter_names and CognitiveModeling.continuous_parameter_names read it. The arguments are kept as domain.type, domain.lower and domain.upper.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> learning_rate = ParameterDomain(; lower = 0, upper = 1)\nParameterDomain(; lower = 0, upper = 1)\n\njulia> learning_rate.type, learning_rate.lower, learning_rate.upper\n(Real, 0, 1)\n\njulia> ParameterDomain(; type = Integer, lower = 1)\nParameterDomain(; type = Integer, lower = 1)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.ParameterDomain"},{"category":"type","location":"api/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.ParameterDomainError","page":"Defining models","text":"ParameterDomainError(message)\n\nThe error that is thrown when a parameter of a cognitive model or an environment model has a value outside its domain in parameter_domains: of another type than the domain's, or below its lower bound or above its upper bound.\n\nA simulation and a fit stop with it. A fit which catches an InvalidParametersError, to give the parameters of a sample a probability of zero, doesn't catch this one. Give the parameters priors or transformations that stay within the domains instead, or leave the check out with check_parameter_domains = false in simulate, InstantiatedModel or SessionInfo.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> sprint(showerror, ParameterDomainError(\"The parameter learning_rate is 1.5, which is outside its domain, from 0 to 1.\"))\n\"ParameterDomainError: The parameter learning_rate is 1.5, which is outside its domain, from 0 to 1.\"\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.ParameterDomainError"},{"category":"type","location":"api/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.UnknownWhoStarts","page":"Defining models","text":"UnknownWhoStarts()\n\nThe who_starts of a ModelContext when neither side is known to move first on a timestep, as when a model runs on a single input with simulate, or is used outside a simulation. AgentStarts and EnvironmentStarts are the other two, which a simulation or a fitted session passes on from its own who_starts.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.UnknownWhoStarts"},{"category":"function","location":"api/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.run_timestep","page":"Defining models","text":"run_timestep(model::AbstractTimestepModel, context::ModelContext)::ModelOutput\n\nRun a single timestep of model: update its state, and return what its outputs are sampled from. For a cognitive model the outputs are its actions, and for an environment model the observations it gives.\n\nImplementation\n\nEvery model must add a method of this function, which throws an error otherwise. A simulation samples the outputs itself, from the output_distribution of the ModelOutput that the method returns, so an output that isn't random is wrapped in a Deterministic. In simulations, anything else that is random, such as a change of the state, should be sampled from context.rng. If states are mutated in place, a prepare_state_for_saving method must be added. For use within PPLs, this function can also have special implementations, and there may be special constraints: see the documentation for the specific PPL for details.\n\nExamples\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\nusing Distributions: BernoulliLogit\n\nstruct RescorlaWagner <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.run_timestep(model::RescorlaWagner, context::ModelContext)\n\n    ## Unpack the context ##\n    #Reward observed on this timestep\n    r = context.input.reward\n    #Expected reward from the previous state\n    V₋₁ = context.previous_state.expected_reward\n    #Learning rate parameter\n    α = context.parameters.learning_rate\n    #Inverse temperature parameter\n    β = context.parameters.inverse_temperature\n\n    ## Update state ##\n    #Update expected reward using the Rescorla-Wagner rule\n    Vₜ = V₋₁ + α * (r - V₋₁)\n\n    ## Compute action probabilities ##\n    #Softmax function, instantiated as a Bernoulli logit distribution\n    Pₜ = BernoulliLogit(β * (Vₜ - 0.5))\n\n    return ModelOutput((; choice = Pₜ), (; expected_reward = Vₜ))\nend\n\nparameters = (; learning_rate = 0.1, inverse_temperature = 2.0)\ncontext = ModelContext(; input = (; reward = 1.0), previous_state = (; expected_reward = 0.5), previous_output = (; choice = NoValue()), parameters)\n\nCognitiveModeling.run_timestep(RescorlaWagner(), context).new_state\n\n# output\n\n(expected_reward = 0.55,)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.run_timestep"},{"category":"function","location":"api/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.initialize_state","page":"Defining models","text":"initialize_state(model::AbstractTimestepModel, parameters)\n\nCreate the initial state that model has before its first timestep, which can depend on parameters.\n\nImplementation\n\nEvery model must add a method of this function, which throws an error otherwise. It returns a NamedTuple with one entry for each state in the model's attributes, also when the state is a single object, as in (; rl_env), and a model without a state returns an empty NamedTuple, (;). For use within PPLs, this function can also have special implementations: see the documentation for the specific PPL for details.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> struct RescorlaWagner <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\njulia> function CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(model::RescorlaWagner, parameters)\n           return (; expected_reward = parameters.initial_value)\n       end;\n\njulia> CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(RescorlaWagner(), (; initial_value = 0.5))\n(expected_reward = 0.5,)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.initialize_state"},{"category":"function","location":"api/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.attribute_names","page":"Defining models","text":"attribute_names(model::AbstractTimestepModel)::ModelAttributes\n\nReturn the names of the parameters, states, inputs and outputs of model, in a ModelAttributes.\n\nThe names define the model, so they must match what the model uses. The parameters are the names that run_timestep and initialize_state read from the parameters. The states are the names in the state that initialize_state returns and in new_state, which run_timestep reads from context.previous_state. The inputs are the names that run_timestep reads from context.input, and the outputs are the names in output_distribution, which are also those in context.previous_output. For a cognitive model the inputs are its observations and the outputs its actions, and for an environment model the inputs are the actions it receives and the outputs the observations it gives. Nothing checks that they match while the model runs.\n\nImplementation\n\nEvery model must add a method of this function, which throws an error otherwise. A model without one kind of attribute gives () for it. simulate and InstantiatedModel use the output names for the output before the first timestep, and check the parameters, the previous output and the state against the names. A simulation with an environment also checks that the two models fit together: the outputs of each must be the inputs of the other, unless a NameMap says which of them are passed on, and under which names.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> struct RescorlaWagner <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\njulia> function CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::RescorlaWagner)\n           return ModelAttributes(;\n               parameters = (:learning_rate, :inverse_temperature),\n               states = :expected_reward,\n               inputs = :reward,\n               outputs = :choice,\n           )\n       end;\n\njulia> CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(RescorlaWagner()).parameters\n(:learning_rate, :inverse_temperature)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.attribute_names"},{"category":"function","location":"api/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.prepare_state_for_saving","page":"Defining models","text":"prepare_state_for_saving(model::AbstractTimestepModel, state)\n\nReturn what a simulation saves of state, the state of a cognitive model or of an environment model, as it is about to save it. The state that the next timestep starts from isn't affected.\n\nImplementation\n\nA model can add a method of this function. The default returns state as it is, so nothing is copied. One which changes an object in its state in place must add a method which copies it, or all the saved states show the final values, with no error. The method must return the state with the names in the model's attributes, since a SaveHistory selects states by those names. Nothing checks this while the model runs.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> struct LearnsInPlace <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\njulia> function CognitiveModeling.prepare_state_for_saving(model::LearnsInPlace, state)\n           return (; values = copy(state.values))\n       end;\n\njulia> state = (; values = [0.5, 0.5]);\n\njulia> CognitiveModeling.prepare_state_for_saving(LearnsInPlace(), state).values === state.values\nfalse\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.prepare_state_for_saving"},{"category":"function","location":"api/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.default_parameters","page":"Defining models","text":"default_parameters(model::AbstractTimestepModel)\n\nReturn the default parameter values of a cognitive model or of an environment model, such as (; learning_rate = 0.1), which InstantiatedModel and simulate use for the parameters they aren't given. The display of a model shows each default value after the name of its parameter.\n\nImplementation\n\nA model can add a method of this function. The default is an empty NamedTuple, (;), so there are no default values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.default_parameters"},{"category":"function","location":"api/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.parameter_domains","page":"Defining models","text":"parameter_domains(model::AbstractTimestepModel)\n\nReturn the values that the parameters of model, a cognitive model or an environment model, can take: a NamedTuple with a ParameterDomain under the name of each parameter that has one. A parameter without a domain can take any value.\n\nThe parameters of a simulation and of a fitted session are checked against their domains before anything runs, and a value outside its domain throws a ParameterDomainError, unless simulate, InstantiatedModel or SessionInfo is given check_parameter_domains = false. CognitiveModeling.discrete_parameter_names gives the discrete parameters, and CognitiveModeling.continuous_parameter_names the continuous ones, which are sampled in different ways. The display of a model shows each domain after the name of its parameter.\n\nImplementation\n\nA model can add a method of this function. The default is an empty NamedTuple, (;), so that every parameter can take any value. A domain is best kept to the values the model can't run without, since a value outside it stops a fit.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> struct CountsOptions <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\njulia> function CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::CountsOptions)\n           return ModelAttributes(; parameters = (:learning_rate, :n_options, :bias), states = (), inputs = (), outputs = ())\n       end;\n\njulia> function CognitiveModeling.parameter_domains(model::CountsOptions)\n           return (;\n               learning_rate = ParameterDomain(; lower = 0, upper = 1),\n               n_options = ParameterDomain(; type = Integer, lower = 1),\n           )\n       end;\n\njulia> CognitiveModeling.discrete_parameter_names(CountsOptions())\n(:n_options,)\n\njulia> CountsOptions()\n-- Cognitive model --\nType: CountsOptions\nParameters:\n  learning_rate ∈ [0, 1]\n  n_options ∈ {1, 2, …}\n  bias\nStates: none\nInputs: none\nOutputs: none\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.parameter_domains"},{"category":"function","location":"api/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.stopping_criterion","page":"Defining models","text":"stopping_criterion(model::AbstractTimestepModel, state, parameters)\n\nReturn whether model, a cognitive model or an environment model, with state as its state after a timestep and its parameters, has signaled for a simulation to stop there instead of running all of n_steps.\n\nsimulate and simulate! check it for both models before each timestep, with the states that the timestep before left, unless they are given check_stopping_criterion = false. When it returns true for either, no further timestep runs, and what is returned and added to the histories has the timesteps that ran. When the environment starts, the cognitive model has then made its last observation and acted on it, and the environment doesn't receive that action. When the cognitive model starts, the environment has then responded to the last action with an observation, and the cognitive model doesn't receive that observation. A model whose criterion is already met before the first timestep can't be simulated, and throws an error: a new simulation starts from the initial state, and reset! puts an InstantiatedModel back there. A fitted session checks it as SessionInfo describes.\n\nImplementation\n\nA model can add a method of this function. The default returns false, so a simulation runs all of its timesteps.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> struct Maze <: AbstractEnvironmentModel end\n\njulia> function CognitiveModeling.stopping_criterion(environment::Maze, state, parameters)\n           return state.position == parameters.goal\n       end;\n\njulia> CognitiveModeling.stopping_criterion(Maze(), (; position = 4), (; goal = 4))\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.stopping_criterion"},{"category":"function","location":"api/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.discrete_parameter_names","page":"Defining models","text":"discrete_parameter_names(model)\n\nReturn the names of the parameters of model that are discrete in parameter_domains, as a tuple. Every other parameter is continuous, see continuous_parameter_names. Useful for choosing how each parameter is sampled, since a discrete parameter needs another kind of sampler than a continuous one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.discrete_parameter_names"},{"category":"function","location":"api/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.continuous_parameter_names","page":"Defining models","text":"continuous_parameter_names(model)\n\nReturn the names of the parameters of model that are continuous, as a tuple, in the order of its attributes: every parameter that isn't discrete in parameter_domains, also those without a domain. The others are in discrete_parameter_names. Useful for choosing how each parameter is sampled, since a sampler which uses gradients can only sample the continuous ones.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.continuous_parameter_names"},{"category":"type","location":"api/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.CommonRLEnvironment","page":"Defining models","text":"CommonRLEnvironment(rl_env::CommonRLInterface.AbstractEnv; action_name)\n\nAn environment model around rl_env, an environment from CommonRLInterface.jl, which needs CommonRLInterface.jl to be loaded. action_name is the name of the cognitive model's action that act! receives, such as :arm.\n\nEnvironments from POMDPs.jl and ReinforcementLearning.jl can be used through it. POMDPTools.jl wraps a POMDP as POMDPCommonRLEnv{Any}(pomdp) and an MDP as MDPCommonRLEnv{Any}(mdp), which give each observation, or each state of an MDP, as the problem gives it. Its convert(CommonRLInterface.AbstractEnv, pomdp) gives the same environment with each converted to an array, which a cognitive model that reads them as the problem gives them, such as one with a belief updater of POMDPTools.jl, can't use. ReinforcementLearningBase.jl defines convert(CommonRLInterface.AbstractEnv, rl_env) for its environments.\n\nThe state is (; rl_env): initialize_state resets rl_env with CommonRLInterface.jl's reset! and returns it in the state, every timestep changes it in place with act! and passes it on in the new state, and prepare_state_for_saving copies it, with clone if it provides it and with deepcopy otherwise. Every simulation with the same CommonRLEnvironment therefore resets and changes the same rl_env, so simulations that run at the same time, such as on several threads, need one each. A method of prepare_state_for_saving for a CommonRLEnvironment with a specific type of rl_env can save something lighter. The parameters aren't used, and neither is the random number generator, since an environment of CommonRLInterface.jl has its own randomness.\n\nEach observation is a NamedTuple (; observation, reward, terminated): what observe gives, copied unless it is plain data such as a number, the reward that act! returned, and whether the episode has ended. Each is given in a Deterministic, so nothing more is sampled. Before the first action, when the action is BeforeFirstTimestep, the environment is at its initial observation, so the reward is NoValue and nothing is done with the action. The same goes for a timestep where the model takes no action, NoValue: the environment stays where it is. Its attribute_names are these three observations, the one action, no parameters and the state rl_env. Use EnvironmentStarts, so that the model observes before it acts.\n\nA simulation stops by itself when the episode ends, since the stopping_criterion of the environment is terminated of rl_env, so n_steps is the most timesteps it can take. An episode that has ended isn't started again, and an action given to it throws an error. A new call of simulate starts a new episode, and so does reset! of an InstantiatedModel, since both reset rl_env through initialize_state.\n\nExamples\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\nusing CommonRLInterface\n\n#A two-armed bandit where the first arm always pays, in episodes of two pulls\nmutable struct Bandit <: CommonRLInterface.AbstractEnv\n    pulls::Int\nend\nCommonRLInterface.reset!(rl_env::Bandit) = (rl_env.pulls = 0; nothing)\nCommonRLInterface.actions(rl_env::Bandit) = 1:2\nCommonRLInterface.observe(rl_env::Bandit) = rl_env.pulls\nCommonRLInterface.act!(rl_env::Bandit, arm) = (rl_env.pulls += 1; arm == 1 ? 1.0 : 0.0)\nCommonRLInterface.terminated(rl_env::Bandit) = rl_env.pulls == 2\n\n#A model which always pulls the first arm\nstruct PullsFirstArm <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\nCognitiveModeling.initialize_state(model::PullsFirstArm, parameters) = (;)\nCognitiveModeling.run_timestep(model::PullsFirstArm, context::ModelContext) = ModelOutput((; arm = Deterministic(1)))\nCognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::PullsFirstArm) = ModelAttributes(; parameters = (), states = (), inputs = (:observation, :reward, :terminated), outputs = :arm)\n\n#The simulation stops when the episode ends, before it has run all of n_steps\nsimulation = simulate(\n    PullsFirstArm(),\n    CommonRLEnvironment(Bandit(0); action_name = :arm);\n    who_starts = EnvironmentStarts(),\n    n_steps = 10,\n    cognitive_parameters = (;),\n    environment_parameters = (;),\n    cognitive_save_history = SaveHistory(SaveEverything()),\n)\n\n#Timestep 0, before the first timestep, and then what the environment gave on each timestep\nforeach(println, simulation.observations)\n\n# output\n\n(observation = BeforeFirstTimestep(), reward = BeforeFirstTimestep(), terminated = BeforeFirstTimestep())\n(observation = 0, reward = NoValue(), terminated = false)\n(observation = 1, reward = 1.0, terminated = false)\n(observation = 2, reward = 1.0, terminated = true)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.CommonRLEnvironment"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/interfacing_with_actr_models/#Models-from-ACTRModels.jl-as-cognitive-models","page":"Models from ACTRModels.jl as cognitive models","text":"Placeholder tutorial. It will show how to use ACTRModels.jl, a package for building ACT-R models, inside a cognitive model.\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Models from ACTRModels.jl as cognitive models"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/simulation/#Internals-of-simulation","page":"Simulation","text":"Pages = [\"simulation.md\"]","title":"Internals of simulation"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/simulation/#Simulating","page":"Simulation","text":"","title":"Simulating"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/simulation/#Instantiated-models-and-name-maps","page":"Simulation","text":"","title":"Instantiated models and name maps"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/simulation/#An-environment-with-fixed-observations","page":"Simulation","text":"","title":"An environment with fixed observations"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.check_names_fit","page":"Simulation","text":"check_names_fit(cognitive_model, environment, observation_map=nothing,\n                action_map=nothing)\n\nThrow an informative error if cognitive_model and environment can't be simulated together. Without a map, the outputs of each must be the inputs of the other in attribute_names: the observations the environment gives are the inputs in the cognitive model's attributes, and the actions the cognitive model gives are the inputs in the environment's attributes. With a NameMap for the observations or the actions, the map must take names in the giver's attributes, and give exactly the names in the receiver's attributes. Used by simulate, simulate!, and SessionInfo for a session with an environment.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_names_fit"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.check_observation_names_fit-Tuple{Any, Any, Any, Any, Nothing}","page":"Simulation","text":"check_observation_names_fit(cognitive_model, environment, cognitive_model_names,\n                            environment_names, observation_map)\ncheck_action_names_fit(cognitive_model, environment, cognitive_model_names,\n                       environment_names, action_map)\n\nThrow an informative error if the observations environment gives, its outputs, don't fit the inputs in the attributes of cognitive_model, or the actions cognitive_model gives, its outputs, don't fit the inputs in the attributes of environment, as check_names_fit describes, with or without a NameMap. Used by check_names_fit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_observation_names_fit"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.check_stopping_criterion_not_met-Tuple{CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel, Any, Any}","page":"Simulation","text":"check_stopping_criterion_not_met(model, state, parameters)\n\nThrow an informative error if stopping_criterion of model is already met with state, before the first timestep, since no timestep can then run. Used for each model by simulate_all_timesteps, and by fitted sessions, where there is nothing to check for an environment a session doesn't have.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_stopping_criterion_not_met"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.collect_initial_timestep_values-Tuple{Val{false}, Vararg{Any, 7}}","page":"Simulation","text":"collect_initial_timestep_values(save_initial_timestep_values::Val,\n                                previous_timestep_values, cognitive_model,\n                                environment, observation_map, action_map,\n                                cognitive_save_history, environment_save_history)\n\nReturn what is saved from before the first timestep, as timestep 0, arranged as the saved values of collect_timestep_values, or nothing when save_initial_timestep_values is Val(false).\n\nThe observation and the action are the previous ones, as each side receives them, the output distributions are BeforeFirstTimestep for every output, since none was given yet, and the states are the states before the first timestep. They must be saved before the first timestep is simulated, since it can change a state in place. Used by simulate_all_timesteps.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.collect_initial_timestep_values"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.collect_timestep_values-NTuple{10, Any}","page":"Simulation","text":"collect_timestep_values(environment_output, cognitive_output, environment_observation,\n                        observation, action, environment_action, cognitive_model,\n                        environment, cognitive_save_history, environment_save_history)\n\nCollect the values of a timestep in a NamedTuple: the state and the action of the cognitive model, and under environment the state and the observation of the environment, which the next timestep starts from, and under saved what cognitive_save_history and environment_save_history select, as map_saved arranges them.\n\nEach side has the values in its own names: observation is the observation as the cognitive model receives it and environment_observation as the environment gave it, and action is the action as the cognitive model gave it and environment_action as the environment receives it. The two of a pair differ when a NameMap selects or renames them. Used by simulate_timestep.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.collect_timestep_values"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.environment_values-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Simulation","text":"environment_values(state, observation)\n\nReturn the values of a timestep that the environment continues from, its state and its observation, in a NamedTuple. Used by collect_timestep_values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.environment_values"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.get_timestep_size-Tuple{AbstractVector, Any}","page":"Simulation","text":"get_timestep_size(timestep_sizes, t)\n\nReturn the size of timestep t. A vector has one size for each timestep, and anything else is the size of all of them. Used on each timestep, by the simulation loop and by fitted sessions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.get_timestep_size"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.initialize_all_storage-Tuple{Nothing, Any, Any}","page":"Simulation","text":"initialize_all_storage(initial_timestep_values, saved, n_steps)\n\nCreate the vectors for saving n_steps timesteps, arranged as map_saved arranges them, and typed by saved, what the first timestep saved. When initial_timestep_values isn't nothing, each vector starts with what it has for timestep 0, from collect_initial_timestep_values. Used by simulate_all_timesteps and by fitted sessions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.initialize_all_storage"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.map_names-Tuple{Any, Nothing}","page":"Simulation","text":"map_names(x, name_map)\n\nReturn what the receiver gets of the outputs x: all of x without a map, and with a NameMap the outputs it takes, under the names it gives them. Used on each timestep of a simulation, by simulate_timestep, and for timestep 0 by collect_initial_timestep_values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.map_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.map_saved-Union{Tuple{N}, Tuple{F}, Tuple{F, Vararg{NamedTuple, N}}} where {F, N}","page":"Simulation","text":"map_saved(f, saved...)\n\nApply f to each kind of saved value in saved, and return the results arranged in the same way: the cognitive model's states, observations, actions and action_distributions, and under environment the environment's states, observations, actions and observation_distributions, as simulate returns them. What a fitted session without an environment saves has no environment, and is arranged without one. With several, f takes the values of the same kind from each. Used to create, fill and shorten the vectors of the storage of a simulation and of a fitted session.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.map_saved"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.merge_default_parameters-Tuple{CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel, Any}","page":"Simulation","text":"merge_default_parameters(model, parameters)\n\nReturn parameters with the values from default_parameters of model, a cognitive model or an environment model, for the parameters it doesn't have, if model provides defaults for them. Used by simulate, InstantiatedModel and fitted sessions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.merge_default_parameters"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.sample_output-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Simulation","text":"sample_output(rng, output_distribution)\n\nSample an output from what a model returned, using rand. Each output in a NamedTuple is sampled on its own, so a NamedTuple inside it gives a NamedTuple of samples, and an entry that is NoValue is kept. A number, a string or a symbol is a value and not something to sample from, and throws an ArgumentError which names Deterministic. Used on each timestep of a simulation, by simulate_timestep.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.sample_output"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.saved_trajectories-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Simulation","text":"saved_trajectories(storage, timestep_sizes)\n\nReturn the saved trajectories of a simulation as simulate returns them: the vectors in storage, with the cognitive model's under states, observations, actions and action_distributions and the environment's under environment, and then timestep_sizes as given. Used by simulate, simulate! and fitted sessions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.saved_trajectories"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.simulate_all_timesteps-NTuple{15, Any}","page":"Simulation","text":"simulate_all_timesteps(previous_timestep_values, who_starts, cognitive_model,\n                       environment, cognitive_parameters, environment_parameters,\n                       cognitive_save_history, environment_save_history,\n                       observation_map, action_map, rng, timestep_sizes, n_steps,\n                       save_initial_timestep_values::Val, check_stopping_criterion)\n\nSimulate up to n_steps timesteps starting from previous_timestep_values, and return the vectors with what the simulated timesteps saved, followed by the values of the last one. When check_stopping_criterion is true, the simulation stops before n_steps timesteps if stopping_criterion of either model is met, and none can be simulated if it is met already. When save_initial_timestep_values is Val(true), each vector starts with what is saved from before the first timestep, as timestep 0.\n\nprevious_timestep_values and the values returned are those of a timestep: the state and the action of the cognitive model, and under environment the state and the observation of the environment, each in its own names. The vectors are arranged as map_saved arranges them, with what cognitive_save_history and environment_save_history select.\n\nsimulate! continues from the values of the last timestep instead of from what was saved, because what is saved of a state can be a selection or a copy of it, and because the next simulation can change a state in place. Used by simulate and simulate!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.simulate_all_timesteps"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.simulate_remaining_timesteps-NTuple{16, Any}","page":"Simulation","text":"simulate_remaining_timesteps(storage, timestep_values, who_starts, cognitive_model,\n                             environment, cognitive_parameters, environment_parameters,\n                             cognitive_save_history, environment_save_history,\n                             observation_map, action_map, rng, timestep_sizes, n_steps,\n                             offset, check_stopping_criterion)\n\nSimulate the timesteps after the first, and return the vectors with what the simulated timesteps saved, followed by the values of the last one. When check_stopping_criterion is true, the simulation stops before n_steps timesteps if stopping_criterion of either model is met. offset is 1 when the vectors start with timestep 0, and 0 otherwise, so that timestep t is element t + offset. Used by simulate_all_timesteps.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.simulate_remaining_timesteps"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.simulate_timestep-NTuple{8, Any}","page":"Simulation","text":"simulate_timestep(model, input, previous_state, previous_output, parameters,\n                  timestep_size, rng, save_history)\n\nSimulate a timestep of model on input, without the other side of a simulation, and return the output sampled from what it gave, its new state, and under saved what save_history selects of its states, inputs, outputs and output_distributions, in a NamedTuple. The input must have the names in the attributes of model. simulate and simulate! on a single input both use this.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.simulate_timestep"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.simulate_timestep-Tuple{AgentStarts, Vararg{Any, 11}}","page":"Simulation","text":"simulate_timestep(::AgentStarts, cognitive_model, environment, cognitive_parameters,\n                  environment_parameters, cognitive_save_history,\n                  environment_save_history, observation_map, action_map, rng,\n                  timestep_size, previous)\n\nSimulate a timestep where the agent moves first, starting from previous, the values of the timestep before, and return its values, from collect_timestep_values. Used by simulate_all_timesteps and simulate_remaining_timesteps.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.simulate_timestep"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.simulate_timestep-Tuple{EnvironmentStarts, Vararg{Any, 11}}","page":"Simulation","text":"simulate_timestep(::EnvironmentStarts, cognitive_model, environment,\n                  cognitive_parameters, environment_parameters,\n                  cognitive_save_history, environment_save_history, observation_map,\n                  action_map, rng, timestep_size, previous)\n\nSimulate a timestep where the environment moves first, starting from previous, the values of the timestep before, and return its values, from collect_timestep_values. Used by simulate_all_timesteps and simulate_remaining_timesteps.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.simulate_timestep"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.store_timestep_values!-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Simulation","text":"store_timestep_values!(storage, saved, t)\n\nStore saved, what timestep t saved, as element t of each vector in storage, with store_value!, and return the vectors arranged in the same way, in which a vector is wider when a value didn't fit it. Used by simulate_remaining_timesteps and by fitted sessions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.store_timestep_values!"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.given_name-Tuple{Symbol}","page":"Simulation","text":"given_name(name)\nreceived_name(name)\n\nReturn the name an output is given under and the name it is received under, from one argument of a NameMap: the two sides of a :given => :received pair, or a single Symbol, which is received under the name it is given with. Used by the constructor of NameMap.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.given_name"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.starting_history-Tuple{CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel, Any, Any, SaveHistory}","page":"Simulation","text":"starting_history(model, state, previous_output, save_history)\n\nReturn the history that an InstantiatedModel starts with: a ModelHistory with what save_history selects of where it starts, as timestep 0, and BeforeFirstTimestep as the size of the timestep, since none has run yet. When nothing is selected, or save_history doesn't save the initial timestep values, the vectors are empty.\n\nA model hasn't received anything when it starts, so that entry has every input in the model's attributes as BeforeFirstTimestep(), and so has every output distribution. reset! starts from the same history as the constructors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.starting_history"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.fixed_observation-Tuple{Any}","page":"Simulation","text":"fixed_observation(value)\n\nReturn an entry of a FixedObservationsEnvironment as the environment gives it: in a Deterministic, so that nothing is sampled, or as it is when it marks that there is no value, such as NoValue. Used by the timestep of a FixedObservationsEnvironment.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.fixed_observation"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.n_fixed_timesteps-Tuple{FixedObservationsEnvironment}","page":"Simulation","text":"n_fixed_timesteps(environment)\n\nReturn the number of timesteps that environment, a FixedObservationsEnvironment, has observations for. Used by its timestep and by its stopping_criterion.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.n_fixed_timesteps"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/getting_started/#Getting-started","page":"Getting started","text":"Placeholder tutorial. Each file in docs/literate/ is a plain Julia script that becomes a page like this one when the documentation is built.\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Getting started"},{"category":"section","location":"api/fitting_with_turing/#Fitting-with-Turing.jl","page":"Fitting with Turing.jl","text":"The Turing.jl models of the sessions on the page Fitting, what a model can add for them, and reading the trajectories they saved from a chain.\n\nPages = [\"fitting_with_turing.md\"]","title":"Fitting with Turing.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"api/fitting_with_turing/#Turing.jl-models","page":"Fitting with Turing.jl","text":"","title":"Turing.jl models"},{"category":"section","location":"api/fitting_with_turing/#Postprocessing","page":"Fitting with Turing.jl","text":"","title":"Postprocessing"},{"category":"section","location":"api/fitting_with_turing/#Predictive-checks","page":"Fitting with Turing.jl","text":"With Turing.jl loaded, predict_session_trajectories takes a model made by turing_full_model, turing_multiple_behavioral_sessions or turing_behavioral_session, or such a model inside turing_catch_invalid_parameters, and a chain sampled from it. It returns the simulated trajectories as a FlexiChain, as session_trajectories_chain makes it. For a prior predictive check, give a chain sampled from the prior, such as sample(model, Prior(), 1000).\n\nchain = sample(model, NUTS(), 1000)\nsimulated = predict_session_trajectories(model, chain; cognitive_save_history = SaveHistory(; outputs = :choice))\n\n#The simulated choices of participant P1, for every sample\nsimulated[@varname(P1.choice)]","title":"Predictive checks"},{"category":"section","location":"api/fitting_with_turing/#Functions-that-a-model-can-implement-for-Turing.jl","page":"Fitting with Turing.jl","text":"A model can add methods of these to change how it runs when it is fitted with Turing.jl, and must when it samples anything of its own, within a timestep or when it starts. They are public, but not exported.","title":"Functions that a model can implement for Turing.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"api/fitting_with_turing/#Fitting-several-sessions","page":"Fitting with Turing.jl","text":"These are what the parts of turing_full_model are written with, beside the types and functions on the pages Fitting and Parameter models. They are public, but not exported. In a Turing.jl model of several sessions, session i of CognitiveModeling.session_index has its variables under session[i].","title":"Fitting several sessions"},{"category":"function","location":"api/fitting_with_turing/#CognitiveModeling.turing_behavioral_session","page":"Fitting with Turing.jl","text":"turing_behavioral_session(session_info::SessionInfo, cognitive_parameters)\nturing_behavioral_session(session_info::SessionInfo, cognitive_parameters,\n                          environment_parameters=(;))\n\nCreate a Turing.jl model of one session: the cognitive model in session_info goes through the timesteps of its data with cognitive_parameters, and the actions in the data are scored against the action distributions it gives. The second form is for a session with an environment model, which takes its parameters too. This needs Turing.jl to be loaded.\n\nThe model is a building block for a Turing.jl model which samples the parameters, as a submodel with its own prefix: to_submodel(prefix(turing_behavioral_session(...), @varname(session)), false). turing_multiple_behavioral_sessions runs one for each of several sessions, and turing_full_model samples their parameters too.\n\nArguments\n\nsession_info::SessionInfo: the data, the models and the settings of the session, prepared once with SessionInfo.\ncognitive_parameters::NamedTuple: the parameters of the cognitive model, usually the random variables of the model around this one. Those it doesn't have take the values in default_parameters, if the model provides defaults for them.\nenvironment_parameters::NamedTuple=(;): the parameters of the environment model, with its default values for those that aren't given, if it provides any. A session without an environment takes none.\n\nWhat it does\n\nIn outline, the model of a session without an environment looks like this. It is not the actual code, which also checks the names and the parameters, and saves what is selected:\n\n#The state where the session starts, which a model can sample\nstate ~ to_submodel(turing_initialize_state(cognitive_model, cognitive_parameters))\n\nfor t in 1:n_timesteps\n\n    #The cognitive model gives a distribution for each action, under the prefix timestep[t]\n    output ~ to_submodel(turing_run_timestep(cognitive_model, context))\n\n    #An action that is missing in the data is a latent variable with that distribution\n    choice.imputed ~ output.output_distribution.choice\nend\n\n#The actions in the data are scored once, against the distributions of their timesteps\nactions_to_condition_on ~ product_distribution(action_distributions)\n\n#The parameters as they were given, and the trajectories over the timesteps that the save histories select\nreturn (; cognitive_parameters, environment_parameters, saved_behavioral_trajectories)\n\nA session which includes its environment runs the environment model in the same way on each timestep, before the cognitive model when the environment starts and after it when the agent starts, and scores the observations in the data like the actions.\n\nThe states where the session starts come from turing_initialize_state of each model with the parameters, so that a starting state which is a parameter is fitted like any other, and a model can sample its start. Both models are treated the same way on each timestep: turing_run_timestep of the cognitive model runs as a submodel with the prefix timestep[t], so a model which samples within a timestep has its own variables for each timestep, such as timestep[3].noise, and an environment model runs with the prefix timestep[t].environment. The outputs of each, the actions and the observations, are then taken from the data where they are recorded, and where the data has MissingValue they are imputed from what the model gave: as a latent variable with that distribution, named timestep[3].choice.imputed or timestep[3].environment.reward.imputed, or as the value itself if it is a Deterministic. Where the data has UnimputedMissingValue, or a marker for nothing happening, the models receive the marker as it is, and nothing is sampled or scored for it. The sampler must handle the type of a latent variable: a continuous value can be sampled with NUTS, a discrete one needs a sampler for discrete variables. The actions in the data are scored at the end as observed values, in one product_distribution over every action and timestep, against the distributions the cognitive model gave for them. The observations in the data are scored the same way against the distributions the environment gave, so that they inform the missing values and the environment's parameters. A Deterministic is scored as a point mass, with a log density of 0 for its value and -Inf for any other, and only a number or a Bool can be, so a value in the data that the model gives as another kind of Deterministic is an error, and so is one where the model gave nothing, NoValue. When the session doesn't include its environment, as the include_environment of session_info decides, which by default is when nothing in the data is MissingValue() and the environment's history isn't saved, the session is the same as one without an environment, with the same variables. The stopping_criterion of the models is checked as the check_stopping_criterion of session_info says: by default only after the last timestep with data, so that a fit runs every timestep and a session from prepare_for_prediction can end early.\n\nWhat it returns\n\nA NamedTuple with cognitive_parameters and environment_parameters, the parameters as they were given, without the default values of the models, and saved_behavioral_trajectories, the trajectories that the save histories of session_info select, which returned(model, chain) gives for each sample. A model around the session which returns what it returns has its results read with session_trajectories_chain and session_parameters_chain, also when it transforms its variables into the parameters.\n\nThe trajectories are a NamedTuple with the same entries as simulate returns: states, observations, actions and action_distributions of the cognitive model, environment with the states, observations, actions and observation_distributions of the environment when the session has one, which are empty when it didn't run, and timestep_sizes. Each is a vector with one element for timestep 0, before the first timestep, and one for each timestep that ran. Timestep 0 has the previous observation and action, which are BeforeFirstTimestep unless others are given, BeforeFirstTimestep() for every distribution, and the initial states, which can depend on the parameters. Element t + 1 is then timestep t, and a SaveHistory with save_initial_timestep_values = false leaves timestep 0 out. The returned actions and observations are those the models received, with the imputed values. timestep_sizes has the size of each timestep, after BeforeFirstTimestep() for timestep 0 when it is there, when anything is selected, and is empty otherwise. Any latent variable in a session, imputed or from a model which samples within a timestep, greatly increases the amount of estimated variables, which will generally make fitting the model much slower.\n\nExamples\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\nusing Turing\n\n#The model to fit, a Rescorla-Wagner model with a softmax choice\nstruct RescorlaWagner <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(model::RescorlaWagner, parameters)\n    return (; expected_reward = parameters.initial_value)\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.run_timestep(model::RescorlaWagner, context::ModelContext)\n    V₋₁ = context.previous_state.expected_reward\n    Vₜ = V₋₁ + context.parameters.learning_rate * (context.input.reward - V₋₁)\n    Pₜ = BernoulliLogit(context.parameters.inverse_temperature * (Vₜ - 0.5))\n    return ModelOutput((; choice = Pₜ), (; expected_reward = Vₜ))\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::RescorlaWagner)\n    return ModelAttributes(;\n        parameters = (:learning_rate, :inverse_temperature, :initial_value),\n        states = :expected_reward,\n        inputs = :reward,\n        outputs = :choice,\n    )\nend\n\nCognitiveModeling.default_parameters(model::RescorlaWagner) = (; initial_value = 0.5)\n\n#The data of one session, where the third choice wasn't recorded, prepared once\nsession_info = SessionInfo(\n    RescorlaWagner(),\n    (; reward = [1.0, 0.0, 1.0, 1.0]),\n    (; choice = [true, false, MissingValue(), true]);\n    who_starts = EnvironmentStarts(),\n    cognitive_save_history = SaveHistory(; states = :expected_reward),\n)\n\n#Priors on the parameters, and the session as a submodel\n@model function fit_one_session(session_info)\n    learning_rate ~ Beta(2, 2)\n    inverse_temperature ~ LogNormal(0, 1)\n    session ~ to_submodel(\n        prefix(\n            turing_behavioral_session(session_info, (; learning_rate, inverse_temperature)),\n            @varname(session),\n        ),\n        false,\n    )\n    return session\nend\n\nchain = sample(fit_one_session(session_info), NUTS(), 1000)\n\n#The parameters and the expected rewards on each timestep, for each sample\nresults = returned(fit_one_session(session_info), chain)\nresults[1].saved_behavioral_trajectories.states\n\n#The same as chains, which merge into one\nparameters = session_parameters_chain(results)\ntrajectories = session_trajectories_chain(results)\nmerge(parameters, trajectories)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.turing_behavioral_session"},{"category":"function","location":"api/fitting_with_turing/#CognitiveModeling.turing_catch_invalid_parameters","page":"Fitting with Turing.jl","text":"turing_catch_invalid_parameters(model)\n\nCreate a Turing.jl model which runs model, any Turing.jl model, inside a try block: an InvalidParametersError thrown by a cognitive model or an environment model then gives the parameters a log density of -Inf, so that a sampler rejects the sample instead of stopping, and the model returns nothing for that sample. Otherwise it returns what model returns. Any other error is thrown as it is, also a ParameterDomainError for a parameter outside its domain. This needs Turing.jl to be loaded.\n\nIt goes around the part of a model that can throw, with the parameters sampled outside it, as in\n\n@model function fit_one_session(session_info)\n    learning_rate ~ Beta(2, 2)\n    session ~ to_submodel(\n        turing_catch_invalid_parameters(\n            turing_behavioral_session(session_info, (; learning_rate)),\n        ),\n        false,\n    )\nend\n\nand turing_full_model does this with catch_invalid_parameters = true. The variables sampled inside the try block are lost when it catches an error, so around a whole model a sampler can't start when its first draw is rejected. Nothing in the package catches the error unless this is used, so a sampler stops with it otherwise. Not every automatic differentiation backend can differentiate a model with it, as the page on automatic differentiation says.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.turing_catch_invalid_parameters"},{"category":"function","location":"api/fitting_with_turing/#CognitiveModeling.turing_full_model","page":"Fitting with Turing.jl","text":"turing_full_model(cognitive_parameters_model, sessions_info::MultipleSessionsInfo;\n                  environment_parameters_model=NoParameters(),\n                  downstream_model=NoDownstreamModel(),\n                  fixed_cognitive_parameters=(;), fixed_environment_parameters=(;),\n                  catch_invalid_parameters=false, parallelization=SerialSessions(),\n                  include_submodel_prefixes=false)\n\nCreate the Turing.jl model which fits a cognitive model to several sessions, in three parts: cognitive_parameters_model gives or samples the cognitive parameters of every session, and environment_parameters_model those of the environment model, the sessions in sessions_info run with them as turing_multiple_behavioral_sessions, and downstream_model can do something with the trajectories they saved, such as a regression of a measurement on a state. This needs Turing.jl to be loaded.\n\nArguments\n\ncognitive_parameters_model: the parameter model of the cognitive model, which gives or estimates its parameters for every session: a subtype of AbstractParametersModel, such as IndependentParameters with a prior for each parameter. A tuple of parameter models, or a NamedTuple of them, is made into a CompositeParameters of them, with each part of a NamedTuple under its name. So is a tuple or a NamedTuple given as environment_parameters_model.\nsessions_info::MultipleSessionsInfo: the sessions, prepared once with MultipleSessionsInfo.\nenvironment_parameters_model=NoParameters(): the parameter model of the environment model of the sessions, when they have one. By default the environment's parameters take its default values, and FixedParameters gives others. When they are estimated, every session includes its environment, also those with nothing to impute, so that the observations in the data inform them, which is an error for a session prepared with include_environment = false. Sessions without an environment take no parameters.\ndownstream_model=NoDownstreamModel(): a final submodel which is run after the sessions, such as for correlations with other kinds of data: a subtype of AbstractDownstreamModel. The default samples nothing.\nfixed_cognitive_parameters::NamedTuple=(;) and fixed_environment_parameters::NamedTuple=(;): values which every session gets for the parameters they name, such as (; initial_value = 0.3), while the parameter models give the others. A parameter is either fixed here or given by its parameter model, and a fixed parameter must be one in the model's attributes.\ncatch_invalid_parameters::Bool=false: whether an InvalidParametersError in a session rejects the sample instead of stopping the sampler, by running the sessions inside turing_catch_invalid_parameters. If the first draw is rejected and the downstream model has variables of its own, give the sampler valid initial_params. A ParameterDomainError isn't caught, and stops the sampler.\nparallelization=SerialSessions(): whether the sessions run over the threads Julia was started with, with ParallelSessions(). The model must then be marked with setthreadsafe(model, true) from DynamicPPL before it is sampled: without the mark, a threaded model has a wrong log density with no error, so the sessions throw an error instead.\ninclude_submodel_prefixes::Bool=false: whether the variables of the two parameter models and of the downstream model are named under cognitive_parameters_model, environment_parameters_model and downstream_model. Without the prefixes, the names are shorter, but a variable of the same name in two of them is one variable, which sample refuses with an error, and which a log density gives one value with both priors counted. A warning says so when the model is made, when the cognitive model and the environment model have a parameter of the same name and both parameter models sample.\n\nThe variables of a parameter model are named as it names them, or under the parameter model's name with include_submodel_prefixes = true. With IndependentParameters, each parameter is a vector with a value for each session, such as learning_rate.session, so the learning rate of the third session is learning_rate.session[3]. With HierarchicalGroupedParameters, each parameter of a group-level distribution is named after its prior, such as learning_rate.mean.group and learning_rate.sd.group, which is one value without groups, and otherwise has the value of each group under the group's symbol name, such as learning_rate.mean.group.clinical. The values of the sessions before the transformation are learning_rate.standardized, or learning_rate.session for a parameter that is centered. With HierarchicalRegressionParameters, the coefficients of the intercept, the predictors and the interactions of a parameter are one variable, learning_rate.coef, with each under its name, such as learning_rate.coef.intercept, learning_rate.coef.age, learning_rate.coef.diagnosis_clinical for a level of a categorical predictor, and learning_rate.coef.age_by_diagnosis_clinical for an interaction. Varying effects have the variables of each of their coefficients under their name and the coefficient's: the standard deviation, learning_rate.participant.intercept.sd, which has the value of each group under the group's symbol name when the levels are grouped, such as learning_rate.participant.intercept.sd.clinical, and a standardized value for each level, learning_rate.participant.intercept.standardized, or the deviation of each level, learning_rate.participant.intercept.deviation, when they are centered. A regression with a family also has the value of every session as a vector, learning_rate.session, and the other parameters of the family's distribution as one variable, learning_rate.family, with each under its name, such as learning_rate.family.sd. A part of a CompositeParameters that is given with a name has its variables under that name, such as noise.bias.session. The variables of the sessions are under session[i], and those of the downstream model as it names them, or under downstream_model with the prefixes.\n\nThe model returns a NamedTuple with cognitive_parameters, environment_parameters, saved_behavioral_trajectories and downstream_values: the cognitive parameters and the environment parameters of every session as the parameter models gave them, the trajectories each session saved, as turing_multiple_behavioral_sessions returns them, and what the downstream model returned. returned(model, chain) gives them for each sample, so a fitted state is read from the chain without simulating again.\n\nSaving states adds a cost to gradient calculations. Without a downstream model that needs them, a fit is fastest with sessions that save nothing, and the trajectories are then read from the chain afterwards with session_trajectories_chain, or with returned on the model of a copy of the sessions that saves them, MultipleSessionsInfo(sessions_info; cognitive_save_history = SaveHistory(; states = :expected_reward)), since the two models have the same variables. A warning says so when the sessions save anything and there is no downstream model.\n\nExamples\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\nusing Turing\n\n#The data of two sessions, as a table with one vector for each column\ntable = (;\n    id = [\"S1\", \"S1\", \"S1\", \"S2\", \"S2\", \"S2\"],\n    reward = [1.0, 0.0, 1.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0],\n    choice = [true, false, true, false, missing, true],\n)\n\n#The sessions, with the RescorlaWagner model of turing_behavioral_session\nsessions_info = MultipleSessionsInfo(\n    RescorlaWagner(),\n    table;\n    observation_columns = :reward,\n    action_columns = :choice,\n    session_columns = :id,\n    who_starts = EnvironmentStarts(),\n    cognitive_save_history = SaveHistory(; states = :expected_reward),\n)\n\n#Each session has its own parameters, from the same priors\ncognitive_parameters_model = IndependentParameters((;\n    learning_rate = Beta(2, 2),\n    inverse_temperature = LogNormal(0, 1),\n))\n\nmodel = turing_full_model(cognitive_parameters_model, sessions_info)\nchain = sample(model, NUTS(), 1000)\n\n#The parameters and the expected rewards of every session, for each sample\nresults = returned(model, chain)\nresults[1].cognitive_parameters.learning_rate\nresults[1].saved_behavioral_trajectories[2].states\n\n#The same fit without saving, and the states read from the chain afterwards\nsessions_without_saving = MultipleSessionsInfo(\n    sessions_info; cognitive_save_history = SaveHistory(),\n)\nunsaved = turing_full_model(cognitive_parameters_model, sessions_without_saving)\nchain = sample(unsaved, NUTS(), 1000)\nresults = returned(model, chain)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.turing_full_model"},{"category":"function","location":"api/fitting_with_turing/#CognitiveModeling.turing_multiple_behavioral_sessions","page":"Fitting with Turing.jl","text":"turing_multiple_behavioral_sessions(sessions_info::MultipleSessionsInfo,\n                                    cognitive_parameters, environment_parameters=(;);\n                                    parallelization=SerialSessions())\n\nCreate a Turing.jl model which runs turing_behavioral_session for every session in sessions_info, each with its own cognitive parameters, as a submodel with the prefix session[i], so that a latent variable of the third session is named like session[3].timestep[2].choice.imputed. Which session i is stands in sessions_info.session_ids[i], and session_index gives i for a data identifier. Environment parameters can only be given when the sessions have an environment model. With parallelization = ParallelSessions(), the sessions run over the threads Julia was started with. The model that is evaluated, which is the outermost one, must then be marked with setthreadsafe(model, true) from DynamicPPL, since the log density of a threaded model that isn't is wrong with no error; the sessions throw an error when it isn't marked. See parallelization in turing_full_model. This needs Turing.jl to be loaded.\n\ncognitive_parameters is a NamedTuple with one vector for each parameter, with one value for every session in the order of sessions_info, which is what a parameter model returns, see turing_parameters_model, and environment_parameters is in the same form. It returns a NamedTuple with cognitive_parameters and environment_parameters as they were given, and saved_behavioral_trajectories, a vector with the trajectories each session saved, in the same order, as turing_behavioral_session returns them. The model is a building block for a Turing.jl model which samples the parameters of the sessions in some way of its own, as a submodel without a prefix: to_submodel(turing_multiple_behavioral_sessions(sessions_info, parameters), false). turing_full_model does this with parameter models. A model of one's own which returns what it returns has its results read with session_trajectories_chain and session_parameters_chain, also when it transforms its variables into the parameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.turing_multiple_behavioral_sessions"},{"category":"function","location":"api/fitting_with_turing/#CognitiveModeling.session_parameters_chain","page":"Fitting with Turing.jl","text":"session_parameters_chain(results, sessions_info::MultipleSessionsInfo;\n                         iter_indices=1:size(results, 1),\n                         chain_indices=1:size(results, 2))\nsession_parameters_chain(results; iter_indices=1:size(results, 1),\n                         chain_indices=1:size(results, 2))\nsession_parameters_chain(model, chain)\n\nReturn the parameters of every fitted session, for every sample, as a FlexiChain with the same samples as the fit, named after the session: P1.learning_rate for a parameter of the cognitive model, and P1.environment.mean_reward for one of the environment model. This needs FlexiChains.jl to be loaded, which Turing.jl loads.\n\nresults is a matrix with one element for each sample, as returned(model, chain) gives it, which is what one of three models returns, as for session_trajectories_chain: turing_full_model or turing_multiple_behavioral_sessions, given with their MultipleSessionsInfo, or turing_behavioral_session, given without a session info, whose parameters are named without a session, as learning_rate and environment.mean_reward. The parameters are those each session was given, without the default values of its models. With a full model, they are those the parameter models gave each session, so a parameter model other than IndependentParameters gives parameters that aren't in the chain itself, such as one value for every session from IdenticalParameters, and values from FixedParameters that were never sampled. A model of one's own around turing_multiple_behavioral_sessions or turing_behavioral_session gives the parameters it passed to the sessions, such as transformations of its variables, when it returns what they return. The third form, in the Turing extension, takes a model made by one of the three functions, or such a model inside turing_catch_invalid_parameters, and a chain sampled from it, and numbers the samples as the chain does. The result merges with the chain of session_trajectories_chain, which has the same samples, with merge.\n\nExamples\n\nchain = sample(model, NUTS(), 1000)\nparameters = session_parameters_chain(model, chain)\n\n#The learning rate of participant P1, for every sample\nparameters[@varname(P1.learning_rate)]\n\n#The same from a model of one's own around the sessions, which returns what they return\nparameters = session_parameters_chain(returned(own_model, chain), sessions_info)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.session_parameters_chain"},{"category":"function","location":"api/fitting_with_turing/#CognitiveModeling.session_trajectories_chain","page":"Fitting with Turing.jl","text":"session_trajectories_chain(results, sessions_info::MultipleSessionsInfo;\n                           iter_indices=1:size(results, 1),\n                           chain_indices=1:size(results, 2))\nsession_trajectories_chain(results; iter_indices=1:size(results, 1),\n                           chain_indices=1:size(results, 2))\nsession_trajectories_chain(model, chain; cognitive_save_history=SaveHistory(),\n                           environment_save_history=SaveHistory())\n\nReturn the trajectories that fitted sessions saved, for every sample, as a FlexiChain from FlexiChains.jl with the same samples as the fit, so that they can be summarized and plotted like the chain itself. The trajectories of a session are what changes over its timesteps: the states, the observations and the actions of its models, and the distributions they gave. This needs FlexiChains.jl to be loaded, which Turing.jl loads. The names of the entries are the VarNames that FlexiChains.jl uses, written as @varname(P1.expected_value) with FlexiChains.jl 0.6, which Turing.jl uses, and as @vn(P1.expected_value) with 0.7.\n\nresults is a matrix with one element for each sample, iterations down and chains across, as returned(model, chain) gives it in Turing.jl. Its elements are what one of three models returns:\n\nturing_full_model, given with its MultipleSessionsInfo. The entries of each session are named after its symbol name, such as P1.expected_value. The parameters of the sessions are a chain of their own, see session_parameters_chain, and what a downstream model returned isn't included.\nturing_multiple_behavioral_sessions, given with its MultipleSessionsInfo, named in the same way.\nturing_behavioral_session, given without a session info, with no symbol name of a session in the names, such as expected_value.\n\nA model of one's own around turing_multiple_behavioral_sessions or turing_behavioral_session gives its results in the same way when it returns what they return.\n\nThe trajectories each session saved, as its SaveHistorys selected, are one entry for each name, with the session's symbol name first. The cognitive model's values are named after the attribute alone, since a model uses a name for one attribute only: a state as P1.expected_value, an observation as P1.reward and an action as P1.choice. An action distribution is named like its action, so it is under its kind, as P1.action_distributions.choice. The environment's values are under environment, in the same way: P1.environment.level for a state, P1.environment.reward for an observation it gave, P1.environment.choice for an action it received and P1.environment.observation_distributions.reward. Each entry holds a vector over the timesteps for every sample, which starts with timestep 0 when the initial timestep values are saved, so that element t + 1 is timestep t, as everywhere in the package. The observations, the actions and the distributions of timestep 0 are BeforeFirstTimestep, so a summary of those entries needs timestep 0 left out. The symbol name of a session with several session columns joins the name and the value of each column, as in id_S1__block_2, as CognitiveModeling.session_id_to_symbol gives it. iter_indices and chain_indices number the samples as the chain that was fitted does.\n\nThe third form, in the Turing extension, takes a model made by one of the three functions, or such a model inside turing_catch_invalid_parameters, and a chain sampled from it. It makes a copy of the model whose sessions save what the two save histories select, gives it to returned, and makes the chain of the results, numbered as chain is. A fit is fastest when its sessions save nothing, and their trajectories are then read afterwards in this way. Selecting anything in environment_save_history makes every session run its environment, see SessionInfo.\n\nExamples\n\nchain = sample(model, NUTS(), 1000)\nsession_trajectories = session_trajectories_chain(model, chain; cognitive_save_history = SaveHistory(; states = :expected_value))\n\n#The expected values of participant P2 over the timesteps, for every sample\nsession_trajectories[@varname(P2.expected_value)]\n\n#The parameters of the sessions, in the same chain\neverything = merge(session_trajectories, session_parameters_chain(model, chain))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.session_trajectories_chain"},{"category":"function","location":"api/fitting_with_turing/#CognitiveModeling.turing_run_timestep","page":"Fitting with Turing.jl","text":"turing_run_timestep(model::AbstractTimestepModel, context::ModelContext)\n\nCreate a Turing.jl model for a single timestep of model, a cognitive model or an environment model, to be used as a submodel, which returns a ModelOutput. This needs Turing.jl to be loaded.\n\nImplementation\n\nA model can add a method of this function. The default calls run_timestep. A model which samples within a timestep needs to add a method, however; or if any other changes are desired within Turing. It must sample with ~ instead of from context.rng. Otherwise the model has a different log density each time it is evaluated, with no error. The same holds for an environment model, which fitting uses to fill in observations that are MissingValue in the data: what it samples itself, such as a change of its state, must be sampled with ~, while fitting samples the observations from what it returns.\n\nExamples\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\nusing Turing\n\nstruct NoisyRescorlaWagner <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\nTuring.@model function CognitiveModeling.turing_run_timestep(\n        model::NoisyRescorlaWagner,\n        context::ModelContext,\n    )\n\n    ## Unpack the context ##\n    #Reward observed on this timestep\n    r = context.input.reward\n    #Expected reward from the previous state\n    V₋₁ = context.previous_state.expected_reward\n    #Learning rate parameter\n    α = context.parameters.learning_rate\n\n    ## Update state ##\n    #Sample the noise in the update\n    ϵ ~ Normal(0, context.parameters.noise)\n    #Update expected reward using the Rescorla-Wagner rule\n    Vₜ = V₋₁ + α * (r - V₋₁) + ϵ\n\n    return ModelOutput((; choice = BernoulliLogit(Vₜ - 0.5)), (; expected_reward = Vₜ))\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.turing_run_timestep"},{"category":"function","location":"api/fitting_with_turing/#CognitiveModeling.turing_initialize_state","page":"Fitting with Turing.jl","text":"turing_initialize_state(model::AbstractTimestepModel, parameters)\n\nCreate a Turing.jl model which gives the state of model, a cognitive model or an environment model, before the first timestep, to be used as a submodel, as initialize_state does. This needs Turing.jl to be loaded.\n\nImplementation\n\nA model can add a method of this function. The default calls initialize_state, which is enough for most models, since a starting state that is fitted is simply one that is copied from a parameter there: a fitted session runs it with the parameters of each evaluation, so a prior on the parameter is a prior on the starting state. A model which samples something of its own when it initializes needs to add a method, and sample it with ~, as for turing_run_timestep. Its variables are named under initial_cognitive_state, or initial_environment_state for an environment model.\n\nExamples\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\nusing Turing\n\nstruct UncertainStart <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\nTuring.@model function CognitiveModeling.turing_initialize_state(\n        model::UncertainStart,\n        parameters,\n    )\n\n    #Sample where the expected reward starts, around the parameter for it\n    V₀ ~ Normal(parameters.initial_value, parameters.initial_noise)\n\n    return (; expected_reward = V₀)\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.turing_initialize_state"},{"category":"function","location":"api/fitting_with_turing/#CognitiveModeling.turing_parameters_model","page":"Fitting with Turing.jl","text":"turing_parameters_model(parameters_model, sessions_info::MultipleSessionsInfo)\n\nCreate the Turing.jl model of parameters_model, a parameter model, which gives or samples the parameters of every session in sessions_info, for the cognitive model or the environment model of a turing_full_model. This needs Turing.jl to be loaded.\n\nThe model must return a NamedTuple with one entry for each parameter, named as the model names it, and a vector in each with one value for every session, in the order of the sessions. Each subtype of AbstractParametersModel that comes with CognitiveModeling.jl has a method.\n\nImplementation\n\nA parameter model of another kind, such as a regression, is a subtype of AbstractParametersModel with a method of this function. It can read n_sessions and the data identifiers of the sessions from sessions_info, the columns of the table that the sessions were split from, when there is one, with CognitiveModeling.session_column_values, such as the covariates of a regression, and anything else from its own fields. What it reads from the table is best read once, in a method of CognitiveModeling.prepare_parameters_model. The full model treats it as one that samples, so that with it the environment of every session runs, and the observations in the data inform the environment's parameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.turing_parameters_model"},{"category":"function","location":"api/fitting_with_turing/#CognitiveModeling.turing_downstream_model","page":"Fitting with Turing.jl","text":"turing_downstream_model(downstream_model, saved_behavioral_trajectories,\n                        cognitive_parameters, sessions_info::MultipleSessionsInfo)\n\nCreate the Turing.jl model of downstream_model, the final submodel of a turing_full_model, which is run after the sessions, with the trajectories the sessions saved, the cognitive parameters of every session as the parameter model returned them, and the sessions' sessions_info. This needs Turing.jl to be loaded.\n\nThe model can return anything, which the full model returns as its downstream_values. NoDownstreamModel is the default, with a method which samples nothing and returns nothing.\n\nImplementation\n\nA downstream model is a subtype of AbstractDownstreamModel with a method of this function, which can ignore any of the arguments. A regression of some measurement on a state of the cognitive model, for instance, reads the state from saved_behavioral_trajectories, a vector with the saved trajectories of each session as turing_behavioral_session returns them, so the cognitive_save_history of the sessions must select that state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.turing_downstream_model"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/interfacing_with_sequential_sampling_models/#Models-from-SequentialSamplingModels.jl-as-cognitive-models","page":"Models from SequentialSamplingModels.jl as cognitive models","text":"Placeholder tutorial. It will show how to use the sequential sampling models of SequentialSamplingModels.jl, such as the drift diffusion model, as what a cognitive model samples its actions from, so that it gives both a choice and a response time.\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Models from SequentialSamplingModels.jl as cognitive models"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/fitting/#Internals-of-fitting","page":"Sessions","text":"The internals of the sessions and of reading them from a table. Those of parameter models and of the utility functions are on pages of their own: Parameter models, Preparing models and Within models.\n\nPages = [\"fitting.md\"]","title":"Internals of fitting"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/fitting/#Sessions","page":"Sessions","text":"","title":"Sessions"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/fitting/#Reading-tables-with-Tables.jl","page":"Sessions","text":"","title":"Reading tables with Tables.jl"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.prepare_session-Tuple{CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel, Any, Any}","page":"Sessions","text":"prepare_session(cognitive_model, observations, actions; who_starts, <keyword arguments>)\n\nReturn the SessionInfo of one session, with the checks of the data and the names, the missing values in the data marked, whether the environment runs, and what is scored and returned prepared, as its constructor describes. Used by SessionInfo and MultipleSessionsInfo.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.prepare_session"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.column_table-Tuple{NamedTuple}","page":"Sessions","text":"column_table(table)\n\nReturn table as a NamedTuple with one vector for each column. A NamedTuple is returned as it is, and any other table is read with Tables.jl, through the package's extension for it, which loads with Tables.jl, as it does with DataFrames.jl, CSV.jl and the other packages of tables, since they load Tables.jl. Without it, a table that isn't a NamedTuple is an error. Used by MultipleSessionsInfo when it splits a table.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.column_table"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/preparing_models/#Utility-functions-for-preparing-models","page":"Preparing models","text":"What runs outside an inference backend's model: preparing the sessions and the parts of a full model once, before the model is made, and showing the sessions in the REPL.\n\nPages = [\"preparing_models.md\"]","title":"Utility functions for preparing models"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/preparing_models/#Preparing-a-full-model","page":"Preparing models","text":"","title":"Preparing a full model"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/preparing_models/#Preparing-the-data-of-a-session","page":"Preparing models","text":"","title":"Preparing the data of a session"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/preparing_models/#Splitting-a-table-into-sessions","page":"Preparing models","text":"","title":"Splitting a table into sessions"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/preparing_models/#Including-the-environment-in-a-session,-or-leaving-it-out","page":"Preparing models","text":"","title":"Including the environment in a session, or leaving it out"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/preparing_models/#Checking-the-data-identifiers-of-the-sessions","page":"Preparing models","text":"","title":"Checking the data identifiers of the sessions"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/preparing_models/#Reading-the-columns-that-a-parameter-model-uses","page":"Preparing models","text":"","title":"Reading the columns that a parameter model uses"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/preparing_models/#Displaying-sessions","page":"Preparing models","text":"","title":"Displaying sessions"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/preparing_models/#CognitiveModeling.check_fixed_parameter_names-Tuple{NamedTuple, CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel, String}","page":"Preparing models","text":"check_fixed_parameter_names(fixed_parameters, model, keyword)\n\nThrow an informative error if fixed_parameters, the values given with the keyword argument keyword, name a parameter that isn't in the attributes of model, a cognitive model or an environment model, from attribute_names, or if they are given for sessions without an environment, where model is NoEnvironment(). Used by with_fixed_parameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_fixed_parameter_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/preparing_models/#CognitiveModeling.check_given_parameters-Tuple{AbstractParametersModel, CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel, Bool}","page":"Preparing models","text":"check_given_parameters(parameters_model, model, model_runs)\n\nThrow an informative error if parameters_model, a parameter model, gives a parameter that isn't in the attributes of model, a cognitive model or an environment model, from attribute_names, or, when model_runs is true, doesn't give one in its attributes that has no default value in default_parameters. For sessions without an environment, model is NoEnvironment(), and any parameter given is an error. Nothing is checked when parameter_names doesn't know the parameters. Used by prepare_full_model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_given_parameters"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/preparing_models/#CognitiveModeling.check_is_parameters_model-Tuple{AbstractParametersModel}","page":"Preparing models","text":"check_is_parameters_model(parameters_model)\ncheck_is_downstream_model(downstream_model)\n\nThrow an informative error if parameters_model isn't a subtype of AbstractParametersModel, such as the priors on their own, or if downstream_model isn't a subtype of AbstractDownstreamModel. Used by prepare_full_model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_is_parameters_model"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/preparing_models/#CognitiveModeling.with_fixed_parameters-Tuple{Any, NamedTuple, Any, String}","page":"Preparing models","text":"with_fixed_parameters(parameters_model, fixed_parameters, model, keyword)\n\nReturn parameters_model, a parameter model, combined with the values in fixed_parameters, which every session gets for the parameters they name: a CompositeParameters of its parts, which keep their names, and a FixedParameters without a name, or parameters_model itself when no values are fixed. It is an error when a fixed parameter isn't in the attributes of model, and when parameters_model gives it too, which is checked here when parameter_names knows its parameters, and when the full model runs otherwise. keyword is the name of the keyword argument, for the errors. Used by prepare_full_model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.with_fixed_parameters"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/preparing_models/#CognitiveModeling.check_data_fits-Tuple{CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel, NamedTuple, NamedTuple}","page":"Preparing models","text":"check_data_fits(cognitive_model, observations, actions)\n\nThrow an informative error if the data of a session isn't a NamedTuple of vectors for the observations and one for the actions, doesn't have the observations and the actions in the cognitive model's attributes, from attribute_names, or has columns of different lengths, and return the number of timesteps otherwise. Used by SessionInfo.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_data_fits"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/preparing_models/#CognitiveModeling.check_environment_fits-Tuple{CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel, NoEnvironment, NamedTuple, Any, Any}","page":"Preparing models","text":"check_environment_fits(cognitive_model, environment, observations)\n\nThrow an informative error if a session can't run with environment: without one, the observations in the data can't have a MissingValue, since nothing imputes it, and with one, its attributes must have the same observations and actions as those of cognitive_model. Used by SessionInfo.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_environment_fits"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/preparing_models/#CognitiveModeling.collect_values_to_condition_on-Union{Tuple{NamedTuple{names}}, Tuple{names}} where names","page":"Preparing models","text":"collect_values_to_condition_on(columns)\n\nReturn the values to condition on of each action or observation in columns, a NamedTuple with one vector per name, as a NamedTuple of vectors in the order of the timesteps. Fitting scores these against the distributions the model gave on the same timesteps: the actions against the cognitive model's, and the observations against the environment's. Each vector has the element type of its column without MissingValue and the other markers, so that a session without values to condition on for a name gets an empty vector of the same type as other sessions, which scores nothing. A name whose column can only hold those markers is left out, which is decided by its type, so that it is the same for every session of a table. Used by SessionInfo, which keeps the results in its fields actions_to_condition_on and observations_to_condition_on.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.collect_values_to_condition_on"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/preparing_models/#CognitiveModeling.column_can_hold-Tuple{AbstractVector, Any}","page":"Preparing models","text":"column_can_hold(column, value)\n\nReturn whether column can hold value, by its element type, or holds a value which is equal to it without having its type, such as -1.0 for -1, as isequal compares them. Used by mark_missing_values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.column_can_hold"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/preparing_models/#CognitiveModeling.first_timestep_to_check_stopping-Tuple{Nothing, NamedTuple, NamedTuple, Any}","page":"Preparing models","text":"first_timestep_to_check_stopping(check_stopping_criterion, observations, actions,\n                                 n_timesteps)\n\nReturn the first timestep before which a session checks the stopping_criterion of its models, from the check_stopping_criterion of SessionInfo: the timestep after the last one with data when it is nothing, which is 1 when every value is MissingValue, 1 when it is true, and n_timesteps + 1 when it is false, so that no timestep is checked. Used by SessionInfo.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.first_timestep_to_check_stopping"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/preparing_models/#CognitiveModeling.is_missing_data_value-Tuple{Any, Tuple}","page":"Preparing models","text":"is_missing_data_value(value, missing_values)\n\nReturn whether value is missing in the data: MissingValue itself, or equal to one of missing_values, as isequal compares them. Used by build_column, for a value which is built from several columns.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.is_missing_data_value"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/preparing_models/#CognitiveModeling.map_to_environment_outputs-Tuple{Any, NoEnvironment, Any, Any}","page":"Preparing models","text":"map_to_environment_outputs(values, environment, observation_map, value_if_not_passed_on)\n\nReturn values, a NamedTuple in the names under which the cognitive model receives the observations, as environment gives them: under each output name in the environment's attributes, the value that observation_map passes on from it, or value_if_not_passed_on for an output that the map doesn't pass on. Without a map the names are the same. Without an environment values is returned as it is. Used by SessionInfo for the environment's data, with a column of MissingValue for each output that isn't passed on, so that it is imputed, and for the observation the environment starts from, with BeforeFirstTimestep.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.map_to_environment_outputs"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/preparing_models/#CognitiveModeling.mark_missing_values-Tuple{NamedTuple, Tuple, Bool}","page":"Preparing models","text":"mark_missing_values(columns, missing_values, impute)\n\nReturn columns, a NamedTuple with one vector per name, or a single vector, with every value which is equal to one of missing_values, as isequal compares them, replaced by the marker for a missing value: MissingValue when impute is true. When it is false, the marker is UnimputedMissingValue, which every MissingValue() that is in the data already becomes too.\n\nA column gets the type of the marker in its element type when its element type allows one of the values, whether it holds one or not, so that sessions whose columns have the same types keep them, or when it holds a value equal to one of them of another type, such as -1.0 for -1. Any other column is kept as it is, after narrow_element_type. Anything that isn't a NamedTuple of vectors is returned as it is, for check_data_fits to report. Used by SessionInfo on the observations and the actions, and by build_column.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.mark_missing_values"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/preparing_models/#CognitiveModeling.missing_value_marker-Tuple{Bool}","page":"Preparing models","text":"missing_value_marker(impute)\n\nReturn the marker which the missing values in the data of a session become: MissingValue when they are imputed, and UnimputedMissingValue when impute is false. Used by mark_missing_values and build_column.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.missing_value_marker"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/preparing_models/#CognitiveModeling.narrow_element_type-Tuple{AbstractVector}","page":"Preparing models","text":"narrow_element_type(column)\n\nReturn column with the types it holds as its element type, when its element type is Any or another abstract type that isn't a Union, and column itself otherwise. A vector written as [true, MissingValue(), true] has the element type Any, since only missing is promoted to a Union, and an Any in the data makes every timestep run with its types unknown. Used by mark_missing_values and build_column.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.narrow_element_type"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/preparing_models/#CognitiveModeling.value_passed_on-Tuple{Any, Any, Nothing, Any}","page":"Preparing models","text":"value_passed_on(values, name, observation_map, value_if_not_passed_on)\n\nReturn the value in values that the environment's output name is received as: under the same name without a map, under the name observation_map gives it, or value_if_not_passed_on when the map doesn't pass it on. Used by map_to_environment_outputs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.value_passed_on"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/preparing_models/#CognitiveModeling.build_column-Tuple{NamedTuple, Symbol, Any, Any}","page":"Preparing models","text":"build_column(table, specification, missing_values, impute)\n\nReturn the column of an attribute for every row of table, as columns_used describes the specification, with the values that are missing replaced by their marker, as mark_missing_values does: the column itself, or one built row by row from several, where a missing value in any of them makes the whole value of the row missing. The column is made for the whole table, so that every session gets the same element type. Used by build_columns.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.build_column"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/preparing_models/#CognitiveModeling.build_columns-Tuple{NamedTuple, NamedTuple, Any, Any}","page":"Preparing models","text":"build_columns(table, columns, missing_values, impute)\n\nReturn the columns in columns, a NamedTuple from the name the model uses to the specification of the column in table, for every row of the table, as a NamedTuple with the model's names, each built with build_column. Used by MultipleSessionsInfo when it splits a table.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.build_columns"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/preparing_models/#CognitiveModeling.check_table-Tuple{NamedTuple, Tuple{Vararg{Symbol}}}","page":"Preparing models","text":"check_table(table, columns)\n\nThrow an informative error if table, a NamedTuple, doesn't have vectors of one length, or doesn't have every column in columns. Used by MultipleSessionsInfo when it splits a table.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_table"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/preparing_models/#CognitiveModeling.column_entry-Tuple{Symbol}","page":"Preparing models","text":"column_entry(entry)\n\nReturn the name the model uses for an attribute and the specification of its column, as a pair, from one entry of the columns of a kind. An entry is a name, for a column that is named as the model names the attribute, or a pair from the specification of the column to the model's name: :outcome => :reward for a column with another name, (:choicecol, :rtcol) => :ssm_action or (; choice = :choicecol, rt = :rtcol) => :ssm_action for a value built from several columns, and (:choicecol, :rtcol) => combine => :ssm_action for what a function gives for their values. Used by column_map.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.column_entry"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/preparing_models/#CognitiveModeling.column_map-Tuple{Union{Tuple, AbstractVector}, Any, CognitiveModeling.AbstractObservableAttributeKind}","page":"Preparing models","text":"column_map(columns, cognitive_model, kind)\n\nReturn the columns of kind, an AbstractObservableAttributeKind, as a NamedTuple from the name the model uses to the specification of the column, from what observation_columns or action_columns of MultipleSessionsInfo was given:\n\na tuple or a vector with an entry for each attribute, see column_entry: a name, for a column that is named as the model names the attribute, or a pair from the specification of the column to the model's name, such as :outcome => :reward;\none such pair on its own;\na single name, which is the column of the attribute of that kind when the model has only one in its attributes, whatever the column is called. It is an error when the model has several, and when the model's attributes have the name as one of the other kind only, since observation_columns and action_columns may then be swapped. When its attributes have none of that kind, it is a column of that name;\nhow to build a value from several columns, without the model's name: a NamedTuple of names, or a pair of a tuple of names and a function, for the attribute of that kind when the model has only one in its attributes.\n\nA specification is the name of a column, or how to build one from several, see columns_used. Used by MultipleSessionsInfo when it splits a table.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.column_map"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/preparing_models/#CognitiveModeling.columns_used-Tuple{Symbol}","page":"Preparing models","text":"columns_used(specification)\n\nReturn the names of the columns of a table that one specification of an attribute's column uses, as a tuple. A specification is one of:\n\na Symbol, the column itself;\na NamedTuple of Symbols, such as (; choice = :choicecol, rt = :rtcol), for a column of NamedTuples with those names built from the columns, one for each row;\na Tuple of Symbols, such as (:choicecol, :rtcol), for a column of Tuples;\na Pair of a Tuple of Symbols and a function, such as (:choicecol, :rtcol) => (choice, rt) -> [choice, rt], for a column with what the function gives for the values of each row.\n\nUsed by MultipleSessionsInfo when it checks a table, and by build_column.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.columns_used"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/preparing_models/#CognitiveModeling.data_names-Tuple{Any, CognitiveModeling.ObservationKind}","page":"Preparing models","text":"data_names(cognitive_model, kind)\n\nReturn the names in the attributes of cognitive_model, from attribute_names, for the data of kind, an AbstractObservableAttributeKind: its inputs for the observations and its outputs for the actions. Used by column_map and named_data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.data_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/preparing_models/#CognitiveModeling.named_data-Tuple{Any, Any, CognitiveModeling.AbstractObservableAttributeKind}","page":"Preparing models","text":"named_data(data, cognitive_model, kind)\n\nReturn the data of kind, an AbstractObservableAttributeKind, as a NamedTuple of vectors: a single vector as the data of the attribute of that kind when the model has only one in its attributes, which is an error when it has another number of them, and anything else as it is, for check_data_fits to check. A vector of NamedTuples with the model's names is the data written as one row for each timestep, which is returned as it is, for check_data_fits to report. Used by SessionInfo.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.named_data"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/preparing_models/#CognitiveModeling.names_its_attribute-Tuple{Pair}","page":"Preparing models","text":"names_its_attribute(column)\n\nReturn whether column, a pair given as the columns of a kind, ends in the name the model uses for the attribute, as in :outcome => :reward and (:choicecol, :rtcol) => combine => :ssm_action, and not in a function, as in (:choicecol, :rtcol) => combine. Used by column_map.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.names_its_attribute"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/preparing_models/#CognitiveModeling.only_attribute_name-Tuple{Any, CognitiveModeling.AbstractObservableAttributeKind, Any}","page":"Preparing models","text":"only_attribute_name(cognitive_model, kind, columns)\n\nReturn the name of the attribute of kind, an AbstractObservableAttributeKind, when the model has only one in its attributes, for columns, which build its value from several columns without naming it. It is an error when the model has another number of them, since the value then needs the model's name. Used by column_map.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.only_attribute_name"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/preparing_models/#CognitiveModeling.other_kind-Tuple{CognitiveModeling.ObservationKind}","page":"Preparing models","text":"other_kind(kind)\ndescribe_kind(kind)\ncolumns_keyword(kind)\n\nReturn what the messages about the data of kind, an AbstractObservableAttributeKind, need: the other of the two kinds, how the kind is named in a message, \"observations\" or \"actions\", and the keyword argument of MultipleSessionsInfo that gives its columns, \"observation_columns\" or \"action_columns\". Used by column_map, only_attribute_name and named_data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.other_kind"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/preparing_models/#CognitiveModeling.rows_per_session-Tuple{NamedTuple, Tuple{Vararg{Symbol}}}","page":"Preparing models","text":"rows_per_session(table, id_columns)\n\nReturn a vector with one pair for each session in table: its data identifier, and the indices of its rows. A session is every row with one combination of the values in id_columns, in the order of the first row of each. The data identifier is the value itself for a single column, and a NamedTuple of the values for several. Used by MultipleSessionsInfo when it splits a table.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.rows_per_session"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/preparing_models/#CognitiveModeling.select_rows-Tuple{NamedTuple, Any}","page":"Preparing models","text":"select_rows(columns, rows)\n\nReturn the rows rows of each column in columns, a NamedTuple of vectors, which are the data of one session. Used by MultipleSessionsInfo when it splits a table.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.select_rows"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/preparing_models/#CognitiveModeling.select_timestep_sizes-Tuple{NamedTuple, Symbol, Any}","page":"Preparing models","text":"select_timestep_sizes(table, timestep_sizes, rows)\n\nReturn the sizes of the timesteps of one session: the column named timestep_sizes of table at the rows rows, or timestep_sizes itself when it is a size for all. Used by MultipleSessionsInfo when it splits a table.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.select_timestep_sizes"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/preparing_models/#CognitiveModeling.session_id-Tuple{NamedTuple, Tuple{Symbol}, Any}","page":"Preparing models","text":"session_id(table, id_columns, i)\n\nReturn the data identifier of the session that row i of table belongs to. Used by rows_per_session.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.session_id"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/preparing_models/#CognitiveModeling.decide_whether_environment_is_included-Tuple{Union{Nothing, Bool}, Any, Any, Any, SaveHistory, Any}","page":"Preparing models","text":"decide_whether_environment_is_included(include_environment, environment, observations,\n                                       actions, environment_save_history,\n                                       check_stopping_criterion)\n\nReturn whether a session includes its environment, as the include_environment of SessionInfo says: always with true, which needs an environment, never with false, and with nothing only when an observation or an action is MissingValue, the environment's history is saved, or its stopping criterion is checked on every timestep. A session without an environment never includes one. With false, it is an error when an observation is MissingValue(), the environment's history is saved, or its stopping criterion is checked on every timestep, since those need the environment. Used by prepare_session.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.decide_whether_environment_is_included"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/preparing_models/#CognitiveModeling.has_missing_entries-Tuple{NamedTuple}","page":"Preparing models","text":"has_missing_entries(columns)\n\nReturn whether any entry of columns, the observations or the actions in the data of a session with one vector for each name, is MissingValue. Used by SessionInfo, to decide whether the environment runs, and by check_environment_fits.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.has_missing_entries"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/preparing_models/#CognitiveModeling.include_environments_if_estimated-Tuple{Any, MultipleSessionsInfo}","page":"Preparing models","text":"include_environments_if_estimated(environment_parameters_model, sessions_info)\n\nReturn the sessions as a full model runs them with environment_parameters_model: as they are when it estimates nothing, and otherwise with every session that has an environment including it, so that the observations in the data inform the parameters. Used by prepare_full_model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.include_environments_if_estimated"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/preparing_models/#CognitiveModeling.remake_session_including_environment-Tuple{SessionInfo}","page":"Preparing models","text":"remake_session_including_environment(session_info)\n\nReturn the session including its environment when it has one, and everything else the same. A session that was prepared with include_environment = false is an error. Used by include_environments_if_estimated.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.remake_session_including_environment"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/preparing_models/#CognitiveModeling.remake_session_without_environment-Tuple{SessionInfo}","page":"Preparing models","text":"remake_session_without_environment(session_info)\n\nReturn the session with NoEnvironment() as its environment and everything else the same, so that it runs as a session without an environment. Used by fitted sessions whose environment doesn't run, since every observation is in the data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.remake_session_without_environment"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/preparing_models/#CognitiveModeling.check_unique_session_symbol_names-Tuple{AbstractVector}","page":"Preparing models","text":"check_unique_session_symbol_names(session_ids)\n\nThrow an informative error if the data identifiers of two sessions give the same symbol name, as session_id_to_symbol gives it: when they are the same data identifier, or give the same text, such as 1 and \"1\". Used by MultipleSessionsInfo.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_unique_session_symbol_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/preparing_models/#CognitiveModeling.group_sessions-Tuple{MultipleSessionsInfo, Tuple{}, String}","page":"Preparing models","text":"group_sessions(sessions_info, group_by, reader)\n\nReturn the groups of the sessions in sessions_info by the values of the columns in group_by, a tuple of names: a NamedTuple with the group of every session, group_of_each_session, the number of groups, n_groups, and the symbol name of each group, group_symbol_names, made from its values as session_id_to_symbol makes the symbol name of a session from its data identifier. Groups are numbered in the order of their first session, and with no columns every session is in one group. A session with more than one value, or a missing value, in one of the columns is an error, which starts with reader, as for read_level_of_each_session. Used by prepare_parameters_model, for the groups of a HierarchicalGroupedParameters and for the varying effects of a HierarchicalRegressionParameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.group_sessions"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/preparing_models/#CognitiveModeling.read_level_of_each_session-Tuple{MultipleSessionsInfo, Tuple{Vararg{Symbol}}, String}","page":"Preparing models","text":"read_level_of_each_session(sessions_info, columns, reader)\n\nReturn the level of every session of sessions_info in the columns in columns, a tuple of names, as a vector in the order of the sessions: its value of a single column, or a NamedTuple of its values of several. Each value is read with read_one_value_of_each_session, whose errors start with reader. Used by group_sessions and by the categorical predictors of a HierarchicalRegressionParameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.read_level_of_each_session"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/preparing_models/#CognitiveModeling.read_one_value_of_each_session-Tuple{MultipleSessionsInfo, Symbol, String}","page":"Preparing models","text":"read_one_value_of_each_session(sessions_info, column, reader)\n\nReturn the value of column in every session of sessions_info, as a vector in the order of the sessions, from the table that the sessions were split from. A session with more than one value in the column, or with a missing one, is an error, which starts with reader, a description of what reads the column, such as \"HierarchicalGroupedParameters groups learning_rate by\". Used by read_level_of_each_session and by the continuous predictors of a HierarchicalRegressionParameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.read_one_value_of_each_session"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/preparing_models/#CognitiveModeling.print_data_names-Tuple{IO, Any, Any}","page":"Preparing models","text":"print_data_names(io, kind, names)\n\nPrint a line with kind and names, the names of one kind of value in the data, or none when there are none. Used by the show methods of a SessionInfo and a MultipleSessionsInfo.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.print_data_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/preparing_models/#CognitiveModeling.print_model_name-Tuple{IO, CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel}","page":"Preparing models","text":"print_model_name(io, model)\n\nPrint the name of the type of model, or none for NoEnvironment. Used by the show methods of a SessionInfo and a MultipleSessionsInfo.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.print_model_name"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/interfacing_with_pomdps/#Models-from-POMDPs.jl-as-cognitive-models","page":"Models from POMDPs.jl as cognitive models","text":"POMDPs.jl describes a task as a problem object, and has solvers which give a policy for it, and updaters which keep a belief about the hidden state. A cognitive model can keep such a belief as its state, update it with their updater, and choose from the values their policy gives each action.\n\nThis page shows such a cognitive model. It doesn't simulate or fit it yet, and its code isn't run when the documentation is built, to keep the documentation's environment small. The package's tests run it instead, with POMDPs.jl 1.0, POMDPTools.jl 1.1 and QMDP.jl 0.1.\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\nusing Distributions: DiscreteNonParametric\nusing LogExpFunctions: softmax\nusing POMDPs: POMDPs\nusing POMDPTools: DiscreteUpdater, actionvalues\nusing QMDP: QMDPSolver","title":"Models from POMDPs.jl as cognitive models"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/interfacing_with_pomdps/#A-Bayesian-observer-with-a-solved-policy","page":"Models from POMDPs.jl as cognitive models","text":"The cognitive model holds the problem, a policy for it and a belief updater. The problem is solved once, when the cognitive model is created, so nothing is solved on a timestep.\n\nstruct BayesianObserver{Tpomdp,Tpomdp_policy,Tpomdp_updater} <: AbstractCognitiveModel\n    pomdp::Tpomdp\n    pomdp_policy::Tpomdp_policy\n    pomdp_updater::Tpomdp_updater\nend\n\nfunction BayesianObserver(pomdp)\n    return BayesianObserver(\n        pomdp, POMDPs.solve(QMDPSolver(), pomdp), DiscreteUpdater(pomdp)\n    )\nend\n\nIts state is the belief, which starts as the problem's distribution over initial states.\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(model::BayesianObserver, parameters)\n    return (;\n        belief=POMDPs.initialize_belief(\n            model.pomdp_updater, POMDPs.initialstate(model.pomdp)\n        )\n    )\nend\n\nOn each timestep, the belief is updated from the previous action and the observation that followed it, and the action probabilities are a softmax over the values the policy gives each action under that belief, with the softmax from LogExpFunctions.jl. On the first timestep there is no previous action, which AbstractNoValue covers, and the belief is left as it is.\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.run_timestep(model::BayesianObserver, context::ModelContext)\n\n    # Unpack the context ##\n    belief = context.previous_state.belief\n    previous_choice = context.previous_output.choice\n    β = context.parameters.inverse_temperature\n\n    # Update state ##\n    #Update the belief from the previous action and what was observed after it\n    if !(previous_choice isa AbstractNoValue)\n        belief = POMDPs.update(\n            model.pomdp_updater, belief, previous_choice, context.input.observation\n        )\n    end\n\n    # Compute action probabilities ##\n    #The value the policy gives each action under the belief\n    Qₜ = actionvalues(model.pomdp_policy, belief)\n    #Softmax over the action values, as a distribution over the problem's own actions\n    Pₜ = DiscreteNonParametric(collect(POMDPs.actions(model.pomdp)), softmax(β .* Qₜ))\n\n    return ModelOutput((; choice=Pₜ), (; belief))\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::BayesianObserver)\n    return ModelAttributes(;\n        parameters=:inverse_temperature,\n        states=:belief,\n        inputs=:observation,\n        outputs=:choice,\n    )\nend\n\nThe updater returns a new belief on each timestep, so the saved states need no copying.\n\nA problem from POMDPModels.jl gives a cognitive model for its task, such as BayesianObserver(TigerPOMDP()). The problem can be used as the environment too, through CommonRLEnvironment, as in CommonRLEnvironment(POMDPCommonRLEnv{Any}(TigerPOMDP()); action_name = :choice). The {Any} makes POMDPTools.jl give the observations as they are, which the updater needs. Without it, as with convert, each observation is put in an array, and the updater throws an error. That environment also gives reward and terminated, which aren't in this cognitive model's attributes, so a NameMap must select the observation for it: observation_map = NameMap(:observation).\n\nThe learners in TabularTDLearning.jl can't be used this way. They are solvers which run every episode themselves, so there is no single timestep to call.","title":"A Bayesian observer with a solved policy"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/interfacing_with_pomdps/#Fitting","page":"Models from POMDPs.jl as cognitive models","text":"This page doesn't fit the cognitive model yet. When it is fitted with a sampler that uses gradients, use Enzyme.jl in reverse mode with runtime activity as the automatic differentiation backend, which for Turing.jl is AutoEnzyme(; mode = set_runtime_activity(Reverse), function_annotation = Const). It gave the right gradient of the log density of a session for this cognitive model, when checked against finite differences with Enzyme.jl 0.13. So did ForwardDiff.jl and ReverseDiff.jl, while Mooncake.jl and Enzyme's forward mode threw an error.\n\nUse the softmax from LogExpFunctions.jl, as above, and not one written by hand. Written in one line, a softmax got a wrong gradient for this cognitive model from Enzyme's reverse mode, with no error, which the page Automatic differentiation and Reactant describes under its pitfalls.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Fitting"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/interfacing_with_juliacontrol/#Models-from-control-theory-as-cognitive-models","page":"Models from control theory as cognitive models","text":"Control theory describes how a system is steered towards a goal from what is observed of it, which also makes it a model of behavior, such as of tracking a moving target with a hand. The packages of the JuliaControl organization give the parts: DiscretePIDs.jl a PID controller, LowLevelParticleFilters.jl Kalman filters and particle filters, which can model perception, and ControlSystemsBase.jl linear systems, which can model what is controlled.\n\nThis page shows a PID controller as a cognitive model, which controls a simulated plant as its environment, and fits its gains with Turing.jl. It then shows a Kalman filter as the perception of a cognitive model, and ends with how a model with a controller of ModelPredictiveControl.jl is fitted. Its code isn't run when the documentation is built, to keep the documentation's environment small. The package's tests run it instead, with DiscretePIDs.jl 1.0, ControlSystemsBase.jl 1.25 and LowLevelParticleFilters.jl 3.38.\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\nusing ControlSystemsBase: ss, c2d\nusing DiscretePIDs: DiscretePID\nusing Distributions: Normal, LogNormal\nusing LowLevelParticleFilters: KalmanFilter, SimpleMvNormal, correct!, predict!\nusing Random: Xoshiro\nusing Turing","title":"Models from control theory as cognitive models"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/interfacing_with_juliacontrol/#The-plant-as-the-environment","page":"Models from control theory as cognitive models","text":"The environment is what the agent controls: a first-order lag from a force to a position, made with ControlSystemsBase.jl as a system in continuous time and discretized with the size of a timestep. Its state is the plant's state and the number of the timestep. On each timestep it applies the agent's force, and gives the target, which moves along two sines, and the position, observed with noise.\n\nstruct FirstOrderPlant{Tsystem} <: AbstractEnvironmentModel\n    system::Tsystem\nend\n\nfunction FirstOrderPlant(; time_constant=0.5, timestep_size=0.1)\n    return FirstOrderPlant(\n        c2d(ss(-1 / time_constant, 1 / time_constant, 1, 0), timestep_size)\n    )\nend\n\nfunction target_at(timestep, timestep_size)\n    return sin(0.5 * timestep * timestep_size) + 0.5 * sin(1.3 * timestep * timestep_size)\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(environment::FirstOrderPlant, parameters)\n    return (; plant_state=zeros(environment.system.nx), timestep=0)\nend\n\nOn the first timestep the agent hasn't acted yet, which AbstractNoValue covers, and no force is applied.\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.run_timestep(environment::FirstOrderPlant, context::ModelContext)\n\n    # Unpack the context ##\n    (; plant_state, timestep) = context.previous_state\n    force = context.input.force isa AbstractNoValue ? 0.0 : context.input.force\n    (; A, B, C) = environment.system\n\n    # Update state ##\n    #The plant's state after the force has acted for one timestep\n    xₜ = A * plant_state + B * [force]\n\n    # Compute observations ##\n    #The position, observed with noise, and the target of the next timestep\n    position = only(C * xₜ)\n    target = target_at(timestep + 1, environment.system.Ts)\n\n    return ModelOutput(\n        (;\n            target=Deterministic(target),\n            position=Normal(position, context.parameters.observation_noise),\n        ),\n        (; plant_state=xₜ, timestep=timestep + 1),\n    )\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(environment::FirstOrderPlant)\n    return ModelAttributes(;\n        parameters=:observation_noise,\n        states=(:plant_state, :timestep),\n        inputs=:force,\n        outputs=(:target, :position),\n    )\nend","title":"The plant as the environment"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/interfacing_with_juliacontrol/#A-PID-controller-as-the-agent","page":"Models from control theory as cognitive models","text":"The agent sees the target and the position, and pushes with the force its PID controller gives, with motor noise. The controller's gains are the agent's parameters: the proportional gain K, the integral time Ti and the derivative time Td. Its state is the controller of DiscretePIDs.jl, made from the parameters when the state is initialized, so that its numbers are of the type the parameters have when a model is fitted.\n\nstruct PIDController <: AbstractCognitiveModel\n    timestep_size::Float64\nend\n\nThe limits of the force are given, since the controller's default limits need a number type that ReverseDiff.jl's tracked numbers don't have.\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(model::PIDController, parameters)\n    (; K, Ti, Td) = parameters\n    return (; pid=DiscretePID(; K, Ti, Td, Ts=model.timestep_size, umin=(-Inf), umax=Inf))\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.run_timestep(model::PIDController, context::ModelContext)\n\n    # Unpack the context ##\n    pid = context.previous_state.pid\n    σ = context.parameters.motor_noise\n\n    # Update state and compute the action ##\n    #The controller's force for the target and the position, which updates the integral and the derivative it keeps\n    uₜ = pid(context.input.target, context.input.position)\n\n    return ModelOutput((; force=Normal(uₜ, σ)), (; pid))\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::PIDController)\n    return ModelAttributes(;\n        parameters=(:K, :Ti, :Td, :motor_noise),\n        states=:pid,\n        inputs=(:target, :position),\n        outputs=:force,\n    )\nend\n\nThe controller is changed in place on each timestep, so each state that is saved must be a copy.\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.prepare_state_for_saving(model::PIDController, state)\n    return (; pid=deepcopy(state.pid))\nend\n\nThe controller could also be kept as its three numbers, its integral, its derivative and its previous error, and made again on each timestep from them and the parameters, with DiscretePID(; K, Ti, Td, Ts, I, D, yold). Then nothing is changed in place, and nothing needs copying.","title":"A PID controller as the agent"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/interfacing_with_juliacontrol/#Simulating","page":"Models from control theory as cognitive models","text":"A session of the agent controlling the plant, with the observations and the actions saved:\n\ntimestep_size = 0.1\nsimulation = simulate(\n    PIDController(timestep_size),\n    FirstOrderPlant(; timestep_size);\n    who_starts=EnvironmentStarts(),\n    n_steps=150,\n    cognitive_parameters=(; K=2.0, Ti=1.0, Td=0.1, motor_noise=0.2),\n    environment_parameters=(; observation_noise=0.05),\n    cognitive_save_history=SaveHistory(;\n        inputs=(:target, :position), outputs=:force, save_initial_timestep_values=false\n    ),\n    rng=Xoshiro(1),\n)","title":"Simulating"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/interfacing_with_juliacontrol/#Fitting","page":"Models from control theory as cognitive models","text":"Three sessions with different gains, simulated the same way, make a table with one row for each timestep:\n\ntrue_parameters = [\n    (; K=2.0, Ti=1.0, Td=0.1, motor_noise=0.2),\n    (; K=1.0, Ti=0.5, Td=0.05, motor_noise=0.2),\n    (; K=1.5, Ti=2.0, Td=0.05, motor_noise=0.2),\n]\nsimulations = map(enumerate(true_parameters)) do (session, parameters)\n    return simulate(\n        PIDController(timestep_size),\n        FirstOrderPlant(; timestep_size);\n        who_starts=EnvironmentStarts(),\n        n_steps=150,\n        cognitive_parameters=parameters,\n        environment_parameters=(; observation_noise=0.05),\n        cognitive_save_history=SaveHistory(;\n            inputs=(:target, :position), outputs=:force, save_initial_timestep_values=false\n        ),\n        rng=Xoshiro(20 + session),\n    )\nend\ntable = (;\n    id=repeat([\"S1\", \"S2\", \"S3\"]; inner=150),\n    target=[observation.target for s in simulations for observation in s.observations],\n    position=[observation.position for s in simulations for observation in s.observations],\n    force=[action.force for s in simulations for action in s.actions],\n)\n\nThe gains of each session are fitted with a prior of their own, and NUTS samples them:\n\nsessions_info = MultipleSessionsInfo(\n    PIDController(timestep_size),\n    table;\n    observation_columns=(:target, :position),\n    action_columns=:force,\n    session_columns=:id,\n    who_starts=EnvironmentStarts(),\n)\nfull_model = turing_full_model(\n    IndependentParameters((;\n        K=LogNormal(0, 1),\n        Ti=LogNormal(0, 1),\n        Td=LogNormal(-2, 1),\n        motor_noise=LogNormal(-1, 1),\n    )),\n    sessions_info,\n)\nchain = sample(Xoshiro(11), full_model, NUTS(300, 0.65), 300; progress=false)\n\nThe chain has the gains of each session under K.session, Ti.session and Td.session.\n\nNUTS needs the gradient of the log density. ForwardDiff.jl, which is Turing.jl's default, ReverseDiff.jl and Mooncake.jl 0.5 gave the right gradient for this model, when checked against finite differences. Enzyme.jl 0.13 failed: with runtime activity it stopped Julia with an error from its type analysis, and without it, it threw an error for the controller that is changed in place.","title":"Fitting"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/interfacing_with_juliacontrol/#A-Kalman-filter-as-perception","page":"Models from control theory as cognitive models","text":"A cognitive model can also perceive with a filter. In this task a target drifts as a random walk, the agent sees it only through noisy cues, and it reports where it believes the target is. The agent's belief is a Kalman filter of LowLevelParticleFilters.jl, which assumes a random walk with the agent's own drift and cue noise, its parameters.\n\nstruct DriftingTarget <: AbstractEnvironmentModel end\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(environment::DriftingTarget, parameters)\n    return (; target_position=0.0)\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.run_timestep(environment::DriftingTarget, context::ModelContext)\n    #The target drifts, which is sampled with the generator of the context\n    target_position =\n        context.previous_state.target_position +\n        context.parameters.drift * randn(context.rng)\n    return ModelOutput(\n        (; cue=Normal(target_position, context.parameters.cue_noise)), (; target_position)\n    )\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(environment::DriftingTarget)\n    return ModelAttributes(;\n        parameters=(:drift, :cue_noise),\n        states=:target_position,\n        inputs=:report,\n        outputs=:cue,\n    )\nend\n\nThe filter is made from the parameters, with a starting belief of the parameters' number type, since a filter keeps the types it starts with. Its matrices are fixed and already in discrete time, so its check of them, which computes their eigenvalues, is turned off.\n\nstruct KalmanTracker <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(model::KalmanTracker, parameters)\n    (; assumed_drift, assumed_cue_noise) = parameters\n    T = typeof(assumed_drift * assumed_cue_noise)\n    starting_belief = SimpleMvNormal(zeros(T, 1), ones(T, 1, 1))\n    kalman_filter = KalmanFilter(\n        [1.0;;],\n        [0.0;;],\n        [1.0;;],\n        [0.0;;],\n        fill(assumed_drift^2, 1, 1),\n        fill(assumed_cue_noise^2, 1, 1),\n        starting_belief;\n        check=false,\n    )\n    return (; kalman_filter)\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.run_timestep(model::KalmanTracker, context::ModelContext)\n\n    # Unpack the context ##\n    kalman_filter = context.previous_state.kalman_filter\n    no_input = zero(kalman_filter.x)\n\n    # Update state ##\n    #Correct the belief with the cue, as a vector of the belief's number type, keep its mean, and predict the next timestep\n    correct!(kalman_filter, no_input, [context.input.cue] .+ zero(kalman_filter.x))\n    believed_position = kalman_filter.x[1]\n    predict!(kalman_filter, no_input)\n\n    # Compute the action ##\n    #The report is where the agent believes the target is, with motor noise\n    return ModelOutput(\n        (; report=Normal(believed_position, context.parameters.motor_noise)),\n        (; kalman_filter),\n    )\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::KalmanTracker)\n    return ModelAttributes(;\n        parameters=(:assumed_drift, :assumed_cue_noise, :motor_noise),\n        states=:kalman_filter,\n        inputs=:cue,\n        outputs=:report,\n    )\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.prepare_state_for_saving(model::KalmanTracker, state)\n    return (; kalman_filter=deepcopy(state.kalman_filter))\nend\n\nA session of the tracker:\n\ntracking = simulate(\n    KalmanTracker(),\n    DriftingTarget();\n    who_starts=EnvironmentStarts(),\n    n_steps=100,\n    cognitive_parameters=(; assumed_drift=0.3, assumed_cue_noise=0.5, motor_noise=0.1),\n    environment_parameters=(; drift=0.3, cue_noise=0.5),\n    cognitive_save_history=SaveHistory(;\n        inputs=:cue, outputs=:report, save_initial_timestep_values=false\n    ),\n    rng=Xoshiro(2),\n)\n\nFor a filter of one dimension, as here, the filter written by hand is faster. The package earns its place for the extended and unscented Kalman filters and the particle filters, which are more work to write.","title":"A Kalman filter as perception"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/interfacing_with_juliacontrol/#Model-predictive-control","page":"Models from control theory as cognitive models","text":"ModelPredictiveControl.jl gives controllers that plan the next actions by optimizing them over a horizon, together with state estimators and plant models. Its controllers run inside a cognitive model in the same way, but their gradients can't be computed yet, so a model with one is fitted with a sampler that needs no gradient, such as a slice sampler from SliceSampling.jl, or Turing.jl's MH with a covariance matrix, for a random walk. Turing.jl computes one gradient when it looks for initial values for such a sampler, so give it an adtype of finite differences, which only evaluates the model with ordinary numbers:\n\nusing FiniteDifferences: central_fdm\nusing SliceSampling: RandPermGibbs, SliceSteppingOut\n\nsampler = externalsampler(\n    RandPermGibbs(SliceSteppingOut(2.0));\n    adtype = AutoFiniteDifferences(; fdm = central_fdm(3, 1)),\n)\n\nEach step of these samplers evaluates the whole model, so they suit a single session, or sessions fitted one at a time, better than a hierarchical model of many sessions. A LinMPC with soft constraints warns about the conditioning of its problem every time it is made, which is on every evaluation of the model. When only the actions are bounded, hard bounds, with Cwt = Inf, give the same controller without the warning.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Model predictive control"},{"category":"section","location":"#CognitiveModeling.jl","page":"Home","text":"A Julia package for computational modeling of cognition and behavior. It is under development.","title":"CognitiveModeling.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/custom_turing_models/#Custom-Turing.jl-models","page":"Custom Turing.jl models","text":"This page is a draft.\n\nturing_full_model fits several sessions with parameter models, which give the parameters of every session in one of the ways the package offers. A Turing.jl model of one's own can give them in any other way, and run the sessions inside it as a submodel: turing_multiple_behavioral_sessions runs several sessions, and turing_behavioral_session runs one. Both return the parameters they were given, with the trajectories the sessions saved. So when a model of one's own returns what they return, session_parameters_chain and session_trajectories_chain read its fit as they read that of a full model, also when the parameters are transformations of the model's variables, which the chain itself doesn't have.\n\nThis page fits a Rescorla-Wagner model to several participants with a hierarchical model written by hand, and then to a single participant.\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\nusing Distributions: Bernoulli, BernoulliLogit, Exponential, LogNormal, Normal\nusing LogExpFunctions: logistic\nusing Random: Xoshiro\nusing Statistics: mean\nusing Turing","title":"Custom Turing.jl models"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/custom_turing_models/#The-model-and-the-data","page":"Custom Turing.jl models","text":"The Rescorla-Wagner model learns the expected reward of an option from the rewards it gets, and chooses the option with a probability that grows with its expected reward:\n\nstruct RescorlaWagner <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(model::RescorlaWagner, parameters)\n    return (; expected_reward=0.5)\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.run_timestep(model::RescorlaWagner, context::ModelContext)\n    #The expected reward before this timestep, and after its reward\n    V₋₁ = context.previous_state.expected_reward\n    Vₜ = V₋₁ + context.parameters.learning_rate * (context.input.reward - V₋₁)\n\n    #The higher the expected reward, the more likely the option is chosen\n    Pₜ = BernoulliLogit(context.parameters.inverse_temperature * (Vₜ - 0.5))\n    return ModelOutput((; choice=Pₜ), (; expected_reward=Vₜ))\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::RescorlaWagner)\n    return ModelAttributes(;\n        parameters=(:learning_rate, :inverse_temperature),\n        states=:expected_reward,\n        inputs=:reward,\n        outputs=:choice,\n    )\nend\n\nThe rewards come from an environment which gives a reward with a fixed probability on every timestep:\n\nstruct BernoulliRewards <: AbstractEnvironmentModel end\n\nCognitiveModeling.initialize_state(environment::BernoulliRewards, parameters) = (;)\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.run_timestep(\n    environment::BernoulliRewards, context::ModelContext\n)\n    return ModelOutput((; reward=Bernoulli(context.parameters.reward_probability)))\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(environment::BernoulliRewards)\n    return ModelAttributes(;\n        parameters=:reward_probability, states=(), inputs=:choice, outputs=:reward\n    )\nend\n\nFive participants, each with a learning rate of their own, are simulated for 50 timesteps, which gives a table with a row for each timestep:\n\nsimulated_learning_rates = [0.2, 0.35, 0.5, 0.65, 0.8]\nsimulations = map(enumerate(simulated_learning_rates)) do (participant, learning_rate)\n    return simulate(\n        RescorlaWagner(),\n        BernoulliRewards();\n        who_starts=EnvironmentStarts(),\n        n_steps=50,\n        cognitive_parameters=(; learning_rate, inverse_temperature=5.0),\n        environment_parameters=(; reward_probability=0.7),\n        cognitive_save_history=SaveHistory(;\n            inputs=:reward, outputs=:choice, save_initial_timestep_values=false\n        ),\n        rng=Xoshiro(participant),\n    )\nend\ntable = (;\n    participant=repeat([\"P1\", \"P2\", \"P3\", \"P4\", \"P5\"]; inner=50),\n    reward=[\n        float(observation.reward) for s in simulations for observation in s.observations\n    ],\n    choice=[action.choice for s in simulations for action in s.actions],\n)\nsessions_info = MultipleSessionsInfo(\n    RescorlaWagner(),\n    table;\n    observation_columns=:reward,\n    action_columns=:choice,\n    session_columns=:participant,\n    who_starts=EnvironmentStarts(),\n)\nMultipleSessionsInfo\nSessions: 5\nCognitive model: RescorlaWagner\nEnvironment: none\nObservations: reward\nActions: choice","title":"The model and the data"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/custom_turing_models/#A-hierarchical-model-of-one's-own","page":"Custom Turing.jl models","text":"The learning rates of the participants come from a population on the logit scale: each participant's is the mean of the population plus a standardized deviation scaled by the population's standard deviation, which logistic then takes to between 0 and 1. The participants share one inverse temperature. The model gives turing_multiple_behavioral_sessions the parameters as a NamedTuple with a vector for each parameter, with a value for every session in the order of sessions_info.session_ids, and returns what it returns:\n\n@model function hierarchical_learning_rates(sessions_info)\n    n_participants = CognitiveModeling.n_sessions(sessions_info)\n\n    #The population of the learning rates, on the logit scale\n    population_mean ~ Normal(0, 1)\n    population_sd ~ Exponential(0.5)\n\n    #The learning rate of each participant, from its standardized deviation from the mean\n    standardized ~ filldist(Normal(), n_participants)\n    learning_rate = logistic.(population_mean .+ population_sd .* standardized)\n\n    #One inverse temperature for every participant\n    inverse_temperature ~ LogNormal(1, 0.5)\n\n    #The sessions, each with its own parameters, as a submodel without a prefix of its own\n    sessions ~ to_submodel(\n        turing_multiple_behavioral_sessions(\n            sessions_info,\n            (;\n                learning_rate, inverse_temperature=fill(inverse_temperature, n_participants)\n            ),\n        ),\n        false,\n    )\n    return sessions\nend\n\nmodel = hierarchical_learning_rates(sessions_info)\nchain = sample(Xoshiro(1), model, NUTS(), 500; progress=false)\n\u001b[90m╭─\u001b[39m\u001b[0m\u001b[1mFlexiChain (500 iterations, 1 chain)\u001b[22m\u001b[90m ────────────────────────────────────────\u001b[39m\u001b[90m╮\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m \u001b[38;5;209m↓ iter  = 251:750\u001b[39m                                                            \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m \u001b[38;5;32m→ chain = 1:1\u001b[39m                                                                \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m                                                                              \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m \u001b[0m\u001b[1mParameters (4)\u001b[22m\u001b[90m ── AbstractPPL.VarName\u001b[39m                                        \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m  \u001b[32mFloat64        \u001b[39m  population_mean, population_sd, inverse_temperature        \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m  \u001b[32mVector{Float64}\u001b[39m  standardized \u001b[37m(5,)\u001b[39m                                          \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m                                                                              \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m \u001b[0m\u001b[1mExtras (14)\u001b[22m                                                                  \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m  \u001b[32mInt64  \u001b[39m  n_steps, tree_depth                                                \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m  \u001b[32mBool   \u001b[39m  is_accept, numerical_error                                         \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m  \u001b[32mFloat64\u001b[39m  acceptance_rate, log_density, hamiltonian_energy                   \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m           hamiltonian_energy_error, max_hamiltonian_energy_error, step_size  \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m           nom_step_size, logprior, loglikelihood, logjoint                   \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯\u001b[39m","title":"A hierarchical model of one's own"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/custom_turing_models/#Reading-the-fit","page":"Custom Turing.jl models","text":"The chain has the variables of the model: the population's mean and standard deviation, the standardized deviations and the inverse temperature, but not the learning rates, which the model computes from them. returned gives what the model returned for each sample, and session_parameters_chain makes a chain of it with the parameters of each session, named after the session, such as P3.learning_rate:\n\nparameters = session_parameters_chain(returned(model, chain), sessions_info)\n\u001b[90m╭─\u001b[39m\u001b[0m\u001b[1mFlexiChain (500 iterations, 1 chain)\u001b[22m\u001b[90m ────────────────────────────────────────\u001b[39m\u001b[90m╮\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m \u001b[38;5;209m↓ iter  = 1:500\u001b[39m                                                              \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m \u001b[38;5;32m→ chain = 1:1\u001b[39m                                                                \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m                                                                              \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m \u001b[0m\u001b[1mParameters (10)\u001b[22m\u001b[90m ── AbstractPPL.VarName\u001b[39m                                       \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m  \u001b[32mFloat64\u001b[39m  P1.learning_rate, P1.inverse_temperature, P2.learning_rate         \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m           P2.inverse_temperature, P3.learning_rate, P3.inverse_temperature   \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m           P4.learning_rate, P4.inverse_temperature, P5.learning_rate         \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m           P5.inverse_temperature                                             \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m                                                                              \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m \u001b[0m\u001b[1mExtras (0)\u001b[22m                                                                   \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m \u001b[90m (none)\u001b[39m                                                                      \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯\u001b[39m\n\nThe mean learning rate of each participant, next to the one they were simulated with:\n\n[\n    (;\n        participant,\n        simulated,\n        fitted=mean(parameters[@varname($participant.learning_rate)]),\n    ) for (participant, simulated) in\n    zip(Symbol.(sessions_info.session_ids), simulated_learning_rates)\n]\n5-element Vector{@NamedTuple{participant::Symbol, simulated::Float64, fitted::Float64}}:\n (participant = :P1, simulated = 0.2, fitted = 0.4892782127488753)\n (participant = :P2, simulated = 0.35, fitted = 0.5024303816407346)\n (participant = :P3, simulated = 0.5, fitted = 0.4937624332274658)\n (participant = :P4, simulated = 0.65, fitted = 0.8295736315933949)\n (participant = :P5, simulated = 0.8, fitted = 0.7549145442761862)\n\nThe trajectories are read the same way. A fit is fastest when the sessions save nothing, and the same model with a copy of the sessions that save the expected rewards has the same variables, so returned runs it on the samples of the chain, and session_trajectories_chain makes a chain of what the sessions saved:\n\nsaving_sessions = MultipleSessionsInfo(\n    sessions_info; cognitive_save_history=SaveHistory(; states=:expected_reward)\n)\ntrajectories = session_trajectories_chain(\n    returned(hierarchical_learning_rates(saving_sessions), chain), sessions_info\n)\n\u001b[90m╭─\u001b[39m\u001b[0m\u001b[1mFlexiChain (500 iterations, 1 chain)\u001b[22m\u001b[90m ────────────────────────────────────────\u001b[39m\u001b[90m╮\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m \u001b[38;5;209m↓ iter  = 1:500\u001b[39m                                                              \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m \u001b[38;5;32m→ chain = 1:1\u001b[39m                                                                \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m                                                                              \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m \u001b[0m\u001b[1mParameters (5)\u001b[22m\u001b[90m ── AbstractPPL.VarName\u001b[39m                                        \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m  \u001b[32mVector{Float64}\u001b[39m  P1.expected_reward \u001b[37m(51,)\u001b[39m, P2.expected_reward \u001b[37m(51,)\u001b[39m         \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m                   P3.expected_reward \u001b[37m(51,)\u001b[39m, P4.expected_reward \u001b[37m(51,)\u001b[39m         \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m                   P5.expected_reward \u001b[37m(51,)\u001b[39m                                   \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m                                                                              \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m \u001b[0m\u001b[1mExtras (0)\u001b[22m                                                                   \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m \u001b[90m (none)\u001b[39m                                                                      \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯\u001b[39m\n\nEach entry holds, for every sample, the expected rewards of a participant over the timesteps, from timestep 0. The two chains have the samples of the fit, and merge into one:\n\nmerge(parameters, trajectories)\n\u001b[90m╭─\u001b[39m\u001b[0m\u001b[1mFlexiChain (500 iterations, 1 chain)\u001b[22m\u001b[90m ────────────────────────────────────────\u001b[39m\u001b[90m╮\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m \u001b[38;5;209m↓ iter  = 1:500\u001b[39m                                                              \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m \u001b[38;5;32m→ chain = 1:1\u001b[39m                                                                \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m                                                                              \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m \u001b[0m\u001b[1mParameters (15)\u001b[22m\u001b[90m ── AbstractPPL.VarName\u001b[39m                                       \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m  \u001b[32mFloat64        \u001b[39m  P1.learning_rate, P1.inverse_temperature, P2.learning_rate \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m                   P2.inverse_temperature, P3.learning_rate                   \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m                   P3.inverse_temperature, P4.learning_rate                   \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m                   P4.inverse_temperature, P5.learning_rate                   \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m                   P5.inverse_temperature                                     \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m  \u001b[32mVector{Float64}\u001b[39m  P1.expected_reward \u001b[37m(51,)\u001b[39m, P2.expected_reward \u001b[37m(51,)\u001b[39m         \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m                   P3.expected_reward \u001b[37m(51,)\u001b[39m, P4.expected_reward \u001b[37m(51,)\u001b[39m         \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m                   P5.expected_reward \u001b[37m(51,)\u001b[39m                                   \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m                                                                              \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m \u001b[0m\u001b[1mExtras (0)\u001b[22m                                                                   \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m \u001b[90m (none)\u001b[39m                                                                      \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯\u001b[39m","title":"Reading the fit"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/custom_turing_models/#A-single-session","page":"Custom Turing.jl models","text":"A single session goes in a model of one's own with turing_behavioral_session, with a prefix of its own, here with the learning rate sampled on the logit scale:\n\nsession_info = SessionInfo(\n    RescorlaWagner(),\n    (; reward=table.reward[101:150]),\n    (; choice=table.choice[101:150]);\n    who_starts=EnvironmentStarts(),\n    cognitive_save_history=SaveHistory(; states=:expected_reward),\n)\n\n@model function one_session(session_info)\n    #The learning rate on the logit scale, and the inverse temperature\n    learning_rate_logit ~ Normal(0, 1)\n    inverse_temperature ~ LogNormal(1, 0.5)\n\n    #The session, with its variables under the prefix session\n    session ~ to_submodel(\n        prefix(\n            turing_behavioral_session(\n                session_info,\n                (; learning_rate=logistic(learning_rate_logit), inverse_temperature),\n            ),\n            @varname(session),\n        ),\n        false,\n    )\n    return session\nend\n\nsingle_model = one_session(session_info)\nsingle_chain = sample(Xoshiro(2), single_model, NUTS(), 500; progress=false)\n\u001b[90m╭─\u001b[39m\u001b[0m\u001b[1mFlexiChain (500 iterations, 1 chain)\u001b[22m\u001b[90m ────────────────────────────────────────\u001b[39m\u001b[90m╮\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m \u001b[38;5;209m↓ iter  = 251:750\u001b[39m                                                            \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m \u001b[38;5;32m→ chain = 1:1\u001b[39m                                                                \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m                                                                              \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m \u001b[0m\u001b[1mParameters (2)\u001b[22m\u001b[90m ── AbstractPPL.VarName\u001b[39m                                        \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m  \u001b[32mFloat64\u001b[39m  learning_rate_logit, inverse_temperature                           \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m                                                                              \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m \u001b[0m\u001b[1mExtras (14)\u001b[22m                                                                  \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m  \u001b[32mInt64  \u001b[39m  n_steps, tree_depth                                                \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m  \u001b[32mBool   \u001b[39m  is_accept, numerical_error                                         \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m  \u001b[32mFloat64\u001b[39m  acceptance_rate, log_density, hamiltonian_energy                   \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m           hamiltonian_energy_error, max_hamiltonian_energy_error, step_size  \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m           nom_step_size, logprior, loglikelihood, logjoint                   \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯\u001b[39m\n\nWithout a session info, the chains name the parameters and the trajectories without a session, such as learning_rate and expected_reward:\n\nsingle_results = returned(single_model, single_chain)\nmerge(session_parameters_chain(single_results), session_trajectories_chain(single_results))\n\u001b[90m╭─\u001b[39m\u001b[0m\u001b[1mFlexiChain (500 iterations, 1 chain)\u001b[22m\u001b[90m ────────────────────────────────────────\u001b[39m\u001b[90m╮\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m \u001b[38;5;209m↓ iter  = 1:500\u001b[39m                                                              \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m \u001b[38;5;32m→ chain = 1:1\u001b[39m                                                                \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m                                                                              \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m \u001b[0m\u001b[1mParameters (3)\u001b[22m\u001b[90m ── AbstractPPL.VarName\u001b[39m                                        \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m  \u001b[32mFloat64        \u001b[39m  learning_rate, inverse_temperature                         \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m  \u001b[32mVector{Float64}\u001b[39m  expected_reward \u001b[37m(51,)\u001b[39m                                      \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m                                                                              \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m \u001b[0m\u001b[1mExtras (0)\u001b[22m                                                                   \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m│\u001b[39m \u001b[90m (none)\u001b[39m                                                                      \u001b[90m│\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯\u001b[39m","title":"A single session"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/custom_turing_models/#What-a-model-of-one's-own-needs","page":"Custom Turing.jl models","text":"It returns what turing_multiple_behavioral_sessions or turing_behavioral_session returns, as it is, for returned to give it to session_parameters_chain and session_trajectories_chain.\nThe parameters of several sessions are a NamedTuple with one vector for each parameter, with a value for every session in the order of sessions_info.session_ids. CognitiveModeling.session_index gives the place of a session's data identifier.\nThe variables of the sessions are under session[i], or under the prefix of a single session, so the model's own variables need other names.\nThe forms of the two functions which take a model and a chain only take the package's own models, which know their sessions, so a model of one's own gives them its results.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"What a model of one's own needs"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#Internals","page":"Overview","text":"These are only used inside the package, and can change without notice. What users work with is under API.\n\nDefining models\nSimulation\nSaving histories\nFitting, with the internals of parameter models, and the utility functions for preparing models and within models\n\nPages = [\n    \"defining_models.md\",\n    \"simulation.md\",\n    \"saving_histories.md\",\n    \"fitting.md\",\n    \"parameter_models.md\",\n    \"preparing_models.md\",\n    \"within_models.md\",\n]","title":"Internals"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/discrete_parameters/#Discrete-parameters","page":"Discrete parameters","text":"This page is a sketch of a tutorial. Its code isn't run when the documentation is built, and the sections that are marked as planned describe what isn't in the package yet.\n\nSome parameters of a cognitive model aren't continuous: whether a participant uses a strategy at all, which of several strategies they use, or how many items they can hold in memory. This page shows how a model says that a parameter is discrete, and how such a parameter is fitted.\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\nusing LogExpFunctions: logistic\nusing Turing\nusing Turing: Bijectors, DynamicPPL","title":"Discrete parameters"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/discrete_parameters/#A-model-with-a-discrete-parameter","page":"Discrete parameters","text":"This model learns the expected reward of an option only when uses_rewards is true, and otherwise keeps expecting a half. The domain of the parameter says that it is discrete, by its type:\n\nstruct LearnsOrNot <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\nCognitiveModeling.initialize_state(model::LearnsOrNot, parameters) = (; expected_reward=0.5)\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.run_timestep(model::LearnsOrNot, context::ModelContext)\n    V₋₁ = context.previous_state.expected_reward\n    Vₜ = if Bool(context.parameters.uses_rewards)\n        V₋₁ + context.parameters.learning_rate * (context.input.reward - V₋₁)\n    else\n        V₋₁\n    end\n    return ModelOutput((; choice=BernoulliLogit(5 * (Vₜ - 0.5))), (; expected_reward=Vₜ))\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::LearnsOrNot)\n    return ModelAttributes(;\n        parameters=(:learning_rate, :uses_rewards),\n        states=:expected_reward,\n        inputs=:reward,\n        outputs=:choice,\n    )\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.parameter_domains(model::LearnsOrNot)\n    return (;\n        learning_rate=ParameterDomain(; lower=0, upper=1),\n        uses_rewards=ParameterDomain(; type=Bool),\n    )\nend\n\nCognitiveModeling.discrete_parameter_names and CognitiveModeling.continuous_parameter_names then give the parameters of each kind.","title":"A model with a discrete parameter"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/discrete_parameters/#Sampling-a-discrete-parameter","page":"Discrete parameters","text":"A sampler which uses gradients, such as NUTS, can't sample a discrete parameter. With Gibbs, each kind of variable gets a sampler of its own. Here every session has its own parameters, with sessions_info made from a table as on the other pages:\n\nparameters_model = IndependentParameters((;\n    learning_rate=Beta(2, 2), uses_rewards=Bernoulli(0.5)\n))\nmodel = turing_full_model(parameters_model, sessions_info)\n\nThe names of the variables of each kind can be read from the model. DynamicPPL.jl, which Turing.jl is built on, gives the distribution that every variable is sampled from, and a variable is discrete when its distribution is. A function of one's own keeps the lines together:\n\nfunction discrete_and_continuous_variable_names(model)\n    distributions = DynamicPPL.extract_priors(model)\n    variable_names = collect(keys(distributions))\n    is_discrete = map(name -> distributions[name] isa DiscreteDistribution, variable_names)\n    return (;\n        discrete=variable_names[is_discrete], continuous=variable_names[.!is_discrete]\n    )\nend\n\nvariable_names = discrete_and_continuous_variable_names(model)\n\nThis finds every discrete variable of the model, also a choice that wasn't recorded and is imputed, which is a variable without being a parameter. Each kind then gets its sampler:\n\nsampler = Gibbs(variable_names.continuous => NUTS(), variable_names.discrete => MH())\nchain = sample(model, sampler, 2000; nadapts=1000, discard_initial=1000)\n\nInside a Gibbs sampler, NUTS only adapts its step size when sample is given nadapts, and the samples of those steps are kept unless discard_initial is given too. Without nadapts, it may never move, see Turing.jl's issue 2874. Gibbs also takes no sampler without variables, so a model without discrete variables is sampled with NUTS alone.\n\nThe share of the samples in which a session's uses_rewards is true is the posterior probability that the session learned from its rewards.","title":"Sampling a discrete parameter"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/discrete_parameters/#A-regression-on-a-discrete-parameter","page":"Discrete parameters","text":"A RegressionParameter gives a number for each session, which a discrete parameter can't be. With a family, the value of each session is sampled from a distribution instead, as a variable of its own, and the regression gives a parameter of that distribution. Here it gives the log odds that a session uses its rewards, which rise or fall with age:\n\nparameters_model = HierarchicalRegressionParameters((;\n    learning_rate=RegressionParameter(Intercept(Normal(0, 1)); transformation=logistic),\n    uses_rewards=RegressionParameter(\n        Intercept(Normal(0, 1)),\n        ContinuousPredictor(:age, Normal(0, 0.5));\n        family=BernoulliLogit,\n    ),\n))\nmodel = turing_full_model(parameters_model, sessions_info)\n\nA distribution which takes the sum of the regression as it is, such as BernoulliLogit, is the safe choice. With transformation = logistic and Bernoulli, a sampler which uses ForwardDiff.jl for its gradients stops with an error as soon as it tries a sum that is high enough for the probability to be exactly 1.\n\nThe coefficients are continuous and the values of the sessions discrete, so each kind gets its sampler again:\n\nvariable_names = discrete_and_continuous_variable_names(model)\nsampler = Gibbs(variable_names.continuous => NUTS(), variable_names.discrete => MH())\nchain = sample(model, sampler, 2000; nadapts=1000, discard_initial=1000)","title":"A regression on a discrete parameter"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/discrete_parameters/#Ordered-levels-and-counts","page":"Discrete parameters","text":"A parameter with ordered levels, such as a number of planning steps from one to four, needs one regression and a cutpoint between each pair of neighboring levels, as in an ordinal regression. The cutpoints are other parameters of the family's distribution, with a prior of their own, and are the same for every session. A RegressionFamily has the parameters of the distribution in the order in which it takes them, with RegressionOutcome for the one that the regression gives and a prior for each of the others. The regression has no intercept, since the cutpoints take its place:\n\nplanning_steps = RegressionParameter(\n    ContinuousPredictor(:age, Normal(0, 1));\n    family=RegressionFamily(\n        (;\n            location=RegressionOutcome(),\n            cutpoints=Bijectors.ordered(filldist(Normal(0, 2), 3)),\n        );\n        distribution=OrderedLogistic,\n    ),\n)\n\nA count needs no other parameters: a number of items out of seven is family = logit -> BinomialLogit(7, logit), and a count without an upper bound is family = LogPoisson.","title":"Ordered levels and counts"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/discrete_parameters/#Unordered-categories-(planned)","page":"Discrete parameters","text":"A parameter with more than two unordered categories, such as which of three strategies a participant uses, needs a regression for each category but one, with a softmax across them. This may come through BayesianRegressionModels.jl.","title":"Unordered categories (planned)"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/discrete_parameters/#Without-sampling-the-discrete-values-(planned)","page":"Discrete parameters","text":"A discrete parameter with few values can be summed out: the session is run once for each value, and the results are weighted by the probabilities of the values. The model then has no discrete variables, so NUTS can fit all of it, and the probability of each value for a session is computed afterwards.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Without sampling the discrete values (planned)"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/interfacing_with_common_rl/#Interfacing-with-CommonRLInterface.jl","page":"Interfacing with CommonRLInterface.jl","text":"Placeholder tutorial. It will show how to use an environment from CommonRLInterface.jl as the environment of a cognitive model, which also covers the environments of POMDPs.jl, through the wrappers of POMDPTools.jl, such as POMDPCommonRLEnv{Any}(pomdp), and those of ReinforcementLearning.jl, which convert to it.\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Interfacing with CommonRLInterface.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/defining_models/#Internals-of-defining-models","page":"Defining models","text":"Pages = [\"defining_models.md\"]","title":"Internals of defining models"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/defining_models/#Types","page":"Defining models","text":"","title":"Types"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/defining_models/#Utility-functions","page":"Defining models","text":"","title":"Utility functions"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/defining_models/#Checking-a-model's-own-methods","page":"Defining models","text":"","title":"Checking a model's own methods"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/defining_models/#Comparing-names","page":"Defining models","text":"","title":"Comparing names"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/defining_models/#Checking-attributes-against-a-model's-names","page":"Defining models","text":"","title":"Checking attributes against a model's names"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/defining_models/#Checking-parameters-against-their-domains","page":"Defining models","text":"","title":"Checking parameters against their domains"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/defining_models/#Outputs-and-inputs-before-the-first-timestep","page":"Defining models","text":"","title":"Outputs and inputs before the first timestep"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/defining_models/#Displaying-models,-and-describing-them-in-messages","page":"Defining models","text":"","title":"Displaying models, and describing them in messages"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.AbstractAttributeKind","page":"Defining models","text":"AbstractAttributeKind\n\nSupertype of the kinds of attributes of a model: ParameterKind for its parameters, StateKind for its states, and the subtypes of AbstractObservableAttributeKind, ObservationKind for the observations and ActionKind for the actions. Used to say which kind of attribute something is.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.AbstractAttributeKind"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.AbstractObservableAttributeKind","page":"Defining models","text":"AbstractObservableAttributeKind\n\nSupertype of the kinds of attributes that the data of a session has: ObservationKind for the observations, which are the inputs in the attributes of its cognitive model, and ActionKind for the actions, which are its outputs. Used when a table is split into sessions and when the data of a session is named, to say which of the two a column or a vector of data is.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.AbstractObservableAttributeKind"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel","page":"Defining models","text":"AbstractTimestepModel\n\nSupertype for timestep models: cognitive models and environment models, which every function that a model implements or is used with takes. A model is a subtype of AbstractCognitiveModel or of AbstractEnvironmentModel, and generally not of this type itself.\n\nThe two kinds work the same way, one timestep at a time: on each timestep a model receives an input and gives an output, in a ModelContext and a ModelOutput. In a simulation or a fitted session, the outputs of the cognitive model are its actions, which are the inputs of the environment model, and the outputs of the environment model are the observations, which are the inputs of the cognitive model. A cognitive model can therefore also serve as the environment of another.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.ActionKind","page":"Defining models","text":"ActionKind()\n\nThe kind of attribute that the actions of a session are, see AbstractObservableAttributeKind.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.ActionKind"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.ObservationKind","page":"Defining models","text":"ObservationKind()\n\nThe kind of attribute that the observations of a session are, see AbstractObservableAttributeKind.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.ObservationKind"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.ParameterKind","page":"Defining models","text":"ParameterKind()\n\nThe kind of attribute that the parameters of a model are, see AbstractAttributeKind.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.ParameterKind"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.StateKind","page":"Defining models","text":"StateKind()\n\nThe kind of attribute that the states of a model are, see AbstractAttributeKind.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.StateKind"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.as_tuple-Tuple{Tuple}","page":"Defining models","text":"as_tuple(values)\n\nReturn values as a Tuple: a tuple as it is, a vector as a tuple, and anything else in a tuple of one. Used for the arguments that take one value or several: the names given to ModelAttributes, SaveHistory, MultipleSessionsInfo, HierarchicalGroupedParameters and HierarchicalRegressionParameters, which are then asserted to be Symbols, and the values that stand for a missing value in SessionInfo.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.as_tuple"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.check_unique_attribute_names-Tuple{ModelAttributes}","page":"Defining models","text":"check_unique_attribute_names(attributes)\n\nThrow an ArgumentError if a name in attributes, a ModelAttributes, is used for more than one attribute, within a kind or across the kinds, and return nothing otherwise. Whether the names are unique is decided by unique_names, and the message is only made when they aren't. Used by the constructor of ModelAttributes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_unique_attribute_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.is_discrete-Union{Tuple{ParameterDomain{Ttype}}, Tuple{Ttype}} where Ttype","page":"Defining models","text":"is_discrete(domain)\n\nReturn whether a parameter with domain, a ParameterDomain, is discrete: when its type is a type of whole numbers, or isn't a type of real numbers. Used by discrete_parameter_names and by the display of a model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.is_discrete"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.throw_duplicate_attribute_name-Tuple{ModelAttributes}","page":"Defining models","text":"throw_duplicate_attribute_name(attributes)\n\nThrow the ArgumentError of check_unique_attribute_names, naming the first name of attributes that is used twice and the kinds it is used for.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.throw_duplicate_attribute_name"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.unique_names-Tuple{Tuple{}}","page":"Defining models","text":"unique_names(names)\n\nReturn whether no name in the tuple names appears twice. It is unrolled over the names with has_name. Used by check_unique_attribute_names.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.unique_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.check_model_output-Tuple{ModelOutput, CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel}","page":"Defining models","text":"check_model_output(output, model)\n\nReturn output if it is the ModelOutput that an run_timestep method of model must return, and throw an error otherwise. Used on each timestep of simulate, simulate! and fitted sessions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_model_output"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.check_type_attribute_names-Tuple{ModelAttributes, CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel}","page":"Defining models","text":"check_type_attribute_names(attributes, model)\n\nReturn attributes if it is the ModelAttributes that a method of attribute_names must return, and throw an informative error otherwise. model is the cognitive model or the environment model whose method returned it. Used wherever the names of a model are read: by the checks of simulations, instantiated models, save histories and fitted sessions, and by the display of a model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_type_attribute_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.has_attribute_names-Tuple{CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel}","page":"Defining models","text":"has_attribute_names(model)\n\nReturn whether model, a cognitive model or an environment model, has its own method of attribute_names, instead of the fallback which throws. Used by the display of a model, which shows one without a method of its own as not specified.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.has_attribute_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.has_name-Tuple{Symbol, Tuple{}}","page":"Defining models","text":"has_name(name, names)\nhas_every_name(names, other_names)\n\nReturn whether name is among the tuple names, and whether every name in the tuple names is among other_names. Used by unique_names, and by the checks that a name map, a save history or the data has names in a model's attributes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.has_name"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.same_names-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Defining models","text":"same_names(names, other_names)\n\nReturn whether two tuples of names have the same names, in any order. Used by the checks of names in simulations and fitted sessions, such as check_names_fit and check_match_attribute_names.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.same_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.check_input_names-Tuple{CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel, Any}","page":"Defining models","text":"check_input_names(model, input)\n\nThrow an informative error if input, given to a model without the other side of a simulation, isn't a NamedTuple with every input in the attributes of model, from attribute_names. It can have more than those. Used by simulate and simulate! on a single input.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_input_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.check_match_attribute_names-Tuple{CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel, Any, Any, Any}","page":"Defining models","text":"check_match_attribute_names(model, parameters, state, previous_output)\n\nThrow an informative error if the names of the parameters, of the previous output or of the state aren't those in the attributes of model, a cognitive model or an environment model, from attribute_names, or if the previous output or the state isn't a NamedTuple. Used by InstantiatedModel, simulate and fitted sessions, before anything runs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_match_attribute_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.check_domains_fit_model-Tuple{CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel, NamedTuple{()}}","page":"Defining models","text":"check_domains_fit_model(model, domains)\n\nThrow an ArgumentError if domains, what parameter_domains returns for model, isn't a NamedTuple of ParameterDomains, or names a parameter that isn't in the model's attributes, from attribute_names. Used by check_parameters_in_domains, discrete_parameter_names and the display of a model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_domains_fit_model"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.check_each_parameter_in_domain-Union{Tuple{names}, Tuple{CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel, NamedTuple, NamedTuple{names}}} where names","page":"Defining models","text":"check_each_parameter_in_domain(model, parameters, domains)\n\nCheck the value in parameters of each parameter with a domain in domains, with check_values_in_domains. parameters must have every parameter with a domain, which it has when its names are those in the model's attributes. Used by check_parameters_in_domains.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_each_parameter_in_domain"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.check_parameters_in_domains-Tuple{CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel, NamedTuple}","page":"Defining models","text":"check_parameters_in_domains(model, parameters)\n\nThrow a ParameterDomainError if a parameter in parameters is outside its domain in parameter_domains of model: of another type than the domain's, or below its lower bound or above its upper bound. Real numbers and values that aren't numbers are checked, and so is every element of an array. A number that isn't a real number is left alone. It is an ArgumentError when the domains aren't ParameterDomains under the names of parameters in the model's attributes. Used by simulate, InstantiatedModel and fitted sessions, before anything runs, unless they are given check_parameter_domains = false.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_parameters_in_domains"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.check_value_in_domain-Union{Tuple{Ttype}, Tuple{CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel, Symbol, Real, ParameterDomain{Ttype}}} where Ttype","page":"Defining models","text":"check_value_in_domain(model, name, value, domain)\n\nThrow a ParameterDomainError if value, the value of the parameter name of model, is outside domain, a ParameterDomain: a real number that the domain's type doesn't take, as takes_value decides, or that is below the lower bound or above the upper bound, which a NaN always is, and which is_beyond_bounds decides for a number that isn't one of Julia's own, or a value that isn't a number and isn't of the domain's type. Every element of an array is checked as a value of its own. A number that isn't a real number, and an array of them, is left alone. Used by check_values_in_domains.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_value_in_domain"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.check_values_in_domains-Tuple{CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel, Tuple{}, Tuple{}, Tuple{}}","page":"Defining models","text":"check_values_in_domains(model, names, values, domains)\n\nCheck each of values, a tuple with the values of the parameters names, against the domain at the same place in domains, a tuple of ParameterDomains, with check_value_in_domain, one after the other. Used by check_each_parameter_in_domain.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_values_in_domains"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.is_beyond_bounds-Tuple{Union{AbstractIrrational, AbstractFloat, Integer, Rational}, ParameterDomain}","page":"Defining models","text":"is_beyond_bounds(value, domain)\n\nReturn whether value, a real number which didn't compare as within the bounds of domain, a ParameterDomain, is beyond them. One of Julia's own number types always is. A number of another type, such as one of automatic differentiation, is when it is NaN, or when it is at or beyond the nearest floating-point number below the lower bound or above the upper bound. Used by check_value_in_domain.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.is_beyond_bounds"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.takes_value-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Type{T}, Real}} where T","page":"Defining models","text":"takes_value(type, value)\n\nReturn whether a ParameterDomain with type takes the real number value: when it is of the type, when the type is a type of whole numbers and it is a whole number in another number type, such as 3.0, and when the type is Bool and it is 0 or 1. Used by check_value_in_domain.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.takes_value"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.throw_not_of_type-Tuple{Any, Symbol, Any, ParameterDomain}","page":"Defining models","text":"throw_not_of_type(model, name, value, domain)\nthrow_outside_bounds(model, name, value, domain)\n\nThrow the ParameterDomainError for the parameter name of model, whose value isn't of the type of domain, or is outside its bounds. Used by check_value_in_domain.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.throw_not_of_type"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.output_before_first_timestep-Tuple{CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel}","page":"Defining models","text":"output_before_first_timestep(model)\ninput_before_first_timestep(model)\noutput_before_first_timestep(attributes)\ninput_before_first_timestep(attributes)\n\nReturn the output and the input from before the first timestep: a NamedTuple with the output or the input names in the attributes of model, a cognitive model or an environment model, from attribute_names, or those that attributes, a ModelAttributes, has, each BeforeFirstTimestep. A model's names are checked to be a ModelAttributes first. Used wherever a model starts: by InstantiatedModel, reset!, simulate and fitted sessions, for the previous output and for the output distributions saved as timestep 0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.output_before_first_timestep"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.describe_domain-Union{Tuple{ParameterDomain{Ttype}}, Tuple{Ttype}} where Ttype","page":"Defining models","text":"describe_domain(domain)\n\nReturn domain, a ParameterDomain, as the display of a model writes it after the name of its parameter: an interval for a continuous parameter, such as [0, 1] or [0, ∞), the whole numbers of a discrete one, such as {1, 2, …} or {0, 1, …, 5}, and the type of a parameter that isn't a number, such as AbstractString. Used by print_parameter_lines.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.describe_domain"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.describe_model-Tuple{AbstractCognitiveModel}","page":"Defining models","text":"describe_model(model)\n\nReturn how model is named in an error message: as a cognitive model or as an environment model, with its type. Used in the error messages of the checks on models, in simulations and in fitted sessions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.describe_model"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.domains_to_display-Tuple{CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel}","page":"Defining models","text":"domains_to_display(model)\ndefaults_to_display(model)\n\nReturn the domains and the default values of the parameters of model for its display: what parameter_domains and default_parameters return when it is a NamedTuple, and none otherwise, so that a model whose domains or default values aren't right is still shown. Used by the show method of a model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.domains_to_display"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.has_domain_or_default-Tuple{Symbol, NamedTuple, NamedTuple}","page":"Defining models","text":"has_domain_or_default(name, domains, defaults)\n\nReturn whether the parameter name has a ParameterDomain in domains or a default value in defaults, so that the display of a model has more to show of it than its name. Used by print_attribute_names.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.has_domain_or_default"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.model_kind-Tuple{AbstractCognitiveModel}","page":"Defining models","text":"model_kind(model)\n\nReturn which kind of model model is, as a display names it: \"cognitive model\", \"environment model\", or \"model\" for one of neither kind. Used by the show methods of a model and of an InstantiatedModel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.model_kind"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.n_parameter_lines-Tuple{IO, Tuple{Vararg{Symbol}}}","page":"Defining models","text":"n_parameter_lines(io, names)\n\nReturn how many of the parameters names a display on io lists one on a line: all of them when io isn't limited, as when printing to a file, and at most ten otherwise. Used by print_parameter_lines.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.n_parameter_lines"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.print_attribute_names-Tuple{IO, ModelAttributes}","page":"Defining models","text":"print_attribute_names(io, attributes; shorten=false, domains=(;), defaults=(;))\n\nPrint a line for each kind of attribute in attributes, a ModelAttributes, with none if it has no names of that kind. With shorten, a kind with more names than shows_every_name allows shows only how many there are. When a parameter has a ParameterDomain in domains or a default value in defaults, the parameters are listed one on a line instead, as print_parameter_lines writes them.\n\nThe show methods of a ModelAttributes, of a cognitive model and of an environment model each print a header of their own, and then these lines. Only those of the models shorten them and give the domains and the default values, since a ModelAttributes is where the full lists are read.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.print_attribute_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.print_current_values-Tuple{IO, Any, NamedTuple, Any}","page":"Defining models","text":"print_current_values(io, label, current_values, field_name)\n\nPrint a line with label and current_values, the fields of a model or the parameters or the state of an InstantiatedModel, in a NamedTuple: each name with its value, none when it is empty, and only how many there are, with where to read them, when shows_every_name doesn't allow listing them all. Values are printed compactly, and large arrays are shortened in the REPL. Used by the show methods of a model and of an InstantiatedModel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.print_current_values"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.print_parameter_lines-Tuple{IO, Tuple{Vararg{Symbol}}, NamedTuple, NamedTuple}","page":"Defining models","text":"print_parameter_lines(io, names, domains, defaults; shorten=false)\n\nPrint the parameters names one on a line, each with its domain in domains after its name, as describe_domain writes it, and then its default value in defaults, as in inverse_temperature ∈ [0, ∞) (default 2.0). A parameter without either has its name alone. With shorten, only as many are listed as n_parameter_lines allows, and the last line says how many more there are. Used by print_attribute_names.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.print_parameter_lines"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.shows_every_name-Tuple{IO, Any}","page":"Defining models","text":"shows_every_name(io, names)\n\nReturn whether a display on io lists every one of names: always when io isn't limited, as when printing to a file, and otherwise when there are at most five. Used by the show methods of a model and of an InstantiatedModel, which give how many there are instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.shows_every_name"},{"category":"section","location":"api/simulation/#Simulation","page":"Simulation","text":"Running a cognitive model, an environment model or the two together. What a simulation saves is chosen with a SaveHistory, on the page Saving histories.\n\nPages = [\"simulation.md\"]","title":"Simulation"},{"category":"section","location":"api/simulation/#Simulating","page":"Simulation","text":"","title":"Simulating"},{"category":"section","location":"api/simulation/#Instantiated-models-and-name-maps","page":"Simulation","text":"","title":"Instantiated models and name maps"},{"category":"section","location":"api/simulation/#An-environment-with-fixed-observations","page":"Simulation","text":"","title":"An environment with fixed observations"},{"category":"method","location":"api/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.simulate!-Tuple{InstantiatedModel, Any}","page":"Simulation","text":"simulate!(instantiated_model::InstantiatedModel, input; timestep_size=1,\n          rng=default_rng())\n\nRun a single timestep of instantiated_model on input, update it, and return the sampled output. What instantiated_model.save_history selects of the state, the input, the output and the output distribution is added to instantiated_model.history, together with the size of the timestep.\n\nThe output is in a NamedTuple with the names the model gave it, as in output.choice, as for simulate. The input must be a NamedTuple with every input in the model's attributes, from attribute_names, or an ArgumentError is thrown.\n\nArguments\n\ninstantiated_model::InstantiatedModel: the model to update.\ninput::NamedTuple: what the model receives on this timestep, such as (; reward = 1.0) for a cognitive model. An entry that isn't there is NoValue.\ntimestep_size=1: how much time this timestep covers.\nrng::AbstractRNG=default_rng(): the random number generator to sample from.\n\nExamples\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\nusing Distributions: Bernoulli\n\nstruct RescorlaWagner <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(model::RescorlaWagner, parameters)\n    return (; expected_reward = 0.5)\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.run_timestep(model::RescorlaWagner, context::ModelContext)\n    V₋₁ = context.previous_state.expected_reward\n    Vₜ = V₋₁ + context.parameters.learning_rate * (context.input.reward - V₋₁)\n    return ModelOutput((; choice = Bernoulli(Vₜ)), (; expected_reward = Vₜ))\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::RescorlaWagner)\n    return ModelAttributes(; parameters = :learning_rate, states = :expected_reward, inputs = :reward, outputs = :choice)\nend\n\nagent = InstantiatedModel(RescorlaWagner(), (; learning_rate = 0.5); save_history = SaveHistory(SaveEverything()))\n\nsimulate!(agent, (; reward = 1.0))\nsimulate!(agent, (; reward = 1.0))\n\nagent.history.states\n\n# output\n\n3-element Vector{Any}:\n (expected_reward = 0.5,)\n (expected_reward = 0.75,)\n (expected_reward = 0.875,)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.simulate!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.simulate!-Tuple{InstantiatedModel, InstantiatedModel}","page":"Simulation","text":"simulate!(agent::InstantiatedModel, environment::InstantiatedModel; <keyword arguments>)\n\nSimulate agent interacting with environment from where they are, update them both, and return the trajectories of this call in the same NamedTuple as simulate, without timestep 0: what agent.save_history selects of the states, observations, actions and action distributions on each timestep, what environment.save_history selects of the environment's under environment, and the sizes of the timesteps. What the two select is also added to agent.history and environment.history, in each model's own names, together with the sizes of the timesteps, unless a selection is empty. A history has timestep 0 from where the model started, when its SaveHistory saves the initial timestep values.\n\nThe two models must fit together, as for simulate, directly or through observation_map and action_map. The agent's history holds the observations as its model received them, and the environment's history the actions as its model did.\n\nThe simulation stops before n_steps timesteps if stopping_criterion of either model is met, unless check_stopping_criterion is false. A model where it is met already can't be simulated further, and throws an error until it is reset!.\n\nAn instantiated model isn't type stable, as InstantiatedModel describes. Use simulate where that matters, or if most calls are for a single timestep.\n\nArguments\n\nwho_starts::Union{AgentStarts, EnvironmentStarts}: who moves first on each timestep.\nn_steps::Int: the number of timesteps to simulate, which must be at least 1. It is the most timesteps there can be when a model has a stopping_criterion.\ntimestep_sizes=1: how much time each timestep covers. A value that isn't a vector, such as a single number, is the size of every timestep of this call. A vector has one size for each timestep of this call, so its length must be n_steps.\nrng::AbstractRNG=default_rng(): the random number generator to sample from.\nobservation_map::Union{Nothing, NameMap}=nothing: which observations of the environment the agent receives, and under which names, as for simulate.\naction_map::Union{Nothing, NameMap}=nothing: which actions of the agent the environment receives, and under which names, as for simulate.\ncheck_stopping_criterion::Bool=true: whether stopping_criterion of the two models is checked before each timestep, as for simulate.\n\nExamples\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\nusing Distributions: Bernoulli\n\nstruct RandomChoices <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(model::RandomChoices, parameters)\n    return (;)\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.run_timestep(model::RandomChoices, context::ModelContext)\n    return ModelOutput((; choice = Bernoulli(0.5)))\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::RandomChoices)\n    return ModelAttributes(; parameters = (), states = (), inputs = :timestep, outputs = :choice)\nend\n\nstruct TimestepCounter <: AbstractEnvironmentModel end\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(model::TimestepCounter, parameters)\n    return (; n_timesteps = 0)\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.run_timestep(model::TimestepCounter, context::ModelContext)\n    n_timesteps = context.previous_state.n_timesteps + 1\n    return ModelOutput((; timestep = Deterministic(n_timesteps)), (; n_timesteps))\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::TimestepCounter)\n    return ModelAttributes(; parameters = (), states = :n_timesteps, inputs = :choice, outputs = :timestep)\nend\n\nagent = InstantiatedModel(RandomChoices(), (;))\nenvironment = InstantiatedModel(TimestepCounter(), (;))\n\nsimulate!(agent, environment; who_starts = EnvironmentStarts(), n_steps = 2)\nsimulate!(agent, environment; who_starts = EnvironmentStarts(), n_steps = 3)\n\nenvironment.state\n\n# output\n\n(n_timesteps = 5,)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.simulate!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.simulate-Tuple{CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel, Any}","page":"Simulation","text":"simulate(model, input; <keyword arguments>)\n\nRun a single timestep of model, a cognitive model or an environment model, on input, without the other side of a simulation, and return the sampled output and the new state of the model.\n\nNothing is kept between calls, so pass the returned state and output on as previous_state and previous_output to run the next timestep. simulate! on a single input does the same for an InstantiatedModel, which keeps them. This is type stable, which an instantiated model isn't, so it is the faster of the two.\n\nThe output is in a NamedTuple with the names the model gave it, as in output.choice. The input must be a NamedTuple with every input in the model's attributes, from attribute_names, or an ArgumentError is thrown. Parameters that aren't given take the values in default_parameters, if model provides defaults for them, and the names of the parameters, the state and the previous output are checked as in a full simulation.\n\nArguments\n\nmodel::AbstractTimestepModel: the model.\ninput::NamedTuple: what the model receives on this timestep, such as (; reward = 1.0) for a cognitive model. An entry that isn't there is NoValue.\nparameters::NamedTuple: the parameters of model, with its default values for those that aren't given, if it provides any.\ntimestep_size=1: how much time this timestep covers.\nrng::AbstractRNG=default_rng(): the random number generator to sample from.\nprevious_state=initialize_state(model, ...): the state of model before this timestep, by default its initial state from the full parameters. A state that model changes in place will be changed.\nprevious_output=output_before_first_timestep(model): the output before this timestep. By default every output is BeforeFirstTimestep.\ncheck_parameter_domains::Bool=true: whether the parameters are checked against their domains in parameter_domains, as for a whole simulation.\n\nExamples\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\nusing Distributions: Bernoulli\n\nstruct RescorlaWagner <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(model::RescorlaWagner, parameters)\n    return (; expected_reward = 0.5)\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.run_timestep(model::RescorlaWagner, context::ModelContext)\n    V₋₁ = context.previous_state.expected_reward\n    Vₜ = V₋₁ + context.parameters.learning_rate * (context.input.reward - V₋₁)\n    return ModelOutput((; choice = Bernoulli(Vₜ)), (; expected_reward = Vₜ))\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::RescorlaWagner)\n    return ModelAttributes(; parameters = :learning_rate, states = :expected_reward, inputs = :reward, outputs = :choice)\nend\n\nparameters = (; learning_rate = 0.5)\n\n#The first timestep starts from the initial state\naction, state = simulate(RescorlaWagner(), (; reward = 1.0); parameters)\n\n#The next one continues from what the first returned\naction, state = simulate(\n    RescorlaWagner(),\n    (; reward = 1.0);\n    parameters,\n    previous_state = state,\n    previous_output = action,\n)\n\nstate\n\n# output\n\n(expected_reward = 0.875,)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.simulate"},{"category":"method","location":"api/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.simulate-Tuple{CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel, CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel}","page":"Simulation","text":"simulate(cognitive_model, environment; <keyword arguments>)\n\nSimulate cognitive_model interacting with environment, and return the trajectories that were saved, which are what changed over the timesteps, in a NamedTuple: states, observations, actions and action_distributions of the cognitive model, environment with the states, observations, actions and observation_distributions of the environment, and timestep_sizes. Each is a vector with one element for timestep 0, before the first timestep, and one for each timestep after it.\n\nEach element is what happened on that timestep, and the states are those after it. The observations are those the cognitive model received, and the actions those it gave, and under environment the observations are those the environment gave and the actions those it received, which differ from the cognitive model's when a NameMap selects or renames them. The action distributions are what the cognitive model gave its actions to be sampled from, and the observation distributions what the environment gave its observations to be sampled from. The element for timestep 0 has the previous observation and action, which are BeforeFirstTimestep unless others are given, BeforeFirstTimestep() for every distribution, and the states before the first timestep. Since Julia counts from 1, element t + 1 is timestep t.\n\ncognitive_save_history and environment_save_history select what is kept of each element of each model, which is nothing by default: give SaveHistory(SaveEverything()) to keep everything, and SaveHistory(SaveEverything(); save_initial_timestep_values = false) to leave out timestep 0, so that element t is timestep t. The vectors of both models have the same length, which cognitive_save_history decides. The sizes of the timesteps are returned as they were given: a single size as it is, and a vector of sizes with the sizes of the timesteps that ran, after BeforeFirstTimestep() for timestep 0 when it is there.\n\nParameters that aren't given take the values in default_parameters, if cognitive_model or environment provides defaults for them. The names of the parameters, the states, the previous action and the previous observation must be those in attribute_names, and the outputs of each model must be the inputs of the other, or an ArgumentError is thrown before the first timestep. When the two name things differently, or one gives something that isn't in the other's attributes, observation_map and action_map say which outputs of one the other receives, and under which names.\n\nThe simulation stops before n_steps timesteps if stopping_criterion of either model is met, unless check_stopping_criterion is false, and everything returned then has the timesteps that ran.\n\nArguments\n\ncognitive_model::AbstractTimestepModel: the cognitive model, usually an AbstractCognitiveModel.\nenvironment::AbstractTimestepModel: the environment model, usually an AbstractEnvironmentModel. A cognitive model can serve as the environment of another when its outputs are the inputs in the other's attributes, and vice versa.\nwho_starts::Union{AgentStarts, EnvironmentStarts}: who moves first on each timestep. It has no default, because a wrong choice shifts the model by a timestep without any error.\nn_steps::Int: the number of timesteps to simulate, which must be at least 1. It is the most timesteps there can be when a model has a stopping_criterion.\ntimestep_sizes=1: how much time each timestep covers, which both cognitive_model and environment receive. A value that isn't a vector, such as a single number, is the size of every timestep. A vector has one size for each timestep, so its length must be n_steps.\ncognitive_parameters::NamedTuple: the parameters of cognitive_model, with its default values for those that aren't given, if it provides any.\nenvironment_parameters::NamedTuple: the parameters of environment, with its default values for those that aren't given, if it provides any.\nrng::AbstractRNG=default_rng(): the random number generator to sample actions and observations from, which cognitive_model and environment also receive in their contexts.\ncognitive_save_history::SaveHistory=SaveHistory(): which states, inputs, outputs and output distributions of cognitive_model are saved, by name, and whether timestep 0 is. Nothing is saved by default, and SaveHistory(SaveEverything()) saves everything. See SaveHistory.\nenvironment_save_history::SaveHistory=SaveHistory(): the same for environment, whose inputs are the actions it receives and whose outputs are the observations it gives. Whether timestep 0 is saved follows cognitive_save_history, so that the vectors of both models have the same length.\nobservation_map::Union{Nothing, NameMap}=nothing: which observations of environment are passed on to cognitive_model, and under which names, as a NameMap from the names environment gives to exactly those in the attributes of cognitive_model. Without one, the two must have the same observations in their attributes, which cognitive_model receives under the same names.\naction_map::Union{Nothing, NameMap}=nothing: which actions of cognitive_model are passed on to environment, and under which names, as a NameMap from the names cognitive_model gives to exactly those in the attributes of environment. Without one, the two must have the same actions in their attributes, which environment receives under the same names.\ncheck_stopping_criterion::Bool=true: whether stopping_criterion of the two models is checked before each timestep. With false, all of n_steps timesteps run.\ncheck_parameter_domains::Bool=true: whether the parameters of the two models are checked against their domains in parameter_domains before the simulation, where a value outside its domain throws a ParameterDomainError.\nprevious_cognitive_state=initialize_state(cognitive_model, ...): the state of cognitive_model before the first timestep, by default its initial state from the full parameters. A state that cognitive_model changes in place will be changed.\nprevious_action=output_before_first_timestep(cognitive_model): the action before the first timestep. By default every action is BeforeFirstTimestep, with the names from attribute_names.\nprevious_environment_state=initialize_state(environment, ...): the state of environment before the first timestep, by default its initial state from the full parameters.\nprevious_observation=output_before_first_timestep(environment): the observation before the first timestep. By default every observation is BeforeFirstTimestep(), with the names from attribute_names of environment.\n\nSee also simulate!, which continues from where the last simulation left off.\n\nExamples\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\nusing Distributions: Bernoulli, BernoulliLogit\n\n#A cognitive model which learns the expected reward\nstruct RescorlaWagner <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(model::RescorlaWagner, parameters)\n    return (; expected_reward = 0.5)\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.run_timestep(model::RescorlaWagner, context::ModelContext)\n    V₋₁ = context.previous_state.expected_reward\n    Vₜ = V₋₁ + context.parameters.learning_rate * (context.input.reward - V₋₁)\n    return ModelOutput((; choice = BernoulliLogit(Vₜ - 0.5)), (; expected_reward = Vₜ))\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::RescorlaWagner)\n    return ModelAttributes(; parameters = :learning_rate, states = :expected_reward, inputs = :reward, outputs = :choice)\nend\n\n#An environment which takes the choice, and gives a reward with a fixed probability whatever it is\nstruct BernoulliRewards <: AbstractEnvironmentModel end\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(environment::BernoulliRewards, parameters)\n    return (;)\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.run_timestep(environment::BernoulliRewards, context::ModelContext)\n    return ModelOutput((; reward = Bernoulli(context.parameters.reward_probability)))\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(environment::BernoulliRewards)\n    return ModelAttributes(; parameters = :reward_probability, states = (), inputs = :choice, outputs = :reward)\nend\n\nsimulation = simulate(\n    RescorlaWagner(),\n    BernoulliRewards();\n    who_starts = EnvironmentStarts(),\n    n_steps = 3,\n    cognitive_parameters = (; learning_rate = 0.5),\n    environment_parameters = (; reward_probability = 1.0),\n    cognitive_save_history = SaveHistory(SaveEverything()),\n)\n\n#The initial state, as timestep 0, and the state after each timestep\nsimulation.states\n\n# output\n\n4-element Vector{@NamedTuple{expected_reward::Float64}}:\n (expected_reward = 0.5,)\n (expected_reward = 0.75,)\n (expected_reward = 0.875,)\n (expected_reward = 0.9375,)\n\nExtended help\n\nThe return type is concrete as long as the types of the outputs don't change between timesteps. If they do, like an observation with an entry that is sometimes NoValue, the vector for that output holds a Union of the types. The same goes for timestep 0 when it has other types than the timesteps after it, which the observations and actions usually do, since they are BeforeFirstTimestep() there.\n\nNothing is copied when outputs are saved. A model or environment which changes an object in its state in place, instead of returning a new one, must add a method of prepare_state_for_saving, or all the saved states show the final values, with no error. Observations and actions have no such function, so they must be new objects on each timestep.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.simulate"},{"category":"type","location":"api/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.InstantiatedModel","page":"Simulation","text":"InstantiatedModel(model, parameters; save_history=SaveHistory(),\n                  check_parameter_domains=true)\nInstantiatedModel(model, parameters, state, previous_output;\n                  save_history=SaveHistory(), check_parameter_domains=true)\n\nA cognitive model or an environment model with its parameters, current state and previous output, which simulate! can update iteratively.\n\nEach argument is kept in a field of the same name, such as agent.state. Parameters that aren't given take the values in default_parameters, if the model provides defaults for them. The state defaults to the output of initialize_state with the full parameters, and the previous output to every output being BeforeFirstTimestep. The previous output and the state must be NamedTuples, and the names of the parameters, of the previous output and of the state must be those in the model's attributes, from attribute_names, or an error is thrown.\n\nagent.history holds a ModelHistory with where the model started, which is timestep 0, and everything that happens to it, as selected by save_history, a SaveHistory. By default nothing is selected and the vectors in agent.history stay empty, and SaveHistory(SaveEverything()) saves everything. States, inputs and outputs can only be selected by name among those in the model's attributes, since the history starts from those names. agent.n_timesteps counts the timesteps the model has simulated, whatever is saved. The parameters are checked against their domains in parameter_domains, where a value outside its domain throws a ParameterDomainError, unless check_parameter_domains is false.\n\nIn the REPL, an instantiated model shows the kind and the type of its model, how many timesteps it has simulated, and the values of its parameters and of its state, with only how many there are when there are more than five. Large arrays are shortened. When the model's stopping_criterion is met, it says so, since it must then be reset! before it can be simulated again. On one line, as inside a vector, it shows the type and the kind of its model and how many timesteps it has simulated.\n\nAn instantiated model is a convenience, and isn't type stable, by design: its state and its previous output can change type, as an output does from BeforeFirstTimestep() to a Bool, and so can what its history saves, so they are kept untyped. Each call of simulate! on it is therefore dynamically dispatched, and the compiler doesn't know what it returns, whatever is saved. Where that matters, use simulate on the models themselves, one timestep at a time or for a whole simulation.\n\nThe same type serves as the agent and as the environment of a simulation, since both are models. See also simulate! and reset!.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> struct LearnsRewards <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\njulia> CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(::LearnsRewards, parameters) = (; expected_reward = 0.5);\n\njulia> function CognitiveModeling.run_timestep(::LearnsRewards, context::ModelContext)\n           V = context.previous_state.expected_reward\n           V = V + context.parameters.learning_rate * (context.input.reward - V)\n           return ModelOutput((; choice = Deterministic(V > 0.5)), (; expected_reward = V))\n       end;\n\njulia> CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(::LearnsRewards) = ModelAttributes(; parameters = :learning_rate, states = :expected_reward, inputs = :reward, outputs = :choice);\n\njulia> agent = InstantiatedModel(LearnsRewards(), (; learning_rate = 0.5));\n\njulia> simulate!(agent, (; reward = 1.0));\n\njulia> agent\n-- Instantiated cognitive model --\nType: LearnsRewards\nTimesteps simulated: 1\nParameters: learning_rate = 0.5\nState: expected_reward = 0.75\n\njulia> print(agent)\nLearnsRewards (instantiated cognitive model, 1 timestep simulated)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.InstantiatedModel"},{"category":"type","location":"api/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.NameMap","page":"Simulation","text":"NameMap(names...)\n\nWhich outputs of one side of a simulation the other side receives, and under which names.\n\nEach argument is :given => :received, for an output which has another name in the receiver's attributes, or a single Symbol, for one which keeps its name. Outputs that aren't in the map aren't passed on, and each name can only be there once.\n\nsimulate and simulate! take one as observation_map, from the observations the environment model gives to those in the cognitive model's attributes, and one as action_map, from the actions the cognitive model gives to those in the environment model's attributes. A cognitive model and an environment which name things differently, or where one gives something that isn't in the other's attributes, can then be used together, and the map says which outputs the receiver gets: it must give exactly the names in the receiver's attributes, from attribute_names. NameMap() passes nothing on, for a receiver without inputs in its attributes. Without a map, the two must have the same names in their attributes.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> NameMap(:payoff => :reward, :cue)\nNameMap(:payoff => :reward, :cue)\n\njulia> NameMap(:payoff => :reward, :cue => :reward)\nERROR: ArgumentError: A NameMap can only give each name once, but it gives (:reward, :reward).\n[...]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.NameMap"},{"category":"method","location":"api/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.reset!-Tuple{InstantiatedModel}","page":"Simulation","text":"reset!(instantiated_model::InstantiatedModel)\n\nPut instantiated_model back to where a new one starts, and return it.\n\nThe state becomes the output of initialize_state with the parameters it has, the previous output has every output as BeforeFirstTimestep again, and n_timesteps is 0. The history is a new one which starts from there, as timestep 0 when the SaveHistory saves the initial timestep values, so a history that was read before the reset, as in history = agent.history, is kept as it was. The model, the parameters and the SaveHistory stay the same.\n\nAn instantiated model that was created with a state or a previous output of its own doesn't return to those. Create a new one for that.\n\nExamples\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\nusing Distributions: Bernoulli\n\nstruct RescorlaWagner <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(model::RescorlaWagner, parameters)\n    return (; expected_reward = 0.5)\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.run_timestep(model::RescorlaWagner, context::ModelContext)\n    V₋₁ = context.previous_state.expected_reward\n    Vₜ = V₋₁ + context.parameters.learning_rate * (context.input.reward - V₋₁)\n    return ModelOutput((; choice = Bernoulli(Vₜ)), (; expected_reward = Vₜ))\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::RescorlaWagner)\n    return ModelAttributes(; parameters = :learning_rate, states = :expected_reward, inputs = :reward, outputs = :choice)\nend\n\nagent = InstantiatedModel(RescorlaWagner(), (; learning_rate = 0.5); save_history = SaveHistory(SaveEverything()))\n\nsimulate!(agent, (; reward = 1.0))\nsimulate!(agent, (; reward = 1.0))\nreset!(agent)\n\nagent.state, agent.previous_output, agent.history.states\n\n# output\n\n((expected_reward = 0.5,), (choice = BeforeFirstTimestep(),), Any[(expected_reward = 0.5,)])\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.reset!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.FixedObservationsEnvironment","page":"Simulation","text":"FixedObservationsEnvironment(observations)\n\nAn environment model which gives the observations in observations in order, an entry of each on every timestep.\n\nArguments\n\nobservations::NamedTuple: a vector for each observation, with an entry for each timestep, such as (; reward = [1.0, 0.0, 1.0]). The vectors must have the same length. Each entry is given in a Deterministic, so nothing is sampled. An entry that is NoValue is given as it is, for a timestep without that observation.\n\nIt takes no actions, so a simulation with a cognitive model that gives actions is given action_map = NameMap(), which passes none of them on. Its attribute_names are the observations as outputs, no inputs, no parameters, and the state n_observations_given, the number of timesteps it has given its observations for. Its stopping_criterion is met when every entry is given, so a simulation stops there by itself, and n_steps is the most timesteps it can take. A timestep after the last entry throws an error.\n\nExamples\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\n\n#A Rescorla-Wagner model which chooses when it expects more than half a reward\nstruct LearnsRewards <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\nCognitiveModeling.initialize_state(model::LearnsRewards, parameters) = (; expected_reward = 0.5)\nfunction CognitiveModeling.run_timestep(model::LearnsRewards, context::ModelContext)\n    V₋₁ = context.previous_state.expected_reward\n    Vₜ = V₋₁ + context.parameters.learning_rate * (context.input.reward - V₋₁)\n    return ModelOutput((; choice = Deterministic(Vₜ > 0.5)), (; expected_reward = Vₜ))\nend\nfunction CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::LearnsRewards)\n    return ModelAttributes(; parameters = :learning_rate, states = :expected_reward, inputs = :reward, outputs = :choice)\nend\n\n#The rewards of three timesteps\nenvironment = FixedObservationsEnvironment((; reward = [1.0, 0.0, 1.0]))\n\n#The simulation stops after the three rewards, before it has run all of n_steps. The environment takes no actions, so the map passes none on\nsimulation = simulate(\n    LearnsRewards(),\n    environment;\n    who_starts = EnvironmentStarts(),\n    n_steps = 10,\n    cognitive_parameters = (; learning_rate = 0.5),\n    environment_parameters = (;),\n    action_map = NameMap(),\n    cognitive_save_history = SaveHistory(; states = :expected_reward, save_initial_timestep_values = false),\n)\n\nsimulation.states\n\n# output\n\n3-element Vector{@NamedTuple{expected_reward::Float64}}:\n (expected_reward = 0.75,)\n (expected_reward = 0.375,)\n (expected_reward = 0.6875,)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.FixedObservationsEnvironment"},{"category":"section","location":"api/saving_histories/#Saving-histories","page":"Saving histories","text":"What a simulation or a fitted session saves of each model, chosen by name, and whether it saves where the models started, as timestep 0.\n\nPages = [\"saving_histories.md\"]","title":"Saving histories"},{"category":"type","location":"api/saving_histories/#CognitiveModeling.ModelHistory","page":"Saving histories","text":"ModelHistory(states, inputs, outputs, output_distributions, timestep_sizes)\n\nWhat has happened to an InstantiatedModel, which simulate! adds to.\n\nEach argument is kept in a field of the same name, such as history.states. The names are the model's own: for a cognitive model the inputs are its observations and the outputs its actions, and for an environment model the inputs are the actions it received and the outputs the observations it gave. The model's SaveHistory selects what is kept of each kind, and when it selects nothing at all, the vectors stay empty. Each vector starts with timestep 0, where the model started, when the SaveHistory saves the initial timestep values, which it does by default, so that element t + 1 is timestep t.\n\nThe vectors hold values of any type, since what is saved can change type from one timestep to the next, as an output does from BeforeFirstTimestep() at timestep 0 to a value.\n\nArguments\n\nstates::Vector{Any}: the states of the model, starting at timestep 0 with its initial state. Later states are those after each timestep. All of them are as returned by prepare_state_for_saving.\ninputs::Vector{Any}: what the model received on each timestep, starting at timestep 0 with every input in the model's attributes as BeforeFirstTimestep, since it has received nothing before the simulation.\noutputs::Vector{Any}: what the model gave on each timestep, starting at timestep 0 with its previous output, which by default has every output as BeforeFirstTimestep().\noutput_distributions::Vector{Any}: what the model gave each output to be sampled from, starting at timestep 0 with BeforeFirstTimestep() for every output, since nothing was given yet.\ntimestep_sizes::Vector{Any}: how much time each timestep covered, starting with BeforeFirstTimestep() for timestep 0, so that it has an entry for each entry of the other vectors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.ModelHistory"},{"category":"type","location":"api/saving_histories/#CognitiveModeling.SaveEverything","page":"Saving histories","text":"SaveEverything()\n\nMarker for saving every attribute of a kind, used in SaveHistory.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.SaveEverything"},{"category":"type","location":"api/saving_histories/#CognitiveModeling.SaveHistory","page":"Saving histories","text":"SaveHistory(; states=(), inputs=(), outputs=(), output_distributions=(),\n            save_initial_timestep_values=true)\nSaveHistory(SaveEverything(); save_initial_timestep_values=true)\n\nWhich states, inputs, outputs and output distributions of a model a simulation or a fitted session saves, by name, and whether it saves them from before the first timestep too.\n\nstates, inputs, outputs and output_distributions are each the names of that kind to save, in a Tuple of Symbols, a single Symbol or a Vector of them, or SaveEverything for all of them. The names are those in the model's attributes, from attribute_names: for a cognitive model the inputs are its observations and the outputs its actions, and for an environment model the inputs are the actions it receives and the outputs the observations it gives. The output distributions are what the model gave its outputs to be sampled from, named like the outputs. A kind that isn't given saves nothing, so SaveHistory() saves nothing, which is the default everywhere, and SaveHistory(SaveEverything()) saves everything. The selections are read as properties, such as save_history.states.\n\nsave_initial_timestep_values is whether what is saved starts with timestep 0, before the first timestep: the initial states, and the previous input and output, which are BeforeFirstTimestep unless others are given. It is true by default, which matters when the initial state depends on parameters that are fitted. Since Julia counts from 1, element t + 1 of what is saved is then timestep t, and with false, element t is.\n\nSaving some names of a kind gives a NamedTuple with those names on each timestep, and saving none gives an empty one, (;). Before the first timestep there is no output distribution, so timestep 0 has BeforeFirstTimestep() for each name that is saved.\n\nEach model of a simulation has one: simulate and SessionInfo take cognitive_save_history and environment_save_history, and an InstantiatedModel takes save_history. The trajectories that a simulation or a session returns are named by their role: the cognitive model's inputs as observations and outputs as actions, and the environment's inputs as actions and outputs as observations under environment, as simulate describes.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> save_history = SaveHistory(; outputs = :choice, states = (:expected_reward, :surprise))\nSaveHistory\nStates: expected_reward, surprise\nInputs: none\nOutputs: choice\nOutput distributions: none\nInitial timestep values: saved\n\njulia> save_history.states\n(:expected_reward, :surprise)\n\njulia> SaveHistory(SaveEverything()).inputs\nSaveEverything()\n\njulia> SaveHistory(SaveEverything(); save_initial_timestep_values = false).save_initial_timestep_values\nfalse\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.SaveHistory"}]
}
